window.ChemistryATAR = [{"id": "che11-atoms", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "title": "Atomic structure, evidence and periodicity", "scope": "Subatomic particles; isotopes; electron configurations; atomic models; spectra and AAS; mass spectrometry; periodic trends", "screens": [{"title": "Atoms, ions and the three scales", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemistry connects a visible observation with a particle explanation and a symbolic representation. A flame colour is a macroscopic observation. An electron changing energy level is the particle explanation. An atomic symbol and electron configuration represent the atom symbolically. An atom contains a compact positive nucleus and electrons distributed around it. Protons have charge +1, neutrons 0 and electrons −1 in relative units. Protons and neutrons each have approximately one atomic mass unit. An electron has much smaller mass. Electrical neutrality means equal numbers of protons and electrons, not an absence of charged particles. Chemical reactions redistribute electrons and rearrange atoms without changing the nuclei.", "diagram": "atom"}, {"title": "Atomic number, mass number and ionic charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "Atomic number Z counts protons and fixes the element. Mass number A counts protons plus neutrons in one isotope. Therefore neutrons = A − Z. For an ion with signed charge q, electrons = Z − q: a 2+ ion has two fewer electrons than its neutral atom. For chlorine-37, Z = 17, so there are 17 protons and 20 neutrons. Its chloride ion has 18 electrons. Never use a decimal periodic-table atomic weight as a mass number. The decimal value describes an isotopic average, whereas a particular nucleus contains whole numbers of protons and neutrons. Write isotope symbols with mass number at upper left and atomic number at lower left.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Isotopes and relative atomic mass", "kind": "learn", "text": "Isotopes share atomic number but differ in neutron number. Their neutral atoms have the same electron configuration, so ordinary chemical behaviour is similar. Their masses and some physical properties differ. Relative atomic mass Aᵣ compares the mean mass per atom with one twelfth of a carbon-12 atom. It is a dimensionless weighted average. Multiply each isotopic mass by its fractional abundance, then add. If abundances are percentages, divide the sum by 100. Aᵣ need not equal a whole number and does not mean an individual atom contains a fractional neutron. Check that abundances total 100% and that your average lies between the isotope masses.", "diagram": "isotopes"}, {"title": "Mass spectrometry: how the evidence is produced", "kind": "learn", "text": "A sample is converted into gas-phase ions, separated according to mass-to-charge ratio m/z and detected. In a simple elemental spectrum of singly charged ions, peak positions approximately identify isotope masses and relative peak intensities indicate relative abundance. For multiply charged ions, m/z is smaller than the ionic mass number: a 40 u ion carrying charge 2+ appears near m/z = 20. A peak is evidence of detected ions, not a picture of the atom. Do not interpret every peak in a molecular spectrum as an isotope: fragmentation may also produce smaller ions. This module uses simple elemental isotope spectra when calculating Aᵣ.", "diagram": "mass-spectrum"}, {"title": "Dalton and Thomson: models meet new evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dalton used patterns in chemical combination to propose atoms and fixed whole-number ratios in compounds. His claim that all atoms of an element have identical mass needed revision after isotopes were established. Thomson showed that cathode rays were deflected by electric and magnetic fields and consisted of negative particles found in different materials. This supported electrons as subatomic components and contradicted an indivisible atom. His model placed electrons within a diffuse positive region. In an exam, connect each observation to a specific inference, then state what the evidence does not establish. Discovering electrons alone does not demonstrate a tiny nucleus.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rutherford and Chadwick: the nuclear model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Most alpha particles passed through a thin gold foil with little deflection, indicating that most atomic volume offered no concentrated obstacle. A small fraction deflected strongly or returned, supporting a very small, dense, positive nucleus containing most of the mass. Rutherford’s results did not directly locate electrons in particular energy levels. Chadwick’s evidence for neutral particles helped account for nuclear mass without adding positive charge. The modern nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Describe both the common and rare scattering observations. Saying only “particles bounced back” loses the connection between most atoms being largely empty space and mass being concentrated centrally.", "diagram": "scattering"}, {"title": "Electron configurations and shell capacity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electron configurations describe the distribution of electrons among energy levels and, when required, subshells. For the first 20 elements, useful shell configurations include Na 2,8,1; Cl 2,8,7; Ca 2,8,8,2. The formula 2n² gives a shell’s maximum capacity, not the order in which every shell fills. The third shell can hold 18 electrons, but potassium and calcium begin occupying 4s before 3d fills. Shell diagrams are bookkeeping models. They do not show electrons following circular planetary tracks. The number of occupied main shells connects to the period, while main-group valence electrons connect to typical bonding capacity.", "diagram": "shells"}, {"title": "Subshell notation and ion formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The labels s, p and d identify subshells with capacities 2, 6 and 10 respectively. Sodium is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹. Its Na⁺ ion is 1s²2s²2p⁶ because it loses its outer 3s electron. Chlorine is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁵ and Cl⁻ becomes 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶. Losing electrons does not change atomic number. Main-group ions often have noble-gas configurations, but “atoms want an octet” is an informal shortcut, not a physical cause. Stability depends on the energy of the resulting system. Use electrostatic attraction and electron arrangement to explain bonding rather than giving particles human intentions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bohr, quantised energy and line spectra", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bohr’s model introduced allowed electron energies. An electron absorbs a photon only when the photon energy matches an allowed upward energy difference. An excited electron can later fall to a lower level and emit a photon carrying that difference. Different energy gaps produce different wavelengths, so atomic spectra contain distinct lines rather than a continuous rainbow. The model explains hydrogen’s line spectrum but fixed circular orbits do not provide an adequate general picture of many-electron atoms. Modern orbital models describe probabilities. In flame tests, heating excites atomic species and emitted visible light gives characteristic colours. Electrons release energy on downward transitions, not on the upward excitation.", "diagram": "energy-levels"}, {"title": "Flame tests, contamination and identification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Clean the test apparatus, test a known reference and compare the observed colour under consistent conditions. Sodium gives a strong yellow emission that can mask another element. Lithium is crimson, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, barium green and copper compounds often blue-green. A colour is supporting evidence, not always a unique identification of a mixture. A convincing explanation names energy absorption, movement to higher allowed levels, return to lower levels and emission of particular wavelengths. Contamination, mixtures and weak emissions limit interpretation. Do not treat flame intensity as a reliable concentration measurement without calibration and controlled conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Atomic absorption spectroscopy", "kind": "learn", "text": "AAS atomises a prepared sample and passes light of a selected element-specific wavelength through the atomic vapour. Ground-state atoms absorb matching photons as electrons move to higher levels. A detector measures the transmitted intensity and the instrument determines absorbance. Standards of known concentration establish a calibration relationship over a validated range. Interpolate an unknown’s absorbance, then apply its dilution factor. Absorbance is logarithmically related to incident and transmitted intensity, not simply their difference. Blanks, suitable standards and repeated measurements help control contamination and uncertainty. AAS commonly measures concentration of a selected element, not an entire molecular structure.", "diagram": "aas"}, {"title": "Periodic table: the organising evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Elements appear in increasing atomic number. Across a period the same main energy level fills while nuclear charge increases. Main-group elements within a group share a characteristic number of valence electrons and therefore similar chemical patterns. Group 1 atoms commonly form +1 ions, Group 2 +2, Group 13 +3, Group 16 −2 and Group 17 −1. Group 14 often shares electrons rather than forming simple ±4 monatomic ions. Valency describes combining capacity and is not automatically equal to an ionic charge. Noble gases have filled outer shells and low ordinary reactivity. Periodicity follows repeating electron arrangements, not repeating mass numbers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Atomic radius across and down", "kind": "learn", "text": "Across Period 3, nuclear charge increases while electrons enter the same main shell. Shielding by inner shells changes relatively little, so effective attraction on outer electrons increases and atomic radius generally decreases. Down a main group, occupied shells increase. Outer electrons lie farther from the nucleus and experience greater shielding, so radius increases despite more protons. A full explanation balances nuclear charge, distance and shielding. “More protons means smaller atoms” is incomplete when comparing atoms in different periods. When comparing isoelectronic ions, electron count is the same and the ion with more protons generally has the smaller radius.", "diagram": "periodic-trends"}, {"title": "First ionisation energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "First ionisation energy is the energy required to remove one electron from each gaseous atom in one mole of gaseous atoms: Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻. It is positive because attraction must be overcome. It generally increases across a period and decreases down a group. Period 3 contains departures: Al’s outer 3p electron is easier to remove than Mg’s 3s electron, and pairing repulsion makes an electron in S easier to remove than in P. Explain these comparisons through subshell energy or electron pairing. Do not claim the trend is perfectly monotonic. A very large jump in successive ionisation energies can reveal the number of valence electrons.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Electronegativity and bonding predictions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electronegativity describes an atom’s tendency to attract shared electrons in a bond. It generally increases across a period and decreases down a group because nuclear attraction, shielding and distance change. It differs from ionisation energy, which concerns removing an electron from a gaseous atom. An electronegativity difference creates unequal sharing and partial charges in a polar covalent bond. A partial charge is not a full ionic charge. Whether a molecule is polar also depends on geometry. Noble-gas electronegativity values are commonly omitted in basic tables. Use a provided data table consistently rather than mixing numerical scales.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Successive ionisation evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Successive ionisation energies increase as more electrons are removed because the remaining electrons experience a more positive ion. A particularly large jump can indicate removal begins from an inner shell after valence electrons are exhausted. For a main-group atom with a jump between the second and third removals, two valence electrons are a plausible inference. The conclusion depends on identifying the sequence correctly, not choosing the biggest raw number. State the electron arrangement inferred and connect the jump to closer, more strongly attracted inner electrons. Successive removal involves increasingly charged gaseous ions, unlike first ionisation from neutral atoms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Calibration uncertainty and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A calibration line should include a blank and relevant standards, with the unknown inside the validated range. Diluting an over-range unknown can prevent unjustified extrapolation. Sample preparation may introduce contamination or losses even if the instrument itself repeats precisely. Flame colour and AAS response are observations; naming the element is an inference supported by reference comparison. In a written answer, distinguish these roles and give specific evidence. If an absorbance is repeated without independent sample preparation, the result assesses instrumental repeatability more than the full method’s reliability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Isotope and ion accounting", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Isotope and ion accounting", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Weighted isotopic average", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Weighted isotopic average", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: AAS and dilution", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: AAS and dilution", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: A periodic comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: A periodic comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Evidence and model limits", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Emission reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: AAS investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Isotope and ion accounting", "prompt": "A sulfur-34 ion has charge 2−. Determine its numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons. Explain why sulfur-32 has similar chemical properties.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Sulfur has atomic number 16, so 16 protons. [1]", "Neutrons = 34 − 16 = 18. [1]", "The 2− ion has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons. [1]", "Both neutral isotopes have the same electron configuration and valence arrangement. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Weighted isotopic average", "prompt": "An element has isotope masses 10.01 u and 11.01 u with abundances 20.0% and 80.0%. Calculate its relative atomic mass.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Convert to fractions 0.200 and 0.800. [1]", "Aᵣ = 10.01(0.200) + 11.01(0.800). [1]", "Aᵣ = 10.81, dimensionless. It lies between the isotopic masses. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "AAS and dilution", "prompt": "Standards give absorbance 0.100 at 2.00 mg L⁻¹ and 0.300 at 6.00 mg L⁻¹. A 10.0-fold diluted sample gives 0.200. Assume linear calibration. Find the original concentration and name one reliability improvement.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The absorbance is halfway between the standards. [1]", "The diluted concentration is 4.00 mg L⁻¹. [1]", "Original concentration = 4.00 × 10.0 = 40.0 mg L⁻¹. [1]", "Repeat sample preparation and measurement and compare results, or use several standards spanning the unknown. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A periodic comparison", "prompt": "Explain why chlorine has a smaller atomic radius and a greater first ionisation energy than sodium.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Both atoms have three occupied main shells. [1]", "Chlorine has greater nuclear charge with similar inner-shell shielding. [1]", "The greater effective attraction draws outer electrons closer, decreasing radius. [1]", "More energy is required to remove an outer electron against this attraction. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Evidence and model limits", "prompt": "State two observations from Rutherford’s scattering experiment and explain the nuclear model they support.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Most particles passed through with little deflection. [1]", "This supports most atomic volume being largely empty space. [1]", "A small fraction underwent large deflections. [1]", "This supports a small dense positively charged nucleus. Fixed electron shells are not established by this experiment. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Emission reasoning", "prompt": "Explain the origin of a line in an atomic emission spectrum and why an element produces several separate lines.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["An excited electron falls from a higher allowed energy level to a lower one. [1]", "A photon carries the energy difference. [1]", "Different permitted transitions have different energy gaps. [1]", "Each gap corresponds to a particular wavelength, producing distinct lines. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "AAS investigation", "prompt": "A diluted water sample lies above the highest AAS calibration standard. A student extrapolates and reports an exact concentration. Evaluate the method, explain a better procedure and identify why an atomisation measurement does not reveal the full original molecular structure.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Extrapolation beyond the validated range is not justified. [1]", "Dilute the sample to bring its response within the standard range. [1]", "Use a blank and several relevant standards. [1]", "Repeat preparation and measurement to assess variation. [1]", "Apply the additional dilution factor to the interpolated result. [1]", "AAS measures selected atomic absorption after atomisation, not intact molecular connectivity. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Which changes when a neutral sodium atom forms Na⁺?", "o": ["Proton number", "Electron number", "Atomic number", "Neutron number"], "a": 1, "why": "Na loses one outer electron; its nucleus is unchanged."}, {"q": "Aᵣ is best described as", "o": ["The mass number of every atom", "A weighted isotopic average relative to carbon-12", "The neutron count", "A mass measured in grams"], "a": 1, "why": "Aᵣ is a dimensionless relative average."}, {"q": "An emission photon is produced when an electron", "o": ["Falls to a lower energy level", "Moves to a higher level", "Becomes a neutron", "Increases atomic number"], "a": 0, "why": "The photon carries the decrease in atomic electronic energy."}, {"q": "Down Group 1 atomic radius generally", "o": ["Decreases", "Increases", "Stays constant", "Becomes zero"], "a": 1, "why": "Additional occupied shells and shielding increase radius."}, {"q": "Which can reveal isotopic abundance?", "o": ["A simple elemental mass spectrum", "A pH meter", "A conductivity reading alone", "A thermometer"], "a": 0, "why": "Peak positions and relative intensities support isotope analysis."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-materials", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "title": "Materials, separation and bonding models", "scope": "Mixtures and separation; nanomaterials; ionic, metallic, covalent molecular and network structures; structure–property explanations", "screens": [{"title": "Pure substances and mixtures", "kind": "learn", "text": "A pure element contains one type of atom. A pure compound contains elements chemically combined in a fixed proportion. A mixture contains substances physically combined in variable proportions. Oxygen gas contains O₂ molecules and remains an element. A sodium chloride crystal is a compound built from ions, with a 1:1 ratio rather than discrete NaCl molecules. Air is a mixture of gases. A sample can be homogeneous and still be a mixture, such as salt solution. Fixed composition and characteristic properties support purity; appearance alone does not establish it. Physical separation exploits property differences without requiring chemical conversion.", "diagram": "mixtures"}, {"title": "Separation through particle size and solubility", "kind": "learn", "text": "Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid because particles cannot pass through suitable pores. Dissolved ions are far smaller and pass with the solvent. To separate sand and salt, add water to dissolve salt, filter the sand, wash its residue and evaporate or crystallise salt from the filtrate. Each step depends on a stated property difference. Washing removes solution trapped in a residue, improving purity but potentially affecting recovery if the desired solid is slightly soluble. Explain which material is residue and which is filtrate. A method earns scientific credit through the property that makes it work, not merely naming equipment.", "diagram": "filtration"}, {"title": "Distillation, density and chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "Distillation vaporises a more volatile component and condenses its vapour. Fractional distillation improves separation of miscible liquids with closer boiling points through repeated vaporisation and condensation. A separating funnel separates immiscible liquids by density and distinct phases, not dissolved substances within one phase. Chromatography separates components by different interactions with stationary and mobile phases. Choose a method using the actual mixture: salt water, ethanol–water and oil–water require different approaches. Some mixtures need a sequence. A narrow melting range or a single chromatographic spot can support purity, but one test alone may fail to detect impurities with similar properties.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Nanomaterials and surface area", "kind": "learn", "text": "Nanomaterials contain particles approximately 1–100 nm in size. As size decreases, the surface-area-to-volume ratio increases, so a larger proportion of atoms is near the surface. A cube of side L has area 6L², volume L³ and ratio 6/L. Surface interactions can therefore contribute more strongly to reactivity, catalysis and adsorption. Nanoscale optical or electrical properties may also differ through size-dependent electronic behaviour. A nanoparticle is not a new element simply because it is small. Particle diagrams exaggerate scale. Compare equal total masses when judging the effect of dividing a material into smaller particles.", "diagram": "nano"}, {"title": "Nanotechnology: benefits, evidence and risk", "kind": "learn", "text": "A nanoscale catalyst may improve reaction rate by exposing more active sites, and nanomaterials can alter coatings, sensors or drug delivery. Benefits must be judged against evidence about exposure, persistence and effects on organisms. A bulk material’s safety record does not automatically establish a nanoparticle’s safety. Compare dose, particle size, surface chemistry and routes of exposure. A reasoned evaluation states a benefit, a plausible concern, the evidence needed and a practical control. Distinguish an observed effect from a hypothesis and a commercial claim. Science informs a decision, while economic and ethical priorities also influence the final choice.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Electrostatic attraction: the basis of bonding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemical bonds involve electrostatic attraction between positive and negative charge. Ionic bonding is attraction between oppositely charged ions throughout a lattice. Metallic bonding is attraction between positive atomic cores and delocalised electrons. Covalent bonding is attraction between shared electron density and the nuclei of bonded atoms. Electron transfer explains the formation of ions, but transfer itself is not the attractive force holding a crystal together. Bonding lowers the system’s energy relative to appropriately separated particles. Simple diagrams distinguish local electron sharing from extended structures. A line in a structural formula represents a shared electron pair, not a tiny solid rod.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reference: required cation names and formulae", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Use charge balance to construct an ionic formula. A Roman numeral distinguishes the metal oxidation state. These names/formulae cover the cation set specified in the Year 11 syllabus appendix.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ammonium</td><td>NH₄⁺</td></tr><tr><td>caesium</td><td>Cs⁺</td></tr><tr><td>copper(I)</td><td>Cu⁺</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogen</td><td>H⁺</td></tr><tr><td>lithium</td><td>Li⁺</td></tr><tr><td>potassium</td><td>K⁺</td></tr><tr><td>rubidium</td><td>Rb⁺</td></tr><tr><td>silver</td><td>Ag⁺</td></tr><tr><td>sodium</td><td>Na⁺</td></tr><tr><td>barium</td><td>Ba²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>calcium</td><td>Ca²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>cobalt(II)</td><td>Co²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>copper(II)</td><td>Cu²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>iron(II)</td><td>Fe²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>lead(II)</td><td>Pb²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>magnesium</td><td>Mg²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>manganese(II)</td><td>Mn²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>nickel(II)</td><td>Ni²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>strontium</td><td>Sr²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>zinc</td><td>Zn²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>aluminium</td><td>Al³⁺</td></tr><tr><td>chromium(III)</td><td>Cr³⁺</td></tr><tr><td>iron(III)</td><td>Fe³⁺</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Reference: required anions with charge 1−", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Retain each polyatomic ion’s internal formula. A negative charge indicates the whole ion’s electron excess, not an extra atom. Use parentheses when more than one polyatomic group is required.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bromide</td><td>Br⁻</td></tr><tr><td>chloride</td><td>Cl⁻</td></tr><tr><td>cyanide</td><td>CN⁻</td></tr><tr><td>dihydrogenphosphate</td><td>H₂PO₄⁻</td></tr><tr><td>ethanoate (acetate)</td><td>CH₃COO⁻</td></tr><tr><td>fluoride</td><td>F⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogencarbonate</td><td>HCO₃⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogensulfate</td><td>HSO₄⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydroxide</td><td>OH⁻</td></tr><tr><td>iodide</td><td>I⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitrate</td><td>NO₃⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitrite</td><td>NO₂⁻</td></tr><tr><td>permanganate</td><td>MnO₄⁻</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Reference: required anions with charges 2− and 3−", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Distinguish sulfate from sulfite, and nitrate from nitrite: a one-oxygen difference changes the ion’s identity. Hydrogenphosphate HPO₄²⁻ differs from dihydrogenphosphate H₂PO₄⁻.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>carbonate</td><td>CO₃²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>chromate</td><td>CrO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>dichromate</td><td>Cr₂O₇²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogenphosphate</td><td>HPO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>oxalate</td><td>C₂O₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>oxide</td><td>O²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfate</td><td>SO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfide</td><td>S²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfite</td><td>SO₃²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitride</td><td>N³⁻</td></tr><tr><td>phosphate</td><td>PO₄³⁻</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Ionic formulae and polyatomic ions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A neutral ionic formula has total positive charge equal to total negative charge. Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ give MgCl₂. Al³⁺ and O²⁻ give Al₂O₃, using the smallest whole-number ratio. Calcium and nitrate give Ca(NO₃)₂; parentheses show two entire nitrate groups. A sulfate ion remains SO₄²⁻ during ordinary formula construction. Never alter internal subscripts to balance charge. Ionic formulae express ratios in a large lattice, not molecule counts. Common ions include NH₄⁺, OH⁻, NO₃⁻, CO₃²⁻, HCO₃⁻, SO₄²⁻ and PO₄³⁻. A Roman numeral specifies a variable metal oxidation state, as in iron(III) chloride, FeCl₃.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ionic lattices and high melting point", "kind": "learn", "text": "Each ion in an ionic crystal interacts with oppositely charged neighbours in a three-dimensional regular arrangement. Melting requires enough energy to disrupt this ordered network of strong electrostatic attractions, so ionic substances commonly have high melting points. A flat grid represents only a slice of the lattice. Do not describe the solid as many isolated cation–anion pairs. Comparisons between ionic melting points also depend on ionic charges, sizes and lattice arrangement. A greater charge and shorter separation can strengthen attraction, but a full ranking may require data. Dissolving involves both separation of lattice ions and interactions with the solvent.", "diagram": "ionic"}, {"title": "Ionic conductivity and brittleness", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a solid ionic crystal, ions occupy fixed positions and cannot migrate through the material, so it does not conduct electricity through mobile ions. In a molten ionic substance or a sufficiently soluble aqueous solution, ions can move and carry charge. Do not say the ions become electrons or the solid contains no charge. When a force shifts lattice layers, ions of like charge may become adjacent and repel, causing fracture. This explains brittleness. Conductivity requires mobile charged particles and an appropriate pathway. A conductivity answer should identify the carrier and whether it can move in the specified physical state.", "diagram": "ionic-shift"}, {"title": "Metallic bonding and conductivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "A metal contains positive atomic cores in an extended arrangement and delocalised valence electrons that can move through the structure. An applied potential difference causes net electron movement and electrical conduction. Energy also transfers through mobile electrons and lattice vibrations, contributing to thermal conductivity. The model represents collective attraction, not one fixed electron pair connecting each neighbour. Many metals have high melting points because metallic attraction is strong, but there are important exceptions such as mercury. Avoid an absolute statement that all metals have high melting points. A diagram’s negative dots represent electrons, not anions dissolved between metal atoms.", "diagram": "metallic"}, {"title": "Malleability, ductility and alloys", "kind": "learn", "text": "Metallic attraction is not tied to one directional pair of atoms. Layers of positive cores can move while attraction to delocalised electrons maintains cohesion. Metals can therefore be hammered into sheets or drawn into wires without behaving like brittle ionic crystals. Alloying introduces atoms of different sizes that distort the regular arrangement and obstruct easy movement of layers. This often increases hardness or strength, although exact properties depend on composition and structure. Explain an alloy’s property through the altered structure rather than saying it is simply “stronger bonding.” Alloys are typically mixtures, whereas an ionic compound has a fixed chemical composition.", "diagram": "alloy"}, {"title": "Covalent molecular substances", "kind": "learn", "text": "Within a molecule, shared electron pairs are attracted to both bonded nuclei. Between separate molecules, intermolecular attractions act. Melting or boiling a molecular substance generally overcomes enough intermolecular attraction for particles to rearrange or separate; it does not normally break all covalent bonds inside each molecule. Molecular substances often have lower melting points than networks and lack mobile charged particles, so they commonly do not conduct in solid or liquid states. Some molecular substances ionise when reacting with water, producing a conducting solution. Describe the actual state and chemistry rather than assuming every molecular solution is a non-electrolyte.", "diagram": "molecular"}, {"title": "Covalent networks and carbon allotropes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diamond has a three-dimensional network in which each carbon bonds to four neighbours. Strong covalent bonding throughout the network explains hardness and very high thermal stability. It lacks mobile charged particles for ordinary electrical conduction. Graphite has sheets with each carbon bonded to three neighbours and delocalised electrons within the sheets. It conducts along the layers, and relatively weak attraction between layers permits sliding. Fullerenes contain discrete cage molecules, so forces between cages differ from diamond’s continuous network. Allotropes are different structures of the same element, not isotopes. A model must distinguish intralayer bonds from interlayer attraction.", "diagram": "carbon"}, {"title": "Structure–property explanations that earn marks", "kind": "learn", "text": "A complete explanation follows structure, force or carrier, then observed property. For sodium chloride: an extended lattice of oppositely charged ions; strong electrostatic attraction; substantial energy needed to melt. For conductivity: mobile ions in the melt carry charge, while solid ions are fixed. For graphite: delocalised electrons in sheets permit conduction; weak interlayer forces permit sliding. “Strong bonds” alone omits the structure and often names the wrong force. Use unfamiliar data to identify a plausible structure, then check all properties together. One property such as high melting point cannot uniquely distinguish an ionic crystal from a covalent network.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A structure–property comparison table", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ionic: extended ions; solid ions fixed; molten ions mobile; generally high melting point and brittleness. Metallic: positive cores plus delocalised electrons; solid conduction and layer movement supported by collective attraction. Molecular: discrete molecules; intermolecular forces determine phase separation; ordinary neat substances lack mobile ions or electrons. Network: extended covalent bonds; generally high thermal stability, with conductivity dependent on electronic structure. Graphite is an important conducting network exception. Apply all observations before choosing a model. A high melting point and insolubility alone do not uniquely identify one class.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Valency, charge and compound names", "kind": "learn", "text": "Valency counts combining capacity in the relevant model, while oxidation state and ionic charge are different ideas. Magnesium commonly gives Mg²⁺, requiring two chloride ions in MgCl₂. Nitrogen in ammonia makes three N–H bonds but is not a free N³⁺ ion. Roman numerals distinguish a metal oxidation state, as in copper(II) oxide CuO versus copper(I) oxide Cu₂O. In an ionic name, state the cation then anion and retain a polyatomic ion’s identity. In a covalent structural argument, count shared pairs rather than treating every atom as a fully separated ion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Ionic formula construction", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Ionic formula construction", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Structure from observations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Structure from observations", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: A separation sequence", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: A separation sequence", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Nanoparticle calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Nanoparticle calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Diamond and graphite", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Melting versus bond breaking", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Unfamiliar structure evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Ionic formula construction", "prompt": "Write formulae for aluminium sulfate and calcium phosphate, showing how charges balance.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻ require 2:3 to balance +6 and −6. [1]", "Al₂(SO₄)₃. [1]", "Ca²⁺ and PO₄³⁻ require 3:2 to balance +6 and −6. [1]", "Ca₃(PO₄)₂. Internal ion subscripts remain unchanged. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Structure from observations", "prompt": "A solid has a high melting point, is brittle, does not conduct when solid and conducts when molten. Identify its likely bonding and explain the conductivity change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ionic bonding is consistent with the full evidence set. [1]", "The solid contains charged ions fixed in a lattice. [1]", "Melting disrupts the ordered lattice, allowing ions to move. [1]", "Mobile ions carry electrical charge through the liquid. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A separation sequence", "prompt": "Describe how to obtain dry sand and dry salt from a mixture of the two.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Add water to dissolve salt while sand remains insoluble. [1]", "Filter, retaining sand as residue. [1]", "Wash the sand to remove trapped salt solution. [1]", "Dry the washed sand. [1]", "Evaporate water or crystallise salt from the filtrate, then dry the recovered crystals. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Nanoparticle calculation", "prompt": "Compare surface-area-to-volume ratios of cubes with sides 100 nm and 20 nm. Explain one catalytic implication.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["SA/V = 6/L. [1]", "Ratios are 0.060 nm⁻¹ and 0.300 nm⁻¹. [1]", "The smaller cube has five times the ratio. [1]", "For equal material amount, more exposed surface can provide more catalytic active sites. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Diamond and graphite", "prompt": "Explain why graphite conducts electricity and is soft while diamond does not ordinarily conduct and is hard.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Graphite contains delocalised electrons within sheets. [1]", "These mobile electrons carry charge. [1]", "Weak attractions between sheets permit sliding. [1]", "Diamond forms a continuous three-dimensional covalent network. [1]", "Strong bonds throughout resist deformation. [1]", "Its electrons are localised in bonds, leaving no mobile charge carriers. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Melting versus bond breaking", "prompt": "A student says boiling methane breaks C–H bonds. Evaluate the statement and identify the relevant attraction.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The statement confuses bonds within molecules with forces between molecules. [1]", "Methane molecules remain intact during ordinary boiling. [1]", "Energy overcomes intermolecular dispersion attractions sufficiently for vaporisation. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Unfamiliar structure evidence", "prompt": "Solid X has a high melting point and conducts as a solid. Solid Y also melts at high temperature but conducts only when molten. Propose a bonding model for each, explain the charge carriers and identify why high melting point alone is insufficient.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["X is consistent with metallic structure, though graphite is an important alternative requiring more evidence. [1]", "Mobile delocalised electrons explain solid conduction in these possible models. [1]", "Y is consistent with an ionic lattice. [1]", "Its solid ions cannot migrate. [1]", "Molten ions can move and conduct. [1]", "High melting point can arise from ionic, metallic or network bonding, so the full evidence set matters. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Molten NaCl conducts through", "o": ["Mobile ions", "Free protons", "Mobile neutral molecules", "Photons"], "a": 0, "why": "The charged ions can migrate in the melt."}, {"q": "Which has a continuous covalent network?", "o": ["Methane", "Diamond", "Sodium chloride", "Water"], "a": 1, "why": "Diamond has a three-dimensional network of covalently bonded carbons."}, {"q": "Filtration of salt water normally", "o": ["Removes dissolved salt", "Allows dissolved ions through", "Changes Na⁺ to sodium", "Separates water molecules by charge"], "a": 1, "why": "Dissolved ions pass through ordinary filter pores."}, {"q": "An alloy often becomes harder because", "o": ["Atoms lose their nuclei", "Different sizes obstruct layer movement", "All electrons disappear", "It becomes an ionic compound"], "a": 1, "why": "Distortion impedes movement within the structure."}, {"q": "A nanomaterial typically contains particles of size", "o": ["1–100 nm", "1–100 m", "Only exactly 1 nm", "At least 1 mm"], "a": 0, "why": "SCSA specifies approximately the 1–100 nm range."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "31 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "title": "Hydrocarbons: structures, names and reactions", "scope": "Alkanes, alkenes and benzene; C1–C8 naming; structural representations; combustion, addition and substitution", "screens": [{"title": "Carbon and molecular representations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carbon commonly forms four covalent bonds. Single, double and triple bonds count as one, two and three shared pairs respectively when checking valency. Carbon atoms can form chains, branches and rings. A hydrocarbon contains only carbon and hydrogen, whereas the wider category of organic compounds can also contain oxygen, nitrogen and other elements. A molecular formula states atom counts but does not show connectivity. A condensed structural formula such as CH₃CH₂CH₃ identifies the carbon sequence. A displayed formula shows bonds and atoms explicitly. Choose the representation requested in the question; a molecular formula cannot identify one structural isomer uniquely.", "diagram": "hydrocarbon"}, {"title": "Alkanes and the homologous series", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acyclic saturated alkanes contain only C–C single bonds and have general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Consecutive members differ by CH₂ and show related chemical properties. Methane is CH₄, ethane C₂H₆ and propane C₃H₈. The formula assumes an open chain with no rings. A cycloalkane has a different hydrogen count despite containing only single bonds. Carbon’s tetrahedral local bonding means a displayed straight chain is a connectivity diagram, not a claim that all atoms lie on one straight line. Check each carbon has four bonds when drawing a structure. A terminal carbon in an unbranched alkane is usually CH₃.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming straight chains to eight carbons", "kind": "learn", "text": "The stems are meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-, hex-, hept- and oct- for one through eight carbons. Add -ane for an alkane. The longest continuous carbon chain determines the parent name. A bent drawing may still be an unbranched chain. Follow connected atoms instead of counting how many appear in a horizontal row. CH₃(CH₂)₄CH₃ has six carbons and is hexane. Parent-chain selection precedes numbering and branch naming. Formula C₆H₁₄ alone cannot distinguish hexane from a branched isomer. A name must communicate connectivity clearly enough that another person can draw the same structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Simple branching and locants", "kind": "learn", "text": "A one-carbon branch is methyl and a two-carbon branch is ethyl. Select the longest continuous chain, then number to give the substituents the lowest set of locants at the first point of difference. CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is 2-methylbutane, not 3-methylbutane and not a pentane chain. Repeated branches use di-, tri- and appropriate locants, as in 2,3-dimethylpentane. Separate numbers by commas and numbers from words by hyphens. Alphabetise different branch names while ignoring multiplying prefixes. There may be several candidate longest chains; choose a valid chain according to naming conventions, then check total carbon count and valency.", "diagram": "branches"}, {"title": "Alkenes and the double bond", "kind": "learn", "text": "An alkene contains a C=C bond. An acyclic alkene with one double bond has formula CₙH₂ₙ. The double bond is a region of higher electron density and supports characteristic addition reactions. Choose the longest parent chain containing the double bond and number from the end giving that bond its lowest locant. CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃ is pent-2-ene. CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is 2-methylbut-1-ene. The double bond receives numbering priority over an alkyl branch in these simple structures. A general formula helps check atom count but cannot by itself prove that a compound is an alkene because rings can give the same formula.", "diagram": "alkene"}, {"title": "Structural isomers and checking completeness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Structural isomers share a molecular formula but have different connectivity. Butane and 2-methylpropane both have C₄H₁₀. Position isomers differ in the location of a group or bond, as in pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene. Rotating a drawing or bending an identical chain does not produce a new structural isomer. To enumerate candidates, work systematically through distinct carbon skeletons and permitted bond positions. Redraw or name each candidate to identify duplicates. In Year 12, restricted rotation around a double bond adds cis–trans isomerism when each double-bonded carbon has two different attached groups. Structural and geometric differences are separate ideas.", "diagram": "isomers"}, {"title": "Benzene: a delocalised ring model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Benzene is C₆H₆ with a planar ring of six carbon atoms. Each carbon has three sigma-bond connections and contributes to a delocalised electron system extending around the ring. Its six C–C bonds are equivalent and intermediate in length between typical single and double bonds. A hexagon containing alternating bonds is a useful representation, but benzene does not literally alternate between two different structures. A circle inside the hexagon emphasises delocalisation. The extra stability means benzene commonly undergoes substitution that preserves the ring electron system rather than ordinary alkene-like addition. Explain this limitation when using a simple structural diagram.", "diagram": "benzene"}, {"title": "Dispersion forces and physical properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hydrocarbons are largely non-polar and attract each other through dispersion forces. Within a comparable homologous series, larger molecules usually have more polarisable electron clouds and stronger dispersion attraction, raising boiling point. Branching often reduces intermolecular surface contact and lowers boiling point among alkane isomers. Melting points depend strongly on crystal packing and symmetry and do not always follow the same simple pattern. There is no universal rule that every aromatic compound boils above every alkane or every alkane above every alkene of similar carbon count. Use molecular size, shape and actual data rather than an unsupported category ranking.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Complete and incomplete combustion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Complete combustion with sufficient oxygen converts hydrocarbon carbon to CO₂ and hydrogen to H₂O. Balance carbon first, hydrogen second and oxygen last. Methane: CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). Limited oxygen may produce CO, carbon soot and mixtures of products. A specified equation producing CO only can be balanced, but real incomplete combustion is often more complex. Carbon monoxide is toxic and soot contributes to particulate pollution. Water’s state depends on conditions: hot combustion products contain vapour, while standard combustion enthalpies often specify liquid water. State symbols influence thermochemical interpretation.", "diagram": "combustion"}, {"title": "Addition reactions of alkenes", "kind": "learn", "text": "In addition, the C=C becomes a C–C single bond and new groups attach to its two carbons. Ethene + bromine gives 1,2-dibromoethane: CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br. Hydrogenation adds H₂ using a suitable metal catalyst such as nickel, converting an alkene to an alkane. Acid-catalysed hydration adds H and OH to produce an alcohol. Track which atoms are added and retain all carbons. An unsymmetrical alkene can give regioisomeric products; use the conditions and conventions given in the question. Do not assume every conceivable product forms in equal amount.", "diagram": "addition"}, {"title": "Bromine observations and controlled testing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bromine solution is coloured and can be decolourised rapidly by reaction with an alkene under suitable conditions. The bromine atoms become part of an organic addition product. An alkane ordinarily does not react rapidly in the same test without ultraviolet light. A fair comparison uses similar sample amounts, solvent, temperature and lighting. Decolourisation supports a reactive unsaturation or another bromine-consuming process, so the wider chemical context matters. Never identify a functional group from colour loss alone if other reactive substances are possible. State both the observed colour change and the chemical reason, supported by an appropriate structural equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substitution of alkanes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Substitution replaces a hydrogen with another atom or group. Methane reacts with chlorine under UV light: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. UV initiates a radical process by helping split a chlorine molecule homolytically. Further substitutions can occur, so the product is not necessarily pure chloromethane. For an exam at this level, name the reaction type, write a balanced equation and identify required conditions. The C–C skeleton remains unchanged in simple substitution. Compare this with addition, which consumes a double bond without removing a hydrogen. Molecular formulae alone may hide the reaction site, so structural formulae are often preferable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substitution of benzene", "kind": "learn", "text": "Benzene reacts with bromine using an appropriate Lewis-acid catalyst such as FeBr₃: C₆H₆ + Br₂ → C₆H₅Br + HBr. A ring hydrogen is replaced, and the aromatic delocalised system is retained in the product. This differs from an alkene’s rapid bromine addition without such a catalyst. Do not use fluorine as a routine classroom example because its reactivity makes the comparison unhelpful. Catalysts and light requirements belong with the equation rather than being omitted. Distinguish chemical stability from complete unreactivity: benzene can react, but its favoured pathways and conditions differ from those of isolated alkenes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reaction equations and exam representations", "kind": "learn", "text": "An organic equation should preserve every atom and show the functional part of the structure changing. Use a displayed or condensed structure when asked to show connectivity, place conditions above the reaction arrow and include coproducts such as HCl in substitution. Check carbon valency after changing a double bond to a single bond. In a combustion equation, a molecular formula is usually sufficient to count atoms, but obey the question’s exact representation requirement. A balanced formula can still describe the wrong reaction. First identify the reactant class and reaction type, then construct products and balance atom counts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Fuel choices and an evidence-based comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fuels can be compared by energy per mole, per mass or per useful service. These are different comparisons. A fuel with more energy per mole may contain much heavier molecules. Complete combustion still generates CO₂, even if a fuel is described as renewable. A fair life-cycle assessment considers feedstock growth, land use, transport, processing energy and combustion, as well as conversion efficiency and infrastructure. Evaluate one concrete benefit and one limitation using a stated metric. Do not label a fuel carbon-neutral solely because it is a biofuel. Relate claims to evidence and identify which stages an estimate includes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A complete small-isomer example", "kind": "learn", "text": "C₅H₁₂ has three acyclic alkane constitutional isomers: pentane CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃, 2-methylbutane CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ and 2,2-dimethylpropane C(CH₃)₄. These represent five-, four- and three-carbon longest paths respectively. Moving a methyl branch from position 2 to position 3 of a four-carbon drawing gives the same named skeleton after reversing numbering, not a fourth isomer. Count five total carbons and twelve hydrogens in every candidate and check four bond units per carbon. Use a systematic skeleton method so symmetry does not create duplicates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Addition versus substitution in an exam", "kind": "learn", "text": "For addition, retain the entire alkene skeleton, change C=C to C–C and attach the reagent’s atoms across the original double-bond carbons. For substitution, replace an atom such as H and include the small coproduct, for example HBr. Conditions distinguish dark bromine addition to an alkene, UV alkane halogenation and catalysed benzene halogenation. State observations as visible changes, then separately infer products through equations. A chemical name is an inference rather than a visual observation. If a question asks all atoms and bonds, use a displayed drawing rather than a molecular formula.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: A branched structure", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A branched structure", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Combustion balancing", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Combustion balancing", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Addition and observations", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Addition and observations", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Benzene versus ethene", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Benzene versus ethene", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Names and structures", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: A physical-property claim", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Testing unknown hydrocarbons", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A branched structure", "prompt": "Name CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃ and give its molecular formula.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The longest continuous chain has five carbons: pentane. [1]", "The methyl branch has lowest locant 2, giving 2-methylpentane. [1]", "Six carbons and an acyclic saturated structure give C₆H₁₄. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Combustion balancing", "prompt": "Write a balanced equation for complete combustion of liquid octane, producing CO₂(g) and H₂O(g).", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Carbon requires 8CO₂ per C₈H₁₈; hydrogen requires 9H₂O. [1]", "The products contain 25 oxygen atoms per fuel molecule, requiring 12.5O₂. [1]", "2C₈H₁₈(l) + 25O₂(g) → 16CO₂(g) + 18H₂O(g). [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Addition and observations", "prompt": "Draw a condensed equation for ethene reacting with bromine and explain the observed change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br. [2: correct double-bond change and product connectivity]", "The coloured bromine solution becomes colourless as bromine is consumed. [1]", "The process is addition across C=C. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q3", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Benzene versus ethene", "prompt": "Explain why a diagram with alternating single and double bonds is incomplete for benzene, and compare its bromine reaction with ethene.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Benzene has delocalised electrons around its ring. [1]", "All six C–C bonds are equivalent, not alternating different lengths. [1]", "Ethene readily adds bromine across C=C. [1]", "Benzene typically undergoes catalysed substitution that retains aromaticity. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Names and structures", "prompt": "Write condensed structures for 3-methylpent-1-ene and 2,2-dimethylbutane. Verify carbon valency.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["3-methylpent-1-ene: CH₂=CHCH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃. [2]", "2,2-dimethylbutane: CH₃C(CH₃)₂CH₂CH₃. [2]", "Check the branched central carbons have four total bonds. This check supports the structures but is not an additional mark."], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q5", "allocations": [2, 2, 0]}, {"title": "A physical-property claim", "prompt": "Evaluate the statement: “All larger hydrocarbons have higher melting points, so carbon count alone predicts melting point.”", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Carbon count can increase dispersion attraction in comparable compounds. [1]", "Melting also depends on molecular symmetry and crystal packing. [1]", "The absolute claim is unjustified; use structural comparisons and experimental data. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Testing unknown hydrocarbons", "prompt": "Two labelled samples are hexane and hex-1-ene, but labels are lost. Design a chemical comparison, predict observations and products and identify a condition that would spoil the interpretation.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Use a small controlled bromine test under suitable non-UV conditions with matched sample conditions. [1]", "Hex-1-ene consumes bromine and loses its colour. [1]", "CH₂=CH(CH₂)₃CH₃ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCHBr(CH₂)₃CH₃. [1]", "Hexane does not react rapidly under those same dark conditions. [1]", "UV could initiate alkane substitution and undermine the comparison. [1]", "Reference/blank comparisons and controlled amounts strengthen interpretation. Practical work requires approved supervision. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "An acyclic alkane with six carbons has formula", "o": ["C₆H₁₂", "C₆H₁₄", "C₆H₆", "C₆H₁₀"], "a": 1, "why": "CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ gives 14 hydrogens."}, {"q": "Benzene usually reacts with bromine by", "o": ["Catalysed substitution", "Rapid uncatalysed alkene addition", "Neutralisation", "Precipitation"], "a": 0, "why": "Substitution preserves the aromatic delocalised system."}, {"q": "The parent chain for an alkene must", "o": ["Contain the double bond", "Always be horizontal", "Exclude branches entirely", "Have eight carbons"], "a": 0, "why": "Choose the longest suitable chain containing C=C."}, {"q": "Structural isomers have", "o": ["Different molecular formulae", "The same connectivity", "The same molecular formula but different connectivity", "Different proton counts in carbon"], "a": 2, "why": "Connectivity distinguishes structural isomers."}, {"q": "Ordinary hydrocarbon boiling mainly overcomes", "o": ["C–H bonds", "Nuclear forces", "Dispersion forces between molecules", "All C–C bonds"], "a": 2, "why": "The molecules ordinarily remain chemically intact."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "title": "Moles, equations, stoichiometry and chemical energy", "scope": "Mole calculations; composition; mass stoichiometry; limiting quantities; enthalpy; thermochemical equations; fuel comparisons and calorimetry", "screens": [{"title": "The mole and particle identity", "kind": "learn", "text": "A mole contains 6.022 × 10²³ specified entities using the course data-booklet precision. Specify whether these entities are atoms, molecules, ions or formula units. One mole of CO₂ molecules contains one mole of carbon atoms and two moles of oxygen atoms. One mole of CaCl₂ formula units corresponds to one mole of calcium ions and two moles of chloride ions in an ideal fully dissociated solution. Particle count N = nNₐ. A mole is a counting unit, not a fixed mass. Equal amounts in moles of different substances contain equal entity counts but generally have different masses.", "diagram": "mole"}, {"title": "Molar mass and n = m/M", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molar mass M is mass per mole, usually g mol⁻¹. Add atomic masses for every atom in the formula, including atoms multiplied by parentheses. For Ca(OH)₂, M = 40.08 + 2(16.00 + 1.008) = 74.096 g mol⁻¹ using these stated values. Amount n = m/M, mass m = nM. Keep extra digits during calculations and round the final result to an appropriate precision. Dimensional checking gives g ÷ (g mol⁻¹) = mol. Do not substitute a sample mass into a molar-mass expression or assume that a large mass means a large amount without considering M.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Percentage composition", "kind": "learn", "text": "The mass fraction of an element in a compound is its contribution to formula molar mass divided by the total molar mass. Percentage = element contribution / M × 100. For water, oxygen contributes about 16.00 of 18.016 g mol⁻¹, so oxygen is approximately 88.81% by mass. This differs from atomic percentage, where one of the three atoms is oxygen. A subscript gives an atom ratio, not a mass percentage. When using analytical composition data later to find an empirical formula, convert masses to moles before comparing element ratios. Check that a complete set of mass percentages totals approximately 100%.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Balanced chemical equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A balanced equation conserves each element’s atom count and total charge where ionic species appear. Change coefficients, never internal formula subscripts. Aluminium oxide formation is 4Al(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Al₂O₃(s). Coefficients give ratios of particles and amounts in moles. They do not directly give gram ratios because molar masses differ. State symbols identify physical state and may matter to energy changes and reactions. Start with correct chemical formulae, then balance. A formula such as AlO is not repaired by changing coefficients because it represents the wrong compound. Conservation of mass assumes the full relevant system, including gases, is accounted for.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A mass-to-mass calculation pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use a balanced equation to connect known and unknown amounts. First convert the known mass to moles. Second use the coefficient ratio to obtain moles of the requested species. Third multiply by that species’ molar mass. For 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, magnesium and magnesium oxide have a 1:1 mole ratio, even though their masses are unequal. Write the ratio explicitly as n(MgO)/n(Mg) = 2/2. This avoids applying a coefficient to grams directly. State which reagent is in excess or compare both if amounts are given. Theoretical product calculations assume the stated reaction proceeds as described.", "diagram": "stoichiometry"}, {"title": "Limiting reagent and excess material", "kind": "learn", "text": "When amounts of two reactants are supplied, compare n/coefficient for each. The smaller value limits how far the reaction can proceed. In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 3.00 mol H₂ and 2.00 mol O₂ give ratios 1.50 and 2.00. Hydrogen limits, 3.00 mol water forms and 1.50 mol oxygen is consumed, leaving 0.50 mol oxygen. A smaller mass does not necessarily identify the limiting reagent. Compute the excess remaining from initial amount minus amount consumed, then convert units if required. This method becomes especially important in Year 12 chemical synthesis and percentage-yield calculations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conservation, observations and gas loss", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reaction can appear to lose mass if gas escapes from the measured container. The total mass of a properly defined closed system remains conserved in an ordinary chemical reaction. If acid reacts with carbonate in an open beaker, CO₂ leaves and the beaker’s measured mass decreases. That change can measure reaction progress if other losses are controlled. Conversely, burning magnesium in air can increase the collected solid mass because oxygen is incorporated. Always identify the system boundary. Do not infer that matter is destroyed from a balance reading without considering incoming and outgoing material.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "System, surroundings and enthalpy sign", "kind": "learn", "text": "The reacting substances are the system, while the solvent, container and wider environment can form the surroundings. An exothermic reaction transfers heat from system to surroundings and has ΔH < 0. An endothermic reaction absorbs heat and has ΔH > 0. If a well-insulated solution surrounding an exothermic process warms, the system has lost energy even though the measured temperature rises. Temperature and enthalpy are different quantities. A phase change can transfer energy at nearly constant temperature while particles rearrange. Sign conventions follow the defined system; write the definition before assigning a sign.", "diagram": "enthalpy"}, {"title": "Bond breaking and bond formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Breaking an existing bond requires energy input. Forming a bond releases energy. The overall reaction enthalpy reflects the difference between energy needed to separate reactant bonding and energy released as product bonding forms. Using mean bond energies, ΔH ≈ ΣE(bonds broken) − ΣE(bonds formed). An exothermic reaction releases more during formation than it absorbs during breaking. It is incorrect to say energy comes out simply because a fuel’s bonds are broken. Mean bond energies are approximate gas-phase averages; they do not exactly describe every molecule or phase. Count the number and type of bonds carefully before substituting values.", "diagram": "bond-energy"}, {"title": "Thermochemical equations and amount scaling", "kind": "learn", "text": "A thermochemical equation specifies a balanced reaction, states and the enthalpy change for the written amounts. CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), ΔH = −890 kJ means the reaction as written releases 890 kJ per mole of methane consumed. Doubling all coefficients doubles ΔH. Reversing the reaction reverses the sign. Producing gaseous rather than liquid water gives a different ΔH. If a question asks “energy released,” report a positive magnitude while making the direction clear. If it asks ΔH for the system, include the negative sign for an exothermic process.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Energy profile and activation energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "An energy profile plots system potential energy against reaction progress, not elapsed time. Reactants and products occupy different levels, and the highest point represents a transition-state region along the pathway. Activation energy Eₐ is the energy difference from reactants to that barrier. ΔH is products minus reactants. An exothermic reaction has products lower; an endothermic reaction has products higher. A reaction may be energetically favourable overall yet slow because Eₐ is large. A catalyst supplies a different pathway with lower barriers while leaving reactant and product energies, hence ΔH, unchanged.", "diagram": "energy-profile"}, {"title": "Calorimetry and q = mcΔT", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat gained by a solution is estimated using q = mcΔT, where m is solution mass, c specific heat capacity and ΔT = final − initial temperature. With c in J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, q is in joules. For an insulated reaction, q(reaction) ≈ −q(solution), with additional corrections if the apparatus absorbs heat. Divide reaction heat by moles reacted to estimate molar ΔH. Heat loss to surroundings often makes an exothermic measured temperature rise too small, so the estimated ΔH is less negative than the true value. State assumptions: solution density, heat capacity, negligible apparatus heat and complete reaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Significant figures, units and reasonableness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Measured data limit numerical precision. Keep unrounded intermediate values and round the final result sensibly, normally to the least precise relevant measured input. Stoichiometric coefficients are exact and do not limit significant figures. Convert millilitres to litres for molar concentration calculations and joules to kilojoules for a kJ mol⁻¹ result. A percentage cannot exceed 100% for an ideal pure-product yield, though a wet or impure sample can give an apparent value above 100%. Estimate the order of magnitude before trusting a calculator. Include units at each major step so an incorrect factor of 1000 is visible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing fuel energy and emissions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fuel’s molar combustion energy must be divided by molar mass to compare kJ g⁻¹. For CO₂ per unit energy, use the balanced combustion equation to determine moles of CO₂, then divide its mass by useful or released energy as specified. Methane produces one mole of CO₂ per mole burned; ethanol produces two, but comparing molecule counts alone ignores energy output. Complete combustion, incomplete combustion and real device efficiency affect practical performance. Biofuel production can have land-use and processing impacts. A comparison should state its basis and avoid confusing combustion-stage emissions with full life-cycle greenhouse-gas effects.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluating a fuel calorimetry investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Control water amount, burner distance and initial conditions; repeat trials and measure actual fuel mass consumed. Record maximum temperature consistently and minimise draughts. In an open flame setup, substantial heat escapes and incomplete combustion can occur. Soot may indicate a different combustion pathway, and fuel evaporation can exaggerate apparent fuel consumption. Repetition improves estimates of random variation but does not remove systematic heat loss. An improved method must address a specific limitation, such as an insulated calorimeter with a known heat capacity. Explain how the error affects the calculated energy per gram, rather than listing “human error.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Percentage composition worked reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Using stated values Ca 40.08, C 12.01 and O 16.00, M(CaCO₃) = 100.09 g mol⁻¹. Calcium’s mass percentage is 40.08/100.09 × 100 = 40.04%. Oxygen’s contribution is three atoms: 48.00/100.09 × 100 = 47.96%. The percentages describe mass shares, while the formula gives atom numbers 1:1:3. A student who uses one oxygen atomic mass overlooks the subscript. Your working should show the complete molar-mass sum so the marking pathway is visible and the final check can confirm the element percentages total approximately 100%.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apparatus heat and a corrected model", "kind": "learn", "text": "If the calorimeter has heat capacity Ccal, an improved energy estimate is q(surrounding measured parts) = mcΔT + CcalΔT. Then the reaction heat is its negative under the insulation and completion assumptions. Omitting calorimeter heat underestimates exothermic heat release magnitude. Heat escaping to the room adds a separate loss not captured merely by including Ccal. Explain the direction through the measured temperature rise and energy balance. Use a correction only when supplied or justified data support it; inventing an apparatus constant would create false precision.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive limiting-reagent model", "kind": "interactive", "model": "limiting"}, {"title": "Worked question: Moles to particles", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Moles to particles", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Mass stoichiometry", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Mass stoichiometry", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Reaction energy and mass", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Reaction energy and mass", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Calorimetry with assumptions", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Calorimetry with assumptions", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: A limiting reaction", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Mean bond-energy estimate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Fuel comparison and uncertainty", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Moles to particles", "prompt": "Calculate the amount of CO₂ in 8.80 g using M(CO₂) = 44.0 g mol⁻¹, then calculate the number of oxygen atoms.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(CO₂) = 8.80/44.0 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "Each CO₂ molecule contains two O atoms, so n(O atoms) = 0.400 mol. [1]", "N = 0.400 × 6.022 × 10²³. [1]", "N = 2.41 × 10²³ oxygen atoms. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Mass stoichiometry", "prompt": "Magnesium burns in excess oxygen: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. Find MgO mass from 4.86 g Mg, using M(Mg) = 24.3 and M(MgO) = 40.3 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(Mg) = 4.86/24.3 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "n(MgO)/n(Mg) = 2/2 = 1. [1]", "n(MgO) = 0.200 mol. [1]", "m(MgO) = 0.200 × 40.3 = 8.06 g. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Reaction energy and mass", "prompt": "Methane combustion has ΔH = −890 kJ per mole CH₄. Calculate the heat released by 3.20 g CH₄ using M = 16.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["n(CH₄) = 3.20/16.0 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "System heat = 0.200 × (−890) = −178 kJ. [1]", "Heat released is 178 kJ; the system ΔH is negative. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Calorimetry with assumptions", "prompt": "A reaction of 0.0500 mol warms 100.0 g solution from 20.0 to 26.0 °C. Use c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and neglect apparatus heat. Find molar ΔH.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["ΔT = 6.0 K. [1]", "q(solution) = 100.0 × 4.18 × 6.0 = 2508 J. [1]", "q(reaction) = −2.508 kJ. [1]", "ΔH = −2.508/0.0500 = −50.16 kJ mol⁻¹, approximately −50 kJ mol⁻¹ at the limiting precision. [1]", "Assume negligible external heat loss and solution heat capacity as specified. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A limiting reaction", "prompt": "For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, start with 4.00 mol H₂ and 1.50 mol O₂. Find the limiting reagent, water formed and excess remaining.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Compare n/coefficient: hydrogen 2.00; oxygen 1.50. [1]", "O₂ is limiting. [1]", "Water formed = 2 × 1.50 = 3.00 mol. [1]", "Hydrogen consumed = 3.00 mol, leaving 1.00 mol. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Mean bond-energy estimate", "prompt": "For H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl, use H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243 and H–Cl 431 kJ mol⁻¹. Estimate ΔH and explain the sign.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Breaking: 436 + 243 = 679 kJ. [1]", "Formation: 2 × 431 = 862 kJ released. [1]", "ΔH ≈ 679 − 862 = −183 kJ per written reaction. [1]", "More energy is released on bond formation than absorbed on breaking, so the reaction is exothermic. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Fuel comparison and uncertainty", "prompt": "Fuel A releases 1000 kJ mol⁻¹ and has molar mass 50.0 g mol⁻¹; B releases 1500 kJ mol⁻¹ and has mass 100.0 g mol⁻¹. Compare energy per gram, then evaluate an open-flame experimental claim of exact agreement with these values.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["A: 1000/50.0 = 20.0 kJ g⁻¹. [1]", "B: 1500/100.0 = 15.0 kJ g⁻¹. [1]", "A has greater released energy per mass despite B having greater molar energy. [1]", "Open flame heat loss can lower measured energy magnitude. [1]", "Incomplete combustion or evaporation can introduce additional bias. [1]", "Repeat and improve heat accounting; exact agreement cannot be assumed without uncertainty and method evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Balanced coefficients directly express ratios of", "o": ["Grams only", "Moles of species", "Densities", "Temperatures"], "a": 1, "why": "Convert grams to moles before applying stoichiometric coefficients."}, {"q": "An exothermic system has", "o": ["Positive ΔH", "Negative ΔH", "Zero bond formation", "No activation barrier"], "a": 1, "why": "The system transfers heat to its surroundings."}, {"q": "Bond breaking", "o": ["Always releases heat", "Requires energy", "Creates mass", "Is the sole cause of exothermicity"], "a": 1, "why": "Bond formation releases energy; the overall balance determines ΔH."}, {"q": "For 2H₂ + O₂, 1 mol O₂ can form", "o": ["1 mol H₂O", "2 mol H₂O", "0.5 mol H₂O", "4 mol H₂O"], "a": 1, "why": "The O₂:H₂O coefficient ratio is 1:2 with sufficient hydrogen."}, {"q": "Heat loss in exothermic calorimetry usually makes measured ΔH", "o": ["More negative", "Less negative", "Always positive", "Independent of temperature"], "a": 1, "why": "A reduced temperature rise underestimates released heat magnitude."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "title": "Lewis structures, shape and intermolecular forces", "scope": "Lewis diagrams; VSEPR; polarity; dispersion, dipole–dipole and hydrogen bonding; vapour pressure and boiling", "screens": [{"title": "Lewis structures: counting valence electrons", "kind": "learn", "text": "A Lewis structure represents valence electrons as dots or bonding lines. First total the valence electrons, adding electrons for negative charge and subtracting for positive charge. Choose a sensible central atom, connect surrounding atoms with single bonds, complete outer shells where appropriate and allocate remaining electrons. Hydrogen has a two-electron shell and forms one ordinary bond. The usual octet pattern is useful for common main-group molecules but has exceptions. For CO₂, 4 + 2(6) = 16 valence electrons and the final structure is O=C=O with two lone pairs on each oxygen. Check both electron count and atom connectivity.", "diagram": "lewis"}, {"title": "Bonding pairs, lone pairs and multiple bonds", "kind": "learn", "text": "A bonding pair is shared between atoms. A lone pair belongs primarily to one atom in the Lewis representation. A single, double or triple bond contains one, two or three shared pairs. For VSEPR, each bond connection occupies one electron domain irrespective of bond multiplicity. Water has two O–H bonding domains and two lone-pair domains around oxygen. Ammonia has three bonding domains and one lone-pair domain around nitrogen. CO₂ has two bonding domains around carbon and no central lone pairs. Always count domains around the atom whose geometry you are describing, not the total electrons in the whole molecule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "VSEPR: repulsion and geometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Valence-shell electron-pair repulsion models regions of electron density as arranging themselves to reduce repulsion around a central atom. Two domains favour a linear arrangement at 180°, three a trigonal planar arrangement near 120°, and four a tetrahedral arrangement near 109.5°. Electron-domain geometry includes lone pairs; molecular shape describes the positions of atoms. A tetrahedral domain arrangement can therefore give tetrahedral, trigonal pyramidal or bent molecular shapes depending on lone pairs. VSEPR predicts approximate shapes rather than exact atom sizes or all bond angles. A flat drawing uses wedges or other cues to represent three-dimensional arrangement.", "diagram": "vsepr"}, {"title": "Linear and trigonal planar examples", "kind": "learn", "text": "CO₂ is linear because carbon has two bonding domains and no lone pairs. The double bonds count as domains, not four separate directions. Boron trifluoride has three bonding domains and no central lone pair, giving a trigonal planar structure. Boron’s incomplete octet in this molecule is an important reminder that not every stable structure follows a simple octet rule. All atoms in a trigonal planar central arrangement lie approximately in one plane. Identify the central atom, count domains, give geometry and state the angle. Geometry and bond polarity must then be considered together when deciding overall molecular polarity.", "diagram": "linear-planar"}, {"title": "Tetrahedral, pyramidal and bent molecules", "kind": "learn", "text": "CH₄ has four bonding domains, giving a tetrahedral shape. NH₃ has three bonds and one lone pair, so its atoms form a trigonal pyramid. H₂O has two bonds and two lone pairs, so it is bent. Lone-pair electron density occupies more space near the central atom than a bonding pair, tending to compress bond angles. Approximate familiar values are 109.5° for methane, 107° for ammonia and 104.5° for water. These values illustrate a trend rather than a universal formula for every molecule with the same domain count. The lone pairs are essential to explaining the shapes.", "diagram": "shapes"}, {"title": "Bond polarity and partial charges", "kind": "learn", "text": "A covalent bond becomes polar when its atoms attract shared electrons unequally. The more electronegative atom receives δ− and the other δ+. In H–Cl, chlorine is δ−. The shared electrons remain part of the covalent bonding; partial charges are not complete electron transfer into free ions. A bond-dipole arrow often points toward the more electronegative end, with a crossed tail at the positive end. State the arrow convention if your diagram uses one. Compare electronegativity using the data provided. Polarity depends on electron distribution, not whether the substance is currently dissolved in water.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Molecular polarity and vector cancellation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Overall polarity depends on the vector sum of bond dipoles and molecular symmetry. CO₂ has polar C=O bonds but a linear symmetric shape, so the equal opposite dipoles cancel and the molecule is non-polar. Water has polar O–H bonds in a bent arrangement; they do not cancel, so it is polar. CH₄ is symmetric and non-polar, while CH₃Cl is not equivalently substituted and is polar. “Contains a polar bond” is therefore insufficient to establish a polar molecule. Draw the actual geometry, indicate relevant dipoles and explain cancellation or lack of cancellation.", "diagram": "polarity"}, {"title": "Dispersion forces in every molecule", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electron distributions fluctuate, creating temporary dipoles that can induce dipoles in neighbouring particles. These dispersion forces occur in all atoms and molecules, including non-polar species and substances that also hydrogen bond. Larger, more polarisable electron clouds generally produce stronger dispersion attraction. Shape affects how closely molecules can contact one another. Do not equate dispersion strength simply with the number of bonds or with molecular mass in unrelated substances. A small hydrogen-bonding molecule need not boil above every large non-polar molecule. Compare the complete combination of forces and molecular size.", "diagram": "dispersion"}, {"title": "Permanent dipole–dipole attraction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Polar molecules have permanent uneven charge distributions. Opposite partial charges on neighbouring molecules attract, producing dipole–dipole interactions in addition to dispersion forces. A useful diagram shows δ+ near δ− between molecules, not unlike charges within a single bond being labelled as an intermolecular force. Molecules move and rotate, so not every orientation is equally favourable at each instant. Dipole–dipole attraction often raises boiling point when comparing similarly sized molecules, but shape, polarisability and hydrogen bonding must also be considered. Polarity does not automatically imply that a molecule can donate a hydrogen bond.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydrogen bonding: donor and acceptor", "kind": "learn", "text": "A common ATAR hydrogen-bond model involves a hydrogen bonded covalently to N, O or F interacting with a lone pair on N, O or F of another molecule. An O–H bond is a covalent bond within a molecule. An O···H attraction between molecules is a hydrogen bond. Water and ethanol can both donate and accept. A ketone can accept hydrogen bonds from water through oxygen but cannot hydrogen bond to an identical ketone as a donor because it lacks O–H or N–H. All these molecules also experience dispersion forces. The dotted line in the model must connect the correct donor hydrogen and acceptor atom.", "diagram": "hydrogen-bond"}, {"title": "Boiling point explained through forces", "kind": "learn", "text": "A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals external pressure. Stronger intermolecular attractions usually require a higher temperature to achieve this, because more energy is required for molecules to escape into the vapour. Compare ethanol and dimethyl ether of similar molecular formula: ethanol can form hydrogen bonds between its molecules, while the ether lacks a hydrogen-bond donor, so ethanol has a substantially higher boiling point. Explain the species, forces and energy link. Boiling ordinary molecular liquids does not break their internal covalent bonds. Use “overcomes intermolecular attractions” rather than “breaks the molecules.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vapour pressure and temperature", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a closed container, molecules escape from liquid and return by condensation. At equilibrium, vapour pressure depends on temperature and substance. At the same temperature, a liquid with weaker intermolecular attraction generally has a greater equilibrium vapour pressure. Raising temperature increases the fraction of molecules able to escape, increasing vapour pressure. The equilibrium remains dynamic even when no visible change occurs. Evaporation can occur below boiling point; boiling forms bubbles throughout the liquid when vapour pressure reaches external pressure. A lower external pressure lowers the boiling temperature. Keep external pressure distinct from the substance’s equilibrium vapour pressure.", "diagram": "vapour"}, {"title": "Melting and solubility need more than a rule", "kind": "learn", "text": "Melting involves disrupting a solid’s ordered arrangement and depends on crystal structure, molecular symmetry and attraction. Boiling trends cannot always be transferred directly to melting. Dissolution requires breaking some solute–solute and solvent–solvent interactions while forming solute–solvent interactions, with entropy also contributing. “Like dissolves like” is a useful pattern, not a complete mechanism. Polar and hydrogen-bonding groups can favour water solubility, while a large non-polar carbon region can oppose it. Use a specific interaction such as hydrogen bonding or ion–dipole attraction. A molecule may be polar yet only sparingly soluble.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Model limitations and unfamiliar molecules", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lewis structures are two-dimensional electron bookkeeping diagrams. VSEPR approximates spatial arrangement through repulsion. Dipole arrows represent charge distribution and vector direction. None shows realistic atom scale, electron motion or every energetic contribution. For an unfamiliar molecule, count electron domains, predict shape, assess bond polarities and cancellation, then identify intermolecular forces. If the molecule is an ion, include its charge and recognise that interactions can differ from those of a neutral molecule. Explain how the evidence supports the model and which predictions are qualitative. A diagram is useful only when its labels and reasoning are understood.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A four-part exam explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "To compare molecular properties, begin with the relevant structure. Next identify molecular polarity and any hydrogen-bond donor and acceptor sites. Then name the intermolecular attractions, including dispersion. Finally connect their combined strength to the energy required for separation or to solvent interactions. This sequence avoids jumping from a formula directly to a boiling-point claim. Match detail to the question: a three-mark comparison might credit the structural distinction, the force difference and the energy consequence. For substantially different molecular sizes, explicitly consider polarisability rather than assuming hydrogen bonding must always dominate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Lewis electrons must actually be shown", "kind": "learn", "text": "For NH₃, nitrogen contributes five valence electrons and three hydrogens contribute three, totaling eight. Three N–H bonding pairs use six, leaving one lone pair. For H₂O, eight total valence electrons form two bonding pairs and two lone pairs. For CO₂, sixteen total electrons give two double bonds and two lone pairs on each oxygen. A displayed bond line counts as two electrons. Verify the drawing’s electron total and show the lone pairs when a Lewis structure is requested. A molecular-shape sketch without dots is not a complete Lewis answer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ion–dipole differs from dipole–dipole", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ion interacting with a polar molecule experiences ion–dipole attraction. Na⁺ in water is stabilised by the oxygen-rich partial-negative region, while Cl⁻ interacts with partial-positive hydrogen regions. Dipole–dipole attraction instead refers to permanent partial charges on neutral polar molecules. A water-solubility comparison for an ionic salt needs ion–dipole language, not merely hydrogen bonding. A charge label should distinguish a full ionic charge from δ notation. The named attraction must connect the actual interacting species, because generic force labels without chemical context do not fully explain a property.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Shape and polarity of water", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Shape and polarity of water", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: A boiling comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: A boiling comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Intermolecular site identification", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Intermolecular site identification", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Ammonia model", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Vapour pressure reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: An unfamiliar molecular comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Shape and polarity of water", "prompt": "Use electron domains to explain water’s shape and polarity.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["O has two bonding domains and two lone-pair domains. [1]", "Four domains have an approximately tetrahedral electron-domain arrangement. [1]", "With only two bonded atoms, the molecular shape is bent. [1]", "O–H bonds are polar toward oxygen. [1]", "The bent arrangement prevents dipole cancellation, giving a polar molecule. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "prompt": "Explain why CO₂ is non-polar despite containing polar C=O bonds.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["O is more electronegative, so each C=O bond is polar. [1]", "CO₂ is linear and symmetric. [1]", "The equal bond dipoles oppose and cancel. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A boiling comparison", "prompt": "Ethanol and dimethyl ether both have formula C₂H₆O. Explain why ethanol’s boiling point is higher.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ethanol has an O–H group, while dimethyl ether does not. [1]", "Ethanol molecules can hydrogen bond to one another. [1]", "The ether has dispersion and dipole–dipole interactions but no identical-molecule hydrogen-bond donor. [1]", "More energy is required to separate ethanol molecules, increasing boiling point. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Intermolecular site identification", "prompt": "Explain why propanone can hydrogen bond with water but does not form ordinary donor–acceptor hydrogen bonds between identical propanone molecules.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Its carbonyl oxygen has lone pairs and can accept a hydrogen bond. [1]", "Water provides an O–H donor hydrogen. [1]", "Propanone has no N–H, O–H or F–H group to act as that donor to itself. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ammonia model", "prompt": "Draw a Lewis structure of NH₃, predict its molecular shape and identify its intermolecular forces.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["N has three N–H bonding pairs and one lone pair; the drawing accounts for eight valence electrons. [2]", "Trigonal pyramidal shape. [1]", "It is polar and can form hydrogen bonds between molecules. [1]", "Dispersion forces and dipole–dipole interactions also occur. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q5", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Vapour pressure reasoning", "prompt": "At the same temperature, liquid A has weaker intermolecular attractions than similarly sized liquid B. Predict their relative vapour pressures and justify.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["A has the greater equilibrium vapour pressure. [1]", "Its molecules require less energy to escape intermolecular attraction. [1]", "A larger fraction enters the vapour at the given temperature until dynamic equilibrium is established. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "An unfamiliar molecular comparison", "prompt": "Compare CH₄, CH₃Cl and CH₃OH for molecular polarity and possible intermolecular forces. Explain why a boiling comparison must also consider molecular size.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["CH₄ is symmetric and non-polar, with dispersion between molecules. [1]", "CH₃Cl is unsymmetrically substituted and polar. [1]", "It has dispersion and dipole–dipole interactions. [1]", "CH₃OH is polar and has an O–H hydrogen-bond donor plus oxygen acceptor. [1]", "Methanol therefore also hydrogen bonds between molecules. [1]", "Polarisability and size affect dispersion, so a force label alone is not a universal boiling ranking. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Water has how many electron domains around oxygen?", "o": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "a": 2, "why": "Two bonding domains and two lone pairs give four domains."}, {"q": "A double bond counts in VSEPR as", "o": ["One domain", "Two directions", "Four domains", "No electron density"], "a": 0, "why": "Multiple bonding along one atom connection occupies one domain."}, {"q": "Dispersion forces occur in", "o": ["Non-polar molecules only", "All molecules", "Only ionic solids", "Only water"], "a": 1, "why": "Temporary fluctuations can occur in every molecular electron cloud."}, {"q": "CO₂ is non-polar because", "o": ["Its bonds are non-polar", "It has no electrons", "Its linear bond dipoles cancel", "O has lower electronegativity"], "a": 2, "why": "Geometry cancels its polar bond contributions."}, {"q": "Boiling a molecular liquid normally", "o": ["Breaks all covalent bonds", "Overcomes intermolecular attractions", "Changes nuclei", "Requires ion formation"], "a": 1, "why": "Molecules ordinarily remain intact in the vapour."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "title": "Chromatography and gas behaviour", "scope": "TLC, GC and HPLC interpretation; mobile/stationary interactions; kinetic theory; pressure, volume and temperature; gas stoichiometry at SCSA STP", "screens": [{"title": "Two phases in chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chromatography uses a stationary phase that remains in place and a mobile phase that carries components through or over it. Substances interact differently with the two phases, so they spend different proportions of time moving. A component more strongly retained by the stationary phase usually moves more slowly under the same conditions. Separation depends on chemical interactions, not simply particle size or mass. The choice of solvent and stationary material changes the result. Two substances may fail to separate under one set of conditions and separate under another. Label the phases and direction of movement in any explanatory diagram.", "diagram": "tlc"}, {"title": "Thin-layer chromatography procedure", "kind": "learn", "text": "TLC uses a thin stationary coating such as silica on a plate. Place small sample spots on a pencil baseline above the starting solvent level. The mobile solvent rises by capillary action. Mark the solvent front promptly after removing the plate and visualise spots if needed. If the baseline is submerged, samples can dissolve directly into the reservoir and undermine separation. Pencil avoids introducing soluble ink dyes. Use the same plate, solvent and conditions for samples and reference standards. Large overloaded spots may overlap, reducing interpretability. Practical decisions should be explained through their effects on separation and measurement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Retention factor calculations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For TLC, Rf = distance travelled by centre of component spot / distance travelled by solvent front, both measured from the same baseline. Rf is dimensionless and is normally between 0 and 1. A spot 3.0 cm from the baseline with solvent front 6.0 cm gives Rf = 0.50. Measuring from the bottom of the plate instead introduces a systematic error. Rf depends on the phase materials and conditions and is not an intrinsic constant valid in every solvent. Similar Rf values support identity when references run under matching conditions, but co-migration does not prove two samples contain the same substance.", "diagram": "rf"}, {"title": "Purity and mixture identification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Several separated spots show that a sample contains at least several detectable components under those conditions. One spot supports apparent purity but does not prove absolute purity: substances can co-migrate or fail to be detected. Compare unknown spots with known standards run together. A shared position is evidence that a component may match, strengthened by repeating with a different solvent system. Do not count spot darkness as mole quantity without a validated calibration. An exam answer should distinguish observation, inference and limitation: “two spots” is observed; “at least two detectable components” is inferred; hidden overlap remains possible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gas chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "GC uses a carrier gas as mobile phase and a stationary material within a column. A vaporised sample’s components interact differently with the stationary phase and travel to a detector at different retention times. Components must be sufficiently volatile and thermally stable under the chosen conditions. A chromatogram displays detector response against time. Peaks correspond to detected components, while a reference comparison helps identify them. Retention time depends on column and operating conditions. Molecular mass alone does not determine the order because volatility and stationary-phase affinity both matter. Calibration is necessary for quantitative use of peak areas.", "diagram": "chromatogram"}, {"title": "High-performance liquid chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "HPLC pumps a liquid mobile phase through a packed column under high pressure. It can analyse less volatile or heat-sensitive compounds that may not suit GC. Separation reflects differences in interactions with the stationary and mobile phases. In reversed-phase HPLC, a relatively non-polar stationary phase can retain non-polar components more strongly, but the specific mobile-phase composition also matters. Avoid a universal rule that “polar always comes first” without specifying the system. A detector records peaks. Changing solvent composition, flow rate or column material changes retention, so unknown and standard data must be comparable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reading a chromatogram quantitatively", "kind": "learn", "text": "Peak retention time is commonly used in identification comparisons. Peak area can support quantity when detector response and calibration are known. Equal peak areas do not automatically mean equal masses for different substances because response factors can differ. Overlapping peaks can hide components. A blank run can reveal contamination, and replicate injections can assess instrumental repeatability. To estimate composition from area, state the assumptions or use supplied response-factor data. A chromatogram does not directly show molecular structure. Combine it with known references, chemical tests and other analytical evidence as the question requires.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Kinetic theory of an ideal gas", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ideal-gas model treats particles as moving continuously and randomly, with negligible particle volume relative to the container and negligible intermolecular attraction. Collisions are elastic in the ideal model. Gas pressure arises from particles transferring momentum to container walls, not from the particles being “compressed” internally. Temperature in kelvin relates to average kinetic energy. Particles have a distribution of speeds; they do not all move at the same speed. Real gases deviate more at high pressure and low temperature because volume and attractions become significant. Use the model to explain trends while stating these limitations.", "diagram": "gas-particles"}, {"title": "Pressure and volume at constant temperature", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, reducing volume increases the frequency of collisions with each unit area of the container wall, so pressure rises. The average particle kinetic energy remains constant because temperature is fixed. The ideal relationship is inverse, P ∝ 1/V, or P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ when a numerical gas-law calculation is requested. Halving volume doubles pressure under the assumptions. A explanation should not claim particles move faster solely because the container gets smaller. Distinguish collision frequency with walls from the energies of the particles.", "diagram": "boyle"}, {"title": "Temperature at constant volume or pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "At fixed amount and volume, raising kelvin temperature increases average particle kinetic energy. Wall collisions become more forceful and more frequent, increasing pressure. At fixed pressure, the volume expands as temperature rises, reducing collision frequency per area enough to balance the greater particle energy. Proportional gas relationships require kelvin, where 0 °C = 273.15 K. Doubling degrees Celsius does not generally double absolute temperature. State which variables remain constant before applying a relationship. In an exam explanation, connect the temperature change to particle motion and then to the observed pressure or volume.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amount of gas and the ideal gas equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "At fixed temperature and volume, adding more gas particles increases wall-collision frequency and pressure. The ideal-gas equation PV = nRT combines amount, pressure, volume and temperature. With P in kPa and V in L, R = 8.314 kPa L mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ is numerically equivalent to the booklet’s 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ because 1 kPa L = 1 J. Year 11 SCSA explicitly requires qualitative kinetic relationships and gas volumes at STP. The general equation is useful preparation and data-booklet literacy; numerical use is identified where included. Never mix pressure or volume units without converting.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "SCSA standard temperature and pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2026 SCSA Chemistry data booklet defines STP as 0.0 °C and 100.0 kPa. One mole of an ideal gas occupies 22.71 L under those conditions. Thus n = V/22.71 when volume is in litres. Do not substitute the familiar 22.4 L value associated with a different pressure convention. The molar volume applies to a gas under the specified conditions, not liquids, solids or a gas at any temperature. Convert millilitres to litres before using the formula. In an exam, use the data booklet supplied for that examination if a future edition changes its constants.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gas stoichiometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "A balanced reaction gives mole ratios, which connect gas volumes under the same temperature and pressure. For 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), hydrogen and oxygen volumes react in a 2:1 ratio if measured comparably. If water condenses, its liquid volume does not follow the gas ratio. For a solid producing CO₂ at STP, convert solid mass to moles, apply the reaction ratio, then multiply gas moles by 22.71 L mol⁻¹. Distinguish initial total gas volume from the volume of one requested product and check limiting reactants if more than one quantity is supplied.", "diagram": "gas-stoich"}, {"title": "Gas collection and experimental limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "A gas syringe measures volume directly but leaks, friction and reading resolution can affect results. Collection over water can dissolve some gas and includes water vapour, so correction may be needed for accurate dry-gas quantities. Temperature and pressure must be known or controlled to compare volumes. Delay after mixing can miss early gas formation. A leak generally underestimates collected gas, leading to a low calculated amount. Repeating trials assesses variation but will not cure a consistent leak. Select an improvement that addresses the specific error and describe the direction of its effect on the reported result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combining analytical evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "To identify an unknown, compare independent evidence rather than relying on one clue. TLC may suggest several components, GC may separate volatile constituents, and a gas-producing reaction can quantify one reactive component. Each method has a domain and limitations. An appropriate method depends on sample amount, volatility, stability, expected concentration and available calibration. Evaluate sensitivity and specificity separately: detecting a small amount is different from uniquely identifying it. Report a supported conclusion with its uncertainty. A persuasive exam response uses actual supplied peak times, distances or gas volumes rather than repeating a generic technique description.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choosing phases changes retention", "kind": "learn", "text": "On a common polar silica TLC phase with a suitable mobile solvent, stronger polar stationary interactions often give lower Rf. In a reversed-phase HPLC arrangement, the relatively non-polar stationary material can retain non-polar substances more strongly. These are contextual tendencies, not one rule for all chromatography. The solvent competes for interactions and changing it can alter separation. When comparing retention times or Rf, state the stationary phase, mobile conditions and relevant molecular groups. Do not identify a compound by retention data from a different column or solvent without verification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked ideal-gas extension", "kind": "learn", "text": "Extension: for 0.100 mol ideal gas at 298 K and 100 kPa, V = nRT/P = (0.100 × 8.314 × 298)/100 = 2.48 L using kPa and L. This differs from its 2.271 L volume at SCSA STP because temperature is higher. Year 11’s explicit gas-volume requirement focuses on STP plus qualitative kinetic relationships; this calculation introduces the general equation found in the booklet. Always state the assumptions and convert temperature to kelvin. The numerical constant works here because kPa L is equivalent to joules.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive gas model: fixed amount", "kind": "interactive", "model": "gas"}, {"title": "Worked question: Rf and comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Rf and comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Interpreting detector areas", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Interpreting detector areas", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Gas volume from a solid", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Gas volume from a solid", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Pressure reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Pressure reasoning", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: A chromatography claim", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: STP volume ratio", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Choosing an analytical method", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Rf and comparison", "prompt": "A TLC spot travels 4.20 cm and the solvent front 7.00 cm from the baseline. Calculate Rf. A reference has the same Rf under these conditions. Is identity proven?", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Rf = 4.20/7.00. [1]", "Rf = 0.600, dimensionless. [1]", "Matching retention supports a possible identity under the same conditions. [1]", "It does not prove identity because different substances can co-migrate. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Interpreting detector areas", "prompt": "Two GC peaks have calibrated response factors of 2.0 area units per mg for A and 1.0 for B. Their areas are both 10.0 units. Find mass percentage of A.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["m(A) = 10.0/2.0 = 5.0 mg. [1]", "m(B) = 10.0/1.0 = 10.0 mg. [1]", "Total mass = 15.0 mg. [1]", "A mass percentage = 5.0/15.0 × 100 = approximately 33%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Gas volume from a solid", "prompt": "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Find CO₂ volume at SCSA STP from 5.00 g CaCO₃, M = 100.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(CaCO₃) = 5.00/100.0 = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "The reactant:CO₂ mole ratio is 1:1. [1]", "n(CO₂) = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "V = 0.0500 × 22.71 = 1.14 L at STP. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Pressure reasoning", "prompt": "A sealed fixed-volume vessel is heated. Explain the pressure increase using kinetic theory.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Average kinetic energy rises with absolute temperature. [1]", "Particles collide with walls more frequently and transfer more momentum per collision on average. [1]", "Greater momentum transfer per unit area per time raises pressure. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A chromatography claim", "prompt": "A sample gives one HPLC peak. Evaluate the claim that it is certainly pure and suggest a stronger investigation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["One detected peak supports apparent purity under the chosen conditions. [1]", "Components may overlap or fail to be detected. [1]", "Use another separation condition or independent analytical method. [1]", "Compare standards and blank runs under matched conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "STP volume ratio", "prompt": "At the same temperature and pressure, 150 mL hydrogen reacts with 60.0 mL oxygen. Which gas limits and how much hydrogen remains? Use 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The gas volume ratio is H₂:O₂ = 2:1. [1]", "60.0 mL O₂ requires 120 mL H₂. [1]", "Oxygen limits because sufficient hydrogen is present. [1]", "150 − 120 = 30 mL H₂ remains, at the stated common conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Choosing an analytical method", "prompt": "A heat-sensitive dissolved compound is to be separated from related impurities. Compare HPLC with GC, explain what one HPLC peak can establish and give a method improvement.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["HPLC uses a liquid mobile phase and need not vaporise the sample in the same way as GC. [1]", "GC generally requires adequate volatility and thermal stability under its conditions. [1]", "HPLC may therefore be more suitable for this compound. [1]", "One peak supports apparent purity under the conditions. [1]", "Co-elution or detector insensitivity may hide impurities. [1]", "Use alternative conditions, reference standards and an independent method to strengthen the conclusion. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "TLC Rf is measured from", "o": ["The plate top", "The same baseline for spot and solvent", "Different reference points", "The solvent reservoir floor"], "a": 1, "why": "Both distances must begin at the sample baseline."}, {"q": "SCSA 2026 STP molar volume is", "o": ["22.4 L", "24.0 L", "22.71 L", "1.00 L"], "a": 2, "why": "The booklet specifies 0 °C and 100 kPa, giving 22.71 L mol⁻¹."}, {"q": "Compressing an ideal gas at constant temperature increases pressure because", "o": ["Average speed necessarily increases", "Wall collision frequency per area increases", "Each atom shrinks", "New gas is formed"], "a": 1, "why": "Temperature fixes the average kinetic energy in this comparison."}, {"q": "GC normally uses which mobile phase?", "o": ["A gas", "A stationary solid", "A vacuum with no particles", "A permanent liquid reservoir"], "a": 0, "why": "A carrier gas moves components through the column."}, {"q": "Equal peak areas for different chemicals imply equal mass", "o": ["Always", "Only with appropriate response calibration assumptions", "Never under any circumstances", "Only if colours match"], "a": 1, "why": "Detector responses can differ between compounds."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "title": "Water, solutions, precipitation and acidity", "scope": "Hydrogen bonding and water; solubility; concentration units; ions and precipitation; Arrhenius acids/bases; pH and solution stoichiometry", "screens": [{"title": "Water’s bent polar structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water has two O–H bonds and two lone pairs around oxygen, giving a bent molecular shape. Oxygen attracts bonding electrons more strongly, so O is δ− and H is δ+. The bond dipoles do not cancel. Water molecules can form extensive hydrogen-bond interactions because each has donor hydrogens and lone-pair acceptor sites. This helps explain water’s unusually high boiling point for a small molecule and substantial surface tension. The hydrogen bonds continually reorganise in a liquid. A diagram with fixed neighbouring molecules is a snapshot, not a rigid structure that every liquid-water molecule occupies permanently.", "diagram": "water"}, {"title": "Ice density and surface tension", "kind": "learn", "text": "In ordinary ice, hydrogen bonding holds water molecules in a relatively open arrangement. Liquid water can pack more closely as the network reorganises, so ordinary ice is less dense and floats. This is not because water molecules themselves grow larger on freezing. Surface molecules experience an imbalance of cohesive attractions, contributing to surface tension and a tendency to minimise surface area. A detergent can change interfacial behaviour by interacting with water and the interface. Explain a property through molecular arrangement and forces, not by saying water is “special.” Density is mass divided by volume and should be distinguished from viscosity.", "diagram": "ice"}, {"title": "Dissolving ionic substances", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water’s oxygen-rich δ− end orients toward cations, while δ+ hydrogen ends orient toward anions. Ion–dipole attractions stabilise separated hydrated ions. Dissolution requires overcoming part of the lattice attraction and reorganising solvent interactions, while hydration forms new interactions. Whether a salt dissolves appreciably depends on the overall energetic and entropy balance, not merely the presence of water. NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) describes dissociation without creating new nuclei. A hydration diagram should show appropriate orientations around both charge types. The dissolved ions remain charged and can migrate, explaining electrolyte conductivity.", "diagram": "hydration"}, {"title": "Molecular solutes and solvent interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polar molecular solute may form dipole interactions or hydrogen bonds with water. Ethanol can hydrogen bond through its O–H group and oxygen lone pairs. A hydrocarbon such as hexane has dispersion interactions but cannot compensate sufficiently for disruption of water’s strong interactions, so water solubility is low. Longer alcohol carbon chains can reduce water solubility despite retaining an O–H group. Dissolution does not always involve ion formation: sugar dissolves mainly as molecules and is not a strong electrolyte. Distinguish dissolve, dissociate and ionise when explaining why a solution does or does not conduct.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Unsaturated, saturated and supersaturated", "kind": "learn", "text": "At a stated temperature, an unsaturated solution can dissolve more solute. A saturated solution is at its equilibrium solubility, often in contact with excess undissolved solid. A supersaturated solution contains more dissolved solute than the equilibrium amount and can crystallise when disturbed or seeded. It is a metastable condition, not a solution simply containing a lot of visible solid. Solubility varies with substance and temperature; many solid solutes become more soluble when heated, but this is not universal. Gases often become less soluble as temperature rises. Use a supplied solubility curve rather than assuming every substance behaves like potassium nitrate.", "diagram": "solubility"}, {"title": "Molar concentration and mass concentration", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molar concentration c = n/V uses solute moles and total solution volume in litres. Mass concentration in g L⁻¹ = solute mass / solution volume in litres. For 0.100 mol solute in 250.0 mL solution, c = 0.100/0.2500 = 0.400 mol L⁻¹. The denominator is final solution volume, not necessarily the initial water volume added. Relate the two units through molar mass: g L⁻¹ = (mol L⁻¹)(g mol⁻¹). Record the chemical species because a solution’s formula-unit concentration differs from individual ion concentrations. Unit conversion is part of the reasoning, not a hidden calculator step.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Parts per million", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mass-based ppm expresses mg solute per kg solution. In a dilute aqueous solution with density close to 1.00 kg L⁻¹, the numerical value is approximately mg L⁻¹. This approximation is not valid for every solvent or concentrated solution. A 2.00 mg solute in 0.500 kg solution gives 4.00 ppm by mass. To convert g L⁻¹ to mol L⁻¹, divide by molar mass. To convert ppm to another concentration, identify the definition and any density assumption first. In water-quality questions, distinguish the mass of an ion or element from the mass of an entire compound used to supply it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Making a solution and dilution", "kind": "learn", "text": "A volumetric flask establishes an accurate final volume. Dissolve a measured solute, transfer quantitatively with washings, allow temperature to equilibrate and fill to the calibration mark. Mix thoroughly. Dilution conserves solute amount, so c₁V₁ = c₂V₂ for the same solute when no reaction or loss occurs. A pipette transfers a measured aliquot into a flask and solvent is added to the final mark. Using a beaker’s markings gives poorer volumetric accuracy. Overfilling a flask makes the actual concentration lower than intended if the solute amount is unchanged. Explain the concentration effect through n/V.", "diagram": "volumetric"}, {"title": "Individual ion concentrations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For complete dissociation, multiply the compound’s concentration by the ion coefficient. A 0.200 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ solution has [Ca²⁺] = 0.200 and [Cl⁻] = 0.400 mol L⁻¹. After mixing solutions without reaction, calculate each ion’s total moles and divide by the total solution volume, assuming volumes are additive. If precipitation occurs, subtract reacting ion amounts before dividing. Ion charge does not multiply the concentration: the “2+” in Ca²⁺ denotes charge, not two calcium ions. Parentheses in Al₂(SO₄)₃ produce two aluminium ions and three sulfate ions per formula unit in the ideal dissociation model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solubility rules and precipitation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use the SCSA data booklet’s solubility rules to predict which ion combination forms a precipitate. All nitrates are listed soluble. Most chlorides are soluble, with exceptions including AgCl and PbCl₂. Most sulfates are soluble, with BaSO₄, SrSO₄ and PbSO₄ listed insoluble and others slightly soluble. These are practical classifications, not a statement of literally zero solubility. Mixing AgNO₃ and NaCl can give white AgCl. Identify the precipitate formula by balanced charges, retain its solid state and separate spectator ions from reacting species. Refer to the current booklet rather than memorising an incomplete rule.", "diagram": "precipitation"}, {"title": "Net ionic equations and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Write correct molecular products and states, separate aqueous strong electrolytes into ions, then cancel species unchanged on both sides. Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) is the net ionic equation. Nitrate and sodium are spectators in this example. Do not split a solid precipitate, liquid water or a weak molecular acid as though fully ionised. Balance atoms and charge in the final equation. Observation describes what is seen, such as “a white precipitate forms,” while the equation explains reacting species. A colourless solution alone does not imply no ions. Different ion combinations can produce precipitates with characteristic colours.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Arrhenius acids, bases and strength", "kind": "learn", "text": "In aqueous solution an Arrhenius acid increases hydrogen-ion concentration and an Arrhenius base increases hydroxide-ion concentration. H⁺(aq) is convenient notation for hydrated proton behaviour; H₃O⁺ is a more explicit representation. HCl is a strong acid that ionises essentially completely in dilute water. Ethanoic acid ionises only partly and is weak. NaOH dissociates to give OH⁻. NH₃ reacts with water reversibly to form NH₄⁺ and OH⁻. Strength describes degree of ionisation, while concentration describes amount per volume. A concentrated weak acid and a dilute strong acid are both possible. Neither word directly specifies the pH by itself.", "diagram": "acid-strength"}, {"title": "Reference: common molecular names and formulae", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>These common formulas support naming, equations and acid/base interpretation. Formula and proton count alone do not establish complete ionisation. Molecular names here cover the syllabus appendix set.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ammonia</td><td>NH₃</td></tr><tr><td>water</td><td>H₂O</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogen peroxide</td><td>H₂O₂</td></tr><tr><td>ethanoic acid</td><td>CH₃COOH</td></tr><tr><td>hydrochloric acid</td><td>HCl</td></tr><tr><td>nitric acid</td><td>HNO₃</td></tr><tr><td>carbonic acid</td><td>H₂CO₃</td></tr><tr><td>sulfuric acid</td><td>H₂SO₄</td></tr><tr><td>sulfurous acid</td><td>H₂SO₃</td></tr><tr><td>phosphoric acid</td><td>H₃PO₄</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "pH, indicators and the logarithmic scale", "kind": "learn", "text": "pH = −log₁₀[H⁺] for the course’s concentration-based calculations. At 25 °C, neutral water has [H⁺] = [OH⁻] and pH approximately 7. Each unit decrease in pH corresponds to a tenfold rise in [H⁺]. A pH 3 solution has 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration of a pH 5 solution. Indicators change colour over a transition range and give approximate classification rather than exact pH. A calibrated pH meter provides a numerical reading. Neutrality depends on equal hydrogen and hydroxide concentrations, and neutral pH can differ from 7 at another temperature. State the temperature assumptions in more advanced comparisons.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Characteristic acid and base reactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acid with a suitable reactive metal can form a salt and hydrogen, for example Mg + 2H⁺ → Mg²⁺ + H₂. Acid with carbonate forms CO₂ and water: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O. Acid with hydroxide gives H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Hydroxide with ammonium gives NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Predictions should include states, charge balance and observations such as effervescence, a metal dissolving or a temperature change. Not all metals react readily with dilute acid, and acid type matters. Avoid giving “gas produced” without identifying which gas and the reaction responsible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solution stoichiometry and water treatment", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a reaction involving solutions, convert known concentrations and volumes to moles using n = cV, apply the balanced ratio and calculate the requested mass, concentration or volume. If two reactant amounts are supplied, check which limits. Water treatment combines methods for different contaminants: filtration removes suspended solids, desalination reduces dissolved salts and disinfection controls microbes. Each method has costs and constraints. Heavy-metal monitoring may use AAS and calibration, while precipitation can remove some ions. A proposed treatment needs evidence of removal effectiveness, safe final concentrations and handling of waste, rather than a claim that one process removes every impurity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Slightly soluble is not zero soluble", "kind": "learn", "text": "The SCSA booklet distinguishes soluble, slightly soluble and insoluble practical ranges. Calcium sulfate is listed slightly soluble rather than being grouped with all highly soluble sulfates. A precipitate prediction must therefore refer to the supplied rules and concentrations when those matter. A saturated solution can contain dissolved ions even when excess solid remains at the bottom. The solid and aqueous ions are different physical parts of the system. It is incorrect to infer that an insoluble-labelled substance has no particles dissolved under any circumstances.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Weak acid neutralisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a weak acid such as ethanoic acid, do not split all reactant molecules into free H⁺ and ethanoate when writing a net ionic neutralisation. CH₃COOH(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → CH₃COO⁻(aq) + H₂O(l) represents the weak molecular acid reacting with hydroxide. Its incomplete initial ionisation does not reduce the total stoichiometric acid amount that can react as equilibrium readjusts. A strong acid’s simplified net equation is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Choose the equation that accurately represents the acid species under the given conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Solution preparation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Solution preparation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Precipitate amount", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Precipitate amount", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Dilution calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Dilution calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Comparing pH", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Comparing pH", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Ions after mixing", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Carbonate reaction", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Water-quality concentration", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Solution preparation", "prompt": "Find the mass of NaCl needed to make 250.0 mL of 0.200 mol L⁻¹ solution, using M = 58.44 g mol⁻¹. Describe the final volume step.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n = cV = 0.200 × 0.2500 = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "m = nM = 0.0500 × 58.44. [1]", "m = 2.92 g. [1]", "Dissolve and transfer quantitatively, then add water to the flask’s final 250.0 mL mark and mix. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Precipitate amount", "prompt": "Mix 25.0 mL 0.200 mol L⁻¹ AgNO₃ with excess NaCl. Calculate AgCl mass, M = 143.32 g mol⁻¹, and give the net ionic equation.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(Ag⁺) = 0.0250 × 0.200 = 0.00500 mol. [1]", "Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s). [2: species/states and balance]", "n(AgCl) = 0.00500 mol. [1]", "m = 0.00500 × 143.32 = 0.717 g. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q2", "allocations": [1, 2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Dilution calculation", "prompt": "What volume of 2.00 mol L⁻¹ stock is needed to make 100.0 mL of 0.150 mol L⁻¹ solution?", "marks": 3, "steps": ["c₁V₁ = c₂V₂ conserves solute moles. [1]", "V₁ = (0.150 × 100.0)/2.00. [1]", "V₁ = 7.50 mL; dilute this aliquot to 100.0 mL total solution. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Comparing pH", "prompt": "Calculate the pH of a strong monoprotic acid with [H⁺] = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹. Compare [H⁺] with a pH 5.00 solution.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["pH = −log₁₀(2.00 × 10⁻³). [1]", "pH = 2.699. [1]", "At pH 5.00, [H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹. [1]", "The first solution has 200 times the hydrogen-ion concentration. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ions after mixing", "prompt": "Mix 20.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ with 30.0 mL water. Assume additive volumes and no reaction. Find calcium and chloride concentrations.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Initial n(Ca²⁺) = 0.0200 × 0.100 = 0.00200 mol. [1]", "n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 0.00200 = 0.00400 mol. [1]", "[Ca²⁺] = 0.00200/0.0500 = 0.0400 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "[Cl⁻] = 0.00400/0.0500 = 0.0800 mol L⁻¹. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Carbonate reaction", "prompt": "Write a net ionic equation for acid reacting with carbonate and describe one observation and its cause.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l). [2: correct species and atom/charge balance]", "Effervescence or bubbling occurs. [1]", "Carbon dioxide gas forms and escapes. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q6", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Water-quality concentration", "prompt": "A water sample contains 3.00 mg lead in 2.00 kg solution. Calculate mass ppm, describe how AAS can support monitoring and explain why filtration alone does not remove dissolved ions.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["ppm = 3.00/2.00 = 1.50. [1]", "Use prepared samples and element-specific AAS with calibration standards. [1]", "Include a blank and relevant concentration range. [1]", "Apply dilution factors and report uncertainty. [1]", "Dissolved ions pass through ordinary filter pores. [1]", "A separate validated removal method is needed for those ions; final monitoring must verify its effectiveness. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "The concentration of Cl⁻ in 0.10 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ is", "o": ["0.05", "0.10", "0.20", "2.00"], "a": 2, "why": "Each formula unit supplies two chloride ions."}, {"q": "A weak acid is one that", "o": ["Is always dilute", "Ionises partly in water", "Contains no hydrogen", "Cannot react with a base"], "a": 1, "why": "Strength and concentration are separate properties."}, {"q": "For dilute water density 1 kg L⁻¹, 5 ppm is approximately", "o": ["5 mg L⁻¹", "5 g L⁻¹", "5 mol L⁻¹", "5 kg L⁻¹"], "a": 0, "why": "Mass ppm is mg per kg of solution."}, {"q": "Ordinary ice floats because", "o": ["Its molecules contain fewer atoms", "Its open structure makes it less dense", "It contains no hydrogen bonds", "It is a different element"], "a": 1, "why": "Hydrogen bonding creates an open solid arrangement."}, {"q": "A pH 3 solution has compared with pH 5", "o": ["Twice [H⁺]", "100 times [H⁺]", "One hundredth [H⁺]", "The same [H⁺]"], "a": 1, "why": "Two pH units represent a factor of 10²."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "title": "Reaction rates, catalysts and investigation skills", "scope": "Collision theory; activation energy; rate measurements; experimental design; errors, graphs, evidence and scientific communication", "screens": [{"title": "Rate as change per time", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reaction rate measures how quickly a reactant is consumed or a product forms. An average rate uses change divided by elapsed time. Gas volume rate might have units mL s⁻¹, mass-loss rate g s⁻¹ and concentration rate mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. Product rate is positive when its quantity rises. For a decreasing reactant amount, quote the positive disappearance rate or clearly state the signed derivative convention. Initial rate refers to the beginning, while an average over a long interval may hide changing rate. The measured quantity must reliably represent reaction progress. Do not compare raw volumes collected over unequal times without calculation.", "diagram": "rate-graph"}, {"title": "Graph gradients and completion", "kind": "learn", "text": "A steeper product–time graph means greater product-formation rate. A tangent gives instantaneous rate; a secant between two points gives interval average rate. A plateau can indicate completion or another limit on net observable change, depending on context. Two trials can reach the same final product amount at different speeds if they begin with the same limiting-reactant amount. A catalyst can shorten the time without increasing that final amount. Label axes with variables and units, choose a sensible scale and use a smooth fit where the process warrants it. A curve is not a series of straight segments simply because measurements are discrete.", "diagram": "rate-curves"}, {"title": "Collision theory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reactant particles must collide with sufficient energy and a suitable orientation to react. Activation energy is the minimum energy barrier for the pathway. Not every collision produces products. A rate explanation should state how a change affects collision frequency or the fraction of collisions meeting the energy requirement. Increasing collision count without suitable energy does not ensure a proportionate rate rise. Collision theory is a simplified model for explaining trends, not a microscopic movie of every chemical step. Reactions with different pathways can respond differently, so use the actual reactants, phases and conditions in the question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concentration and gas pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Increasing concentration places more reacting particles in a given volume, generally increasing collision frequency and rate. For gases at fixed temperature, compressing a given amount increases concentration and collision frequency. An inert gas added at fixed volume does not increase reactant concentration and need not increase reaction rate. Pressure is therefore relevant through reacting gas partial pressures and particle density, not simply a gauge reading. A complete explanation names the reacting particles and the volume or concentration change. Do not state that concentration increases particle kinetic energy; average kinetic energy is determined by temperature in this model.", "diagram": "collisions"}, {"title": "Temperature and energy distribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Higher temperature raises average particle kinetic energy and usually increases collision frequency. More importantly, it increases the fraction of particles with energy at or above Eₐ, often causing a substantial rate increase. A qualitative energy-distribution diagram has a broader lower peak at higher temperature with a larger high-energy tail beyond the fixed Eₐ. The area under each distribution represents the same total particle population in a controlled comparison. Eₐ does not itself fall simply because the reactants are warmer. The particles’ energy distribution changes. Explain both the motion and successful-collision consequences.", "diagram": "distribution"}, {"title": "Surface area of a solid", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dividing a solid exposes more surface to other reactants at the same total mass. More sites are available for collisions, so reaction rate can increase. A powdered carbonate can react faster than one large piece of the same material. The total product amount is unchanged if the same limiting amount reacts completely. Control mass and composition when comparing particle sizes. A larger surface area may also affect heat transfer and gas release, so design the investigation carefully. “Smaller particles move faster” does not explain the central surface effect for a reacting solid. It is the accessible surface that changes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Activation energy and the transition state", "kind": "learn", "text": "An energy profile shows reactants, a high-energy transition-state region and products along reaction progress. Eₐ measures the barrier above reactants. Reverse activation energy is the barrier above products. Their difference matches ΔH for a simple common pathway. A large Eₐ can make an exothermic reaction slow at room temperature. “Energy released” and “energy required to initiate successful reaction” are different quantities. A profile is schematic and does not show reaction speed on its horizontal axis. Read vertical energy differences rather than line length or horizontal distance.", "diagram": "energy-profile"}, {"title": "Catalysts and unchanged reaction energetics", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst participates in steps that provide an alternative pathway with lower activation barriers and is regenerated overall. At a given temperature, a greater fraction of collisions can proceed successfully. It does not supply extra reactant, change the balanced net equation or lower ΔH. In a reversible equilibrium, it speeds both directions and does not change the equilibrium composition. In a completion experiment with fixed limiting reagent, it changes rate but not the theoretical total product. A catalysed energy curve must begin and end at the same energies as the uncatalysed one.", "diagram": "catalysed-profile"}, {"title": "Enzymes, metal surfaces and nanoparticles", "kind": "learn", "text": "Enzymes are protein catalysts with structures that allow specific interactions with substrates and lower the reaction barrier. High temperature or unsuitable pH can disrupt their functioning. Metal surfaces can provide sites where reactants adsorb and react through a different pathway. Nanoparticles can expose a high proportion of surface atoms, increasing active sites per material amount. A catalyst is not necessarily permanently unchanged at every step; regeneration is the overall feature. Poisoning or degradation can reduce practical performance. Compare activity, operating conditions, selectivity and reuse when evaluating industrial catalysts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Catalytic converters and human endeavour", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalytic converter promotes reactions that reduce harmful exhaust components, including oxidation of CO to CO₂ and conversion of nitrogen oxides toward nitrogen. The aim is lower pollutant emissions, not elimination of all greenhouse-gas production. For example, 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ converts toxic CO but still forms CO₂. Performance depends on operating temperature and exhaust composition. An evaluation should distinguish pollutants, use balanced chemical examples and consider resource cost and catalyst durability. A claim of “zero emissions” is not supported merely because a vehicle uses a catalyst.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A testable question and hypothesis", "kind": "learn", "text": "A useful investigation question identifies an independent variable and a measurable dependent variable, for example acid concentration and initial CO₂-production rate with a fixed carbonate mass and particle size. A hypothesis predicts the direction of the relationship and gives a chemical reason. “More acid reacts faster because collision frequency rises” can be tested across several concentrations. State controls such as temperature, total solution volume, solid mass, surface area and apparatus. A dependent variable must be defined operationally, such as slope over the first 20 s, rather than the vague phrase “how much reaction.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Valid, reliable and safe measurements", "kind": "learn", "text": "Validity concerns whether the method answers the intended question without confounding variables. Reliability concerns consistency and the evidence from repeats. Accuracy concerns closeness to a reference or true value, while precision concerns the spread or resolution of measurement. Repeating a biased method may produce precise but inaccurate results. Plan enough levels and repeats to reveal a pattern, use appropriate equipment and assess hazards before any practical work. These screens teach analysis and planning; practical work involving chemicals or heat requires the school’s approved supervision and procedures. Link a risk control to a specific hazard rather than giving an unrelated generic safety sentence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Random error, systematic error and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random effects produce trial-to-trial scatter, such as small timing variation. Systematic effects bias results in a consistent direction, such as a miscalibrated balance or persistent gas leak. Repeats can reduce uncertainty in a mean arising from random variation but do not remove a consistent bias. Resolution sets a measurement limit, and uncertainty propagates through calculations. For a burette titre obtained from two readings, both readings contribute. Explain error direction through the calculation: gas leakage gives low measured volume and therefore low calculated gas amount. “Human error” does not identify a scientific mechanism or justify a direction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Data processing and anomalous values", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tabulate raw data with headings and units, preserve meaningful precision and calculate suitable averages or rates. Plot the independent variable horizontally and the dependent variable vertically. A possible anomaly deserves investigation, not automatic deletion because it does not fit a preferred trend. Check recording, apparatus and conditions and report any justified exclusion transparently. Use numeric evidence in a conclusion, such as “initial rate rose from 0.50 to 0.90 mL s⁻¹.” State whether the trend supports the hypothesis within the tested range. Avoid claiming causation from uncontrolled observations or extrapolating far beyond the measurements.", "diagram": "data-error"}, {"title": "Evidence, source quality and scientific reports", "kind": "learn", "text": "Evaluate a claim through method quality, sample size, controls, calibration, reproducibility and relevance of the evidence. Distinguish a primary measurement from an advertisement and a model prediction from an observation. A report presents the question, method, results, analysis, conclusion and specific limitations in a logical order. Chemical formulae, equations and diagrams should support the argument. An improvement should address a named limitation and explain its expected effect. In extended response, consider benefits and constraints and conclude in proportion to the evidence. Uncertainty is part of scientific reasoning, not a reason to ignore well-supported results.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Inverse time as a rate proxy", "kind": "learn", "text": "If each trial reaches the same fixed visible endpoint after the same chemical amount has changed, 1/time can serve as a comparative rate proxy with units s⁻¹. It is not automatically an amount-per-time rate and relies on a consistent endpoint and chemistry. Different observers or obscuring colours may change the endpoint criterion, affecting validity. A gas-volume slope provides more direct formation-rate evidence when collection is reliable. State what the proxy measures and why shorter time implies faster progress under the matched conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A conclusion with numeric evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose controlled trials give mean initial rates 0.40, 0.60 and 0.82 mL s⁻¹ as concentration rises across three specified levels. Describe an increasing trend and refer to the measured values. Collision theory predicts more reacting particles per volume and more frequent encounters. Do not claim exact proportionality merely from these three numbers unless uncertainty and the model support it. Report spread and any unusual trial. A scientific conclusion should answer the testable question within its investigated range and distinguish the evidence from extrapolated predictions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Average and changing rate", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Average and changing rate", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Temperature explanation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Temperature explanation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Reading an energy profile", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Reading an energy profile", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Controlled rate investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Controlled rate investigation", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Catalyst misconceptions", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Error and improvement", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Separating rate from final amount", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Average and changing rate", "prompt": "CO₂ volume rises from 12.0 mL at 10.0 s to 30.0 mL at 40.0 s. Find average rate over the interval and explain why it may differ from initial rate.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["ΔV = 18.0 mL and Δt = 30.0 s. [1]", "Average rate = 18.0/30.0 = 0.600 mL s⁻¹. [1]", "Reactant concentrations or available surface can decrease as reaction proceeds. [1]", "This interval average therefore need not equal the slope at the start. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Temperature explanation", "prompt": "Explain why heating usually increases reaction rate without changing activation energy for the same uncatalysed pathway.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Average particle kinetic energy increases. [1]", "Collision frequency can increase. [1]", "A greater fraction of collisions has energy at or above Eₐ and appropriate orientation. [1]", "The pathway barrier is unchanged; the distribution of reactant energies changes. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Reading an energy profile", "prompt": "Reactants lie at 40 kJ, transition state at 120 kJ and products at 10 kJ on a schematic energy scale. Find forward Eₐ, reverse Eₐ and ΔH.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Forward Eₐ = 120 − 40 = 80 kJ. [1]", "Reverse Eₐ = 120 − 10 = 110 kJ. [1]", "ΔH = 10 − 40 = −30 kJ. [1]", "Products are lower, so the written reaction is exothermic. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Controlled rate investigation", "prompt": "Design a comparison of carbonate particle size and acid reaction rate. Identify variables, measurement and one improvement to reliability.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Independent variable: particle size at several defined levels. [1]", "Dependent variable: initial gas-volume rate using consistent time intervals. [1]", "Keep carbonate mass/composition and acid concentration/volume constant. [1]", "Keep temperature and collection apparatus consistent. [1]", "Repeat each level and compare means and spread. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Catalyst misconceptions", "prompt": "A student says a catalyst increases the final gas volume and lowers reaction enthalpy. Evaluate both statements for fixed limiting reactant undergoing complete reaction.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The catalyst increases rate through an alternative lower-barrier pathway. [1]", "It does not add reactant, so the same theoretical final gas amount forms. [1]", "Reactant and product energy levels remain unchanged. [1]", "Therefore ΔH is unchanged. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Error and improvement", "prompt": "A gas-collection tube leaks consistently during each trial. Explain the effect on calculated initial rate and whether repeated trials alone solve it.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Measured gas volume is systematically too low. [1]", "The estimated formation rate is therefore too low. [1]", "Repeating the same leaking method does not remove the systematic bias. [1]", "Check and seal connections or replace the apparatus and verify it is gas-tight. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Separating rate from final amount", "prompt": "Equal carbonate masses with identical acid excess give gas curves reaching the same plateau at different times. Propose a controlled explanation, identify two variables to check and explain what the plateau cannot prove.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["A difference in particle surface area, temperature or catalyst could affect rate. [1]", "A suitable mechanism changes collision frequency or successful-collision fraction. [1]", "Check temperature and particle size while keeping other reactant conditions matched. [1]", "Equal limiting amounts can give equal final product amounts. [1]", "The faster curve does not imply greater theoretical yield. [1]", "A plateau alone cannot exclude gas leakage, dissolution or incomplete measurement; validate collection. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "A catalyst changes", "o": ["ΔH", "The pathway and activation barriers", "The stoichiometric coefficients", "The initial reactant mass automatically"], "a": 1, "why": "The alternative pathway speeds reaction without changing net energetics."}, {"q": "Higher concentration generally increases rate through", "o": ["Higher average kinetic energy", "More frequent reactant collisions", "Changing proton numbers", "Lower particle masses"], "a": 1, "why": "Concentration changes particle density, while temperature governs kinetic energy."}, {"q": "A tangent to a product–time graph gives", "o": ["Instantaneous formation rate", "Final product mass only", "Molar mass", "Activation energy directly"], "a": 0, "why": "Its gradient is the local change per time."}, {"q": "Repeats alone are least effective at removing", "o": ["A persistent calibration bias", "Random timing scatter", "Variation in averages", "Evidence about repeatability"], "a": 0, "why": "A systematic bias requires correction of its cause."}, {"q": "Equal mass of powder reacts faster than a lump mainly because", "o": ["More surface is exposed", "Its atoms are lighter", "The final yield must increase", "Its Eₐ becomes zero"], "a": 0, "why": "More accessible surface allows more reactant collisions."}], "icon": "⚗️", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "title": "Dynamic equilibrium and responding to change", "scope": "Rates and reversible systems; Le Châtelier; concentration/rate graphs; homogeneous and heterogeneous Kc; ocean carbonate systems", "screens": [{"title": "Open, closed and isolated systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "An open system exchanges matter and energy with its surroundings. A closed system exchanges energy but not matter. An isolated idealisation exchanges neither. A sealed vessel can therefore be closed even if heat enters through its walls. Reversible reactions can establish dynamic equilibrium in a closed system when conditions are stable. An open system is not necessarily incapable of every steady state, but a steady flow condition differs from the closed-system equilibrium studied here. Define the system boundary and whether gases can escape. If a product is continuously removed, its concentration behaviour and reaction extent differ from those in a sealed mixture.", "diagram": "closed-system"}, {"title": "Reversible changes at the particle scale", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reversible process can proceed in both directions under the relevant conditions. In a sealed liquid–vapour system, evaporation and condensation occur simultaneously. In N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), bonds break and form in forward and reverse reactions. Early behaviour depends on starting composition. Starting with mainly reactants usually gives a large forward rate and a small reverse rate; as products accumulate, the reverse rate grows. Equilibrium is reached when rates become equal, not when particles stop reacting. Molecules continue changing identities while average macroscopic properties stay constant. Use a reversible arrow to distinguish the two possible directions.", "diagram": "dynamic-equilibrium"}, {"title": "Rates, concentrations and equilibrium position", "kind": "learn", "text": "At equilibrium, forward and reverse rates are equal, so net concentration changes cease. Reactant and product concentrations are constant but need not be equal. A product-favoured equilibrium can contain substantially more products, yet the two opposing rates still match. Equilibrium position describes relative composition under the conditions, while rate describes how fast the system reaches that composition. An equal-rate graph does not imply the reaction has “finished.” Explain which quantity is plotted: a concentration graph approaches plateaus, whereas a rate graph approaches a common rate. Their vertical values mean different things.", "diagram": "equilibrium-graphs"}, {"title": "Forward and reverse activation energies", "kind": "learn", "text": "For one schematic pathway, forward Eₐ is transition-state energy minus reactant energy, and reverse Eₐ is transition-state energy minus product energy. ΔH = forward Eₐ − reverse Eₐ. For an exothermic forward reaction, the reverse barrier measured from lower-energy products is larger. Heating can increase both rates but does not necessarily increase them by the same factor. In an exothermic forward system, the endothermic reverse direction is favoured by a temperature rise, changing the final composition. Avoid the incorrect explanation that one direction entirely stops after conditions change. Both continue throughout re-equilibration.", "diagram": "reversible-energy"}, {"title": "Le Châtelier’s principle", "kind": "learn", "text": "If an equilibrium system is disturbed, its net response tends to counteract the imposed change as it approaches a new equilibrium. Identify the perturbation, predict the net direction, explain its effect and state the new composition. The response only partially counteracts the disturbance; it does not necessarily restore the original concentrations or pressure. A useful explanation also considers collision frequency and the initial imbalance of rates. Le Châtelier is a qualitative prediction tool and should not replace clear reasoning about which species or variable was changed. A catalyst accelerates re-equilibration without causing a position shift.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Adding or removing a dissolved species", "kind": "learn", "text": "For Fe³⁺(aq) + SCN⁻(aq) ⇌ FeSCN²⁺(aq), adding thiocyanate increases its concentration immediately. Forward collision frequency increases before composition readjusts, so forward rate initially exceeds reverse rate. More complex forms until the rates match again. Removing a product favours net product formation. Removing a reactant favours the reverse direction. On a concentration–time graph, the directly added species has an instantaneous upward jump, while species not directly changed respond smoothly afterward. Distinguish the imposed jump from the subsequent reaction response. Adding a solution also changes volume unless the question explicitly neglects dilution.", "diagram": "concentration-shift"}, {"title": "Volume changes for gaseous equilibria", "kind": "learn", "text": "Decreasing volume raises the concentrations and partial pressures of all gaseous species immediately. If the two sides have different gaseous stoichiometric amounts, net reaction commonly favours the side with fewer gas moles. For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, compression favours ammonia. If both sides have equal gaseous mole totals, volume change need not shift the equilibrium position, although individual rates can change. Count gaseous coefficients only; solids and liquids are not part of the gas-mole comparison. On a graph, all gas concentrations jump on compression before the slower chemical response. Kc remains unchanged at fixed temperature.", "diagram": "pressure-shift"}, {"title": "Inert gas and pressure distinctions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adding an inert gas at fixed volume and temperature increases total pressure but leaves reacting species’ concentrations and partial pressures unchanged in the ideal model. Thus equilibrium position is unchanged. If inert gas is added at constant total pressure, the container expands, reducing reactant partial pressures and potentially shifting toward more gas moles. The words “pressure increases” alone do not tell you the cause. Identify whether volume, temperature or gas composition changed. This distinction is common in demanding exam explanations because a simple “high pressure favours fewer moles” slogan can produce an incorrect prediction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Temperature and the sign of ΔH", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an exothermic forward reaction, increasing temperature favours the endothermic reverse direction, lowering product fraction and usually lowering its forward Kc. Decreasing temperature favours the exothermic direction. For an endothermic forward reaction, these predictions reverse. You may represent heat as a conceptual product or reactant to aid reasoning, but heat is not a concentration term in Kc. Heating generally increases kinetic rates while possibly decreasing desired equilibrium yield. State both consequences when discussing industrial conditions. Temperature is the course variable that changes the equilibrium constant for a specified reaction.", "diagram": "temperature-shift"}, {"title": "Catalyst at equilibrium", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst supplies a different pathway with lower activation barriers for forward and reverse processes. Both rates increase while their equality at equilibrium is preserved. Concentrations, equilibrium position, Kc and ΔH are unchanged if temperature and other conditions remain fixed. A catalyst can make industrial production practical because equilibrium is approached faster, but it does not improve the theoretical equilibrium yield solely by being added. A rate graph can show both rates rising together on catalyst addition, whereas a concentration graph stays level. A catalyst in a real plant may allow different operating conditions, which can indirectly alter yield; distinguish this from its direct effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Writing a homogeneous Kc expression", "kind": "learn", "text": "For aA(aq) + bB(aq) ⇌ cC(aq) + dD(aq), Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ). Use equilibrium concentrations and exponents from balanced coefficients. Kc is reaction-specific and temperature-specific. It is not a rate constant. For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, Kc = [NH₃]²/([N₂][H₂]³). Reversing the equation reciprocates Kc, and multiplying coefficients raises Kc to that factor. The course commonly uses concentration-based expressions; more advanced thermodynamic constants use activities. Follow the notation and units conventions requested by the assessment instead of assuming a coefficient is an additive multiplier.", "diagram": "kc"}, {"title": "Heterogeneous equilibria", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a heterogeneous equilibrium, pure solids and pure liquids have effectively constant activities and are omitted from the course Kc expression. For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), the expression is Kc = [CO₂]. Adding more of a pure solid does not change the equilibrium gas concentration while both required solid phases remain present. If a phase is completely used up, the earlier equilibrium description may no longer apply. Do not omit every liquid-labelled or dissolved species indiscriminately: an aqueous solute’s concentration belongs in the expression. Solvent water is normally omitted when its activity is treated as constant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "What a large or small Kc means", "kind": "learn", "text": "A large Kc indicates the written equilibrium favours products in the sense of its concentration expression; a small Kc indicates reactant favouring. It does not tell you the speed, activation energy or complete conversion. Values should be interpreted with stoichiometry and concentration units in mind. A reaction with large Kc can be slow. A very small amount of product can still exist at equilibrium when Kc is small. Year 12 requires writing expressions and qualitatively interpreting constants. Simple numerical substitution is useful practice; lengthy equilibrium-solving algebra beyond supplied information is labelled extension where used.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concentration graphs after a disturbance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read a graph in stages: initial equilibrium, instantaneous imposed change, transient net response and final equilibrium. Direct addition creates a jump only for the species directly changed if volume is unchanged. Compression creates jumps in all gas concentrations. Temperature changes usually cause smooth composition adjustment unless volume also changes. A catalyst causes no concentration shift. Final concentrations generally differ from the original ones, but forward and reverse rates are equal again. Use stoichiometric ratios to compare changes due solely to reaction. Do not measure a total graph jump as if it were all chemical consumption.", "diagram": "equilibrium-graphs"}, {"title": "Ocean carbonate chemistry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rising atmospheric CO₂ can increase dissolved CO₂ and carbonic-acid-related chemistry: CO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H₂CO₃(aq), followed by acid ionisation. Additional H⁺ reacts with carbonate, CO₃²⁻ + H⁺ ⇌ HCO₃⁻, lowering available carbonate ions. This can reduce conditions favouring calcium carbonate formation in marine organisms. Ocean acidification means a decrease in pH; it does not require seawater to become pH below 7. Real seawater contains many coupled equilibria and biological influences, so a simple equation is a partial model. Relate the directional chemistry to evidence rather than claiming every organism responds identically.", "diagram": "carbonate"}, {"title": "Equilibrium investigations and observation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A visible colour change can indicate a composition shift, but intensity also depends on dilution and optical path length. For a coloured complex equilibrium, use matched volumes and containers and control temperature. Explain a shift using an equation and relevant species, not colour alone. Repeated measurements and calibration strengthen quantitative claims. An experiment should distinguish a reversible response from irreversible precipitation or decomposition. A sound conclusion identifies conditions and limitations. In an exam, cite the actual observed colour or concentration data, then connect them to forward/reverse rate imbalance and eventual equal rates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Collision explanations after compression", "kind": "learn", "text": "For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, compression immediately raises all reacting gas concentrations. Collision frequencies for both directions increase, but the reactant-side rate is enhanced more strongly for this equilibrium’s kinetic response, producing net ammonia formation. As reactants fall and ammonia rises relative to that immediate compressed state, opposing rates readjust and become equal. Do not claim only one direction speeds up. A quantitative rate law cannot always be read directly from an overall equation, so this is qualitative collision reasoning, not an invented elementary rate expression.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Heating: both rates change unequally", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heating increases the fraction of particles able to overcome each directional activation barrier. Both forward and reverse rates increase initially. For an exothermic forward reaction, the reverse endothermic barrier is larger in the simple energy-profile model, so its successful-collision fraction is more sensitive to temperature and the reverse rate increases more strongly. Composition shifts toward reactants until rates match. This explains the direction through energy distribution and rate imbalance, while Le Châtelier predicts the same response. State that constant concentrations return at a new equilibrium, not that reaction stops.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive equilibrium quotient", "kind": "interactive", "model": "equilibrium"}, {"title": "Worked question: Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A Kc expression and value", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: A Kc expression and value", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Heating a coloured equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Heating a coloured equilibrium", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: An inert gas comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: An inert gas comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Solid addition", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Ocean reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Graph interpretation investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "prompt": "For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), temperature is fixed while volume halves. Predict the immediate concentration changes, net direction and Kc change.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["All reacting gas concentrations immediately double. [1]", "There are four gas moles on the left and two on the right per equation. [1]", "Net response favours ammonia, the fewer-gas side. [1]", "The system approaches a new equilibrium with equal forward/reverse rates. [1]", "Kc remains unchanged because temperature is fixed. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A Kc expression and value", "prompt": "For H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), equilibrium concentrations are 0.200, 0.100 and 0.800 mol L⁻¹ respectively. Write and evaluate Kc.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Kc = [HI]²/([H₂][I₂]). [1]", "Kc = 0.800²/(0.200 × 0.100). [1]", "Kc = 32.0 for the specified temperature and concentration convention. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Heating a coloured equilibrium", "prompt": "For N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), ΔH > 0. NO₂ is brown and N₂O₄ is colourless. Predict the colour change on heating and the direction of Kc change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The forward direction is endothermic. [1]", "Heating favours net NO₂ formation. [1]", "The mixture becomes more intensely brown if other observational conditions are controlled. [1]", "The forward Kc increases with temperature. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "An inert gas comparison", "prompt": "An inert gas is added to an equilibrium mixture at constant temperature and fixed volume. Explain why increased total pressure does not necessarily shift equilibrium.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Reactant and product amounts and volume remain unchanged. [1]", "Their concentrations and partial pressures therefore remain unchanged. [1]", "Their equilibrium ratio and position remain unchanged despite higher total pressure. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Solid addition", "prompt": "For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), both solids are present. Write Kc and predict the effect of adding more CaCO₃ at fixed temperature and volume.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Kc = [CO₂]. [1]", "Pure solid activities are treated as constant and omitted. [1]", "Adding more solid does not change equilibrium CO₂ concentration under these assumptions. [1]", "Kc is fixed by temperature; the conclusion assumes both solid phases remain available. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ocean reasoning", "prompt": "Explain how additional dissolved CO₂ can reduce carbonate availability without making seawater acidic in the pH-below-7 sense.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Additional CO₂ increases carbonic-acid-related acid equilibria. [1]", "Hydrogen-ion concentration increases and pH falls. [1]", "H⁺ reacts with CO₃²⁻ to form HCO₃⁻, lowering carbonate availability. [1]", "A pH decrease is acidification even if the final pH remains above 7. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Graph interpretation investigation", "prompt": "A gaseous equilibrium with more reactant-side gas moles is compressed at fixed temperature. Describe the concentration graph stages and explain why a measured colour change needs careful interpretation.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["All gas concentrations jump up immediately. [1]", "Net reaction then favours the side with fewer gas moles. [1]", "Chemical concentration changes follow stoichiometric ratios after the imposed jump. [1]", "A new constant-composition equilibrium is reached with equal opposing rates. [1]", "Kc remains constant at fixed temperature. [1]", "Colour intensity is affected by concentration and optical path; distinguish compression from composition shift using matched measurement conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "At dynamic equilibrium", "o": ["All reactions stop", "Forward and reverse rates are equal", "Reactant and product concentrations must be equal", "All products disappear"], "a": 1, "why": "Constant macroscopic composition follows equal opposing rates."}, {"q": "Which directly changes Kc for the same equation?", "o": ["A catalyst", "Adding reactant", "Changing temperature", "Adding more pure solid"], "a": 2, "why": "Kc depends on temperature for a specified reaction."}, {"q": "A catalyst at equilibrium", "o": ["Shifts toward products", "Changes ΔH", "Speeds both directions without changing composition", "Stops the reverse reaction"], "a": 2, "why": "The equilibrium position is unchanged."}, {"q": "For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), Kc contains", "o": ["Both solids", "Only [CO₂]", "No terms", "[CaO]/[CaCO₃] only"], "a": 1, "why": "Pure solid activities are omitted."}, {"q": "Large Kc tells us the reaction is", "o": ["Always fast", "Product favoured, not necessarily fast", "Irreversible in every case", "Endothermic necessarily"], "a": 1, "why": "Equilibrium composition and kinetic rate are different."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "31 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "title": "Acid–base models, salt hydrolysis and buffers", "scope": "Brønsted–Lowry; conjugates; polyprotic acids; strength and Ka; Kw and pH; salt hydrolysis; buffer mechanisms and capacity", "screens": [{"title": "Why acid–base models changed", "kind": "learn", "text": "Davy related acid behaviour to replaceable hydrogen. Arrhenius described acids and bases through H⁺ and OH⁻ production in water, explaining many aqueous observations. Brønsted–Lowry broadened the model to proton transfer: an acid donates a proton and a base accepts one. NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s) illustrates acid–base chemistry without water as solvent. The wider model accounts for ammonia’s basicity without requiring an OH group in its formula. Models are judged by explanatory scope and evidence. Explain what a new model adds rather than saying earlier models were entirely useless.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Proton donation and acceptance", "kind": "learn", "text": "In HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻, HCl donates H⁺ and water accepts it. In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, ammonia accepts H⁺ while water donates it. The proton transfers without its original bonding electron pair, so charges change appropriately. The species losing H⁺ has charge reduced by one unit; the species gaining it has charge increased by one. An acid need not be electrically neutral and a base need not be negative. Identify roles in the actual reaction rather than assigning one permanent role to every species.", "diagram": "proton-transfer"}, {"title": "Conjugate acid–base pairs", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conjugate pair differs by one proton. After donation, an acid becomes its conjugate base; after acceptance, a base becomes its conjugate acid. In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, the pairs are NH₄⁺/NH₃ and H₂O/OH⁻. They are not NH₃/OH⁻, which are both bases in their respective directions. Show each pair’s formula and one-proton difference. Charge must differ by one. A strong acid’s conjugate base has very little tendency to accept protons from water under ordinary conditions. This helps explain why chloride salts of non-hydrolysing cations are often approximately neutral.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiprotic species", "kind": "learn", "text": "An amphiprotic species can donate or accept a proton depending on its reaction partner. HCO₃⁻ can donate H⁺ to form CO₃²⁻ or accept H⁺ to form H₂CO₃. Water can form OH⁻ or H₃O⁺. A species having both hydrogen and a negative charge is a candidate, but those features alone are not proof; the chemical structure and proton-transfer behaviour matter. Write two equations to demonstrate the two roles. The broader term amphoteric describes acting as an acid or base, whereas amphiprotic explicitly refers to proton transfer. Use the model and terminology requested by the question.", "diagram": "amphiprotic"}, {"title": "Monoprotic and polyprotic acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "A monoprotic acid can donate one relevant proton per molecule, while a polyprotic acid can donate more in successive steps. Ethanoic acid is monoprotic despite containing four hydrogens, because only its acidic O–H proton is readily donated in the course context. H₃PO₄ has three successive proton-donation steps, each with a different equilibrium. Do not equate the total hydrogen subscript with acid proticity. Each later donation from an increasingly negative species is typically less favourable. In complete-neutralisation stoichiometry, the number of donated protons affects the acid:OH⁻ ratio, but it does not mean all ionisation steps are complete in water.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strength and concentration are separate", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acid strength describes degree of ionisation at equilibrium under stated conditions. Concentration describes total amount per solution volume. A dilute strong acid may have lower [H⁺] than a concentrated weak acid. Compare strength under suitable matched conditions or use Ka. At equal analytical concentration, a strong monoprotic acid generally has greater [H⁺] than a weak monoprotic acid. Conductivity also depends on ion concentration and mobility, so it alone is not a universal strength ranking across unmatched solutions. A particle diagram should show mostly ions for a strong dilute acid and a mixture dominated by un-ionised molecules for a typical weak acid.", "diagram": "acid-strength"}, {"title": "Acidity constant Ka", "kind": "learn", "text": "For HA(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + A⁻(aq), Ka = [H₃O⁺][A⁻]/[HA] when solvent activity is treated as constant. Larger Ka generally indicates stronger acid at the same temperature. The equilibrium concentration of un-ionised HA is used, not automatically the original total concentration. The Year 12 syllabus asks for strength represented through equations and acidity constants. Qualitative comparison and expression-writing are core. Approximate weak-acid equilibrium calculations are useful extension where explicitly labelled; do not treat every added algebraic method as a separate syllabus requirement. Ka does not describe how fast ionisation reaches equilibrium.", "diagram": "ka"}, {"title": "Water and Kw", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water weakly self-ionises: 2H₂O(l) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq). In course notation, Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C. Neutrality means [H⁺] = [OH⁻], giving each concentration 1.00 × 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹ at that temperature. Adding acid raises [H⁺] and shifts the ionisation equilibrium, reducing [OH⁻]. Kw depends on temperature, so neutral pH is not universally 7. A low ionic concentration explains water’s weak conductivity compared with electrolyte solutions. “Pure water has no ions” is incorrect.", "diagram": "water-ionisation"}, {"title": "pH and strong-acid calculations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a dilute strong monoprotic acid, assume near-complete ionisation and use [H⁺] ≈ analytical acid concentration when water’s own contribution is negligible. Then pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]. If pH is given, [H⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ. A very dilute acid near 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹ requires considering water rather than naively applying this approximation. Do not automatically multiply every polyprotic acid concentration by its total proton count; successive ionisation can be incomplete. For sulfuric acid, the first ionisation is strong and the second depends on equilibrium. Follow any explicit simplifying assumption given in a numerical question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong bases and excess after reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a strong hydroxide, obtain [OH⁻] from dissociation stoichiometry, then [H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] and calculate pH. At 25 °C, pH + pOH = 14.00 in the course approximation. Ba(OH)₂ gives two OH⁻ per formula unit. When acid and base mix, calculate their reacting mole amounts first, determine excess H⁺ or OH⁻ and divide by the combined solution volume. Do not average the initial pH values. Logarithmic pH values are not additive, and neutralisation changes the species. Equivalence composition may involve salt hydrolysis if weak acid or base products are present.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Salt hydrolysis: weak-acid conjugate bases", "kind": "learn", "text": "A salt can dissociate into ions that then react with water. Ethanoate accepts a proton: CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻, making an ethanoate salt solution basic if the cation is effectively non-hydrolysing. Na⁺ is a spectator in this model. Show the net ionic hydrolysis equation and identify the proton acceptor. The fact that a salt forms in neutralisation does not guarantee a neutral final solution. A weak acid’s conjugate base has appreciable basicity compared with a strong acid’s conjugate base. Actual pH also depends on concentration and equilibrium strength.", "diagram": "salt-hydrolysis"}, {"title": "Salt hydrolysis: weak-base conjugate acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ammonium can donate a proton: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺. Therefore NH₄Cl solution is acidic in the course model; chloride has negligible basic hydrolysis. Salts of a strong acid and a strong base with non-hydrolysing ions are approximately neutral at 25 °C. For ions from polyprotic acids, an amphiprotic species may both donate and accept, and relative acid/base tendencies determine the net behaviour. HCO₃⁻ is commonly mildly basic, while H₂PO₄⁻ is commonly acidic. Do not force every salt into a simplistic one-direction rule without considering its ion species.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "What makes a buffer", "kind": "learn", "text": "A buffer typically contains a weak acid and a substantial amount of its conjugate base, or a weak base and its conjugate acid. It resists pH change when small amounts of strong acid or base are added. An ethanoic acid/ethanoate mixture supplies both HA and A⁻. Merely mixing two arbitrary acids is not sufficient. A buffer does not hold pH perfectly constant or work indefinitely. Its components react with the added species, reducing the resulting change in free hydrogen-ion concentration. The relevant SCSA treatment is qualitative mechanism and capacity, so the explanations emphasise equations rather than requiring an unstated quantitative buffer formula.", "diagram": "buffer"}, {"title": "Buffer response to added acid", "kind": "learn", "text": "For HA/A⁻, added H⁺ is consumed mainly by A⁻: A⁻ + H⁺ → HA. In the weak-acid equilibrium HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, increasing H⁺ causes net reverse reaction. The base component decreases and the acid component increases, so the pH changes somewhat rather than remaining exactly fixed. A good explanation names which component reacts, gives the equation and connects consumption of added H⁺ to a smaller pH change. If little A⁻ remains, the buffer cannot absorb much additional acid. Compare with plain water, which lacks a substantial reservoir of the matching conjugate component.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Buffer response to added base and capacity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Added OH⁻ is consumed by the weak acid: HA + OH⁻ → A⁻ + H₂O. The acid component decreases and the base component increases. Buffer capacity depends on the amounts of both components available, not just the starting pH. A more concentrated conjugate mixture can usually absorb more added acid/base before a large pH shift. Dilution reduces amounts per unit volume and practical capacity even when the ratio remains similar. Once a component is substantially exhausted, added strong reagent controls pH more directly. Match a qualitative capacity comparison to actual amounts and additions specified in the question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "An acid–base explanation in an unfamiliar context", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identify the species, compare before/after formulae to locate the proton, then assign donor and acceptor. Identify conjugate pairs and any hydrolysis reaction. If a numerical pH is requested, determine whether complete ionisation, equilibrium or excess after stoichiometric reaction is applicable. For a buffer question, name the reservoir species that consumes the added reagent and explain finite capacity. Use states and balanced charges. A persuasive answer applies the model to the actual formulas provided, rather than merely quoting the definition of an acid. Check that every proposed conjugate differs by exactly one H⁺.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Buffer equilibrium rate explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "After OH⁻ reacts with HA, HA concentration decreases and A⁻ concentration increases. For HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, forward ionisation tendency initially falls relative to the reverse tendency, and the system readjusts. The overall buffer consumes most of the small added base while free [H⁺] changes less than in an unbuffered solution. For added acid, increased H⁺ increases reverse encounter tendency and A⁻ consumes it. These collision and species explanations supply the mechanism that a bare “the system opposes change” sentence omits. The final pH is slightly changed, not identical.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiprotic salts need two tendencies", "kind": "learn", "text": "H₂PO₄⁻ can donate H⁺ to become HPO₄²⁻ or accept H⁺ to become H₃PO₄. Which tendency dominates in water depends on the relevant acid/base constants; its solution is commonly acidic in the course context. HCO₃⁻ also has both roles but commonly gives a mildly basic solution. Show the possible equations when the question asks for amphiprotic behaviour. For a numerical or unfamiliar comparison, use supplied equilibrium data rather than a charge-only rule. Negative charge does not guarantee that every ion’s solution is basic, just as having hydrogen does not guarantee predominant acidity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Conjugate pairs", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Conjugate pairs", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Strong-base pH", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Strong-base pH", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Excess acid after mixing", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Excess acid after mixing", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: A basic salt", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: A basic salt", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Two buffer additions", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Strength evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Salt and buffer reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Conjugate pairs", "prompt": "For HSO₄⁻ + HCO₃⁻ ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₂CO₃, identify the forward acid/base and both conjugate pairs.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺ and is the acid. [1]", "HCO₃⁻ accepts H⁺ and is the base. [1]", "HSO₄⁻/SO₄²⁻ is one conjugate pair. [1]", "H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ is the other. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Strong-base pH", "prompt": "Find pH of 0.0100 mol L⁻¹ Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C, assuming complete dissociation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["[OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0100 = 0.0200 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "[H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0200 = 5.00 × 10⁻¹³. [1]", "pH = −log₁₀(5.00 × 10⁻¹³). [1]", "pH = 12.301. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Excess acid after mixing", "prompt": "Mix 20.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ HCl with 15.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Find final pH at 25 °C with additive volumes.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(H⁺) = 0.00200 mol and n(OH⁻) = 0.00150 mol. [1]", "The reaction is 1:1, leaving 0.000500 mol H⁺. [1]", "Total volume = 0.0350 L. [1]", "[H⁺] = 0.000500/0.0350 = 0.0142857 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "pH = 1.845. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A basic salt", "prompt": "Explain why aqueous sodium ethanoate is basic, using a net ionic equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The salt provides CH₃COO⁻ in solution. [1]", "CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. [1]", "Ethanoate accepts a proton from water. [1]", "OH⁻ formation increases basicity; Na⁺ is a spectator in this model. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Two buffer additions", "prompt": "An ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer receives a small amount of HCl in one trial and NaOH in another. Give equations and explain its response in each.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["With acid: CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃COOH. [1]", "The conjugate base consumes most added H⁺. [1]", "The pH fall is reduced, not eliminated. [1]", "With base: CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O. [1]", "The weak acid consumes added OH⁻. [1]", "Capacity is finite because either component can be depleted. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Strength evidence", "prompt": "Two monoprotic acids have Ka values 1.0 × 10⁻³ and 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ at the same temperature. Identify the stronger acid and explain why this alone does not specify pH.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The acid with Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻³ is stronger. [1]", "Its equilibrium favours ionised products more strongly. [1]", "pH also depends on concentration, so Ka alone is insufficient. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Salt and buffer reasoning", "prompt": "Compare solutions of NaCl, NH₄Cl and sodium ethanoate at 25 °C. Then explain why sodium ethanoate alone is not equivalent in capacity to a prepared ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["NaCl is approximately neutral in the non-hydrolysing-ion model. [1]", "NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ gives acidic ammonium chloride. [1]", "CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻ gives basic sodium ethanoate. [1]", "A prepared buffer has substantial weak acid and conjugate base amounts. [1]", "The weak acid consumes added OH⁻ and the conjugate base consumes added H⁺. [1]", "Ethanoate alone lacks the same substantial acid reservoir, so its response/capacity differs. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Conjugate acid–base pairs differ by", "o": ["One H⁺", "One electron only", "One O atom", "One neutron"], "a": 0, "why": "Proton transfer creates the conjugate species."}, {"q": "NH₄Cl is commonly acidic because", "o": ["Cl⁻ is a strong base", "NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water", "Na⁺ forms", "It is necessarily concentrated"], "a": 1, "why": "Ammonium hydrolysis forms hydronium."}, {"q": "A buffer contains", "o": ["Any two neutral salts", "A substantial conjugate acid/base pair", "Only pure water", "Only a strong acid"], "a": 1, "why": "Both components provide capacity for consuming added acid or base."}, {"q": "At 25 °C Kw is", "o": ["1.00 × 10⁻⁷", "1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴", "14 mol L⁻¹", "Always independent of temperature"], "a": 1, "why": "Kw is the product [H⁺][OH⁻] at the stated temperature."}, {"q": "Ethanoic acid is monoprotic because", "o": ["It has only one hydrogen atom", "Only one relevant acidic proton is donated", "It fully ionises", "It cannot form a conjugate base"], "a": 1, "why": "Total hydrogens and acid proticity are different counts."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-titrations", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "title": "Volumetric analysis and titration exam problems", "scope": "Standards; apparatus; endpoints and indicators; titration curves; concentration, purity, dilution and error direction", "screens": [{"title": "Purpose of volumetric analysis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Volumetric analysis determines an unknown amount from the volume of a known-concentration reagent required by a balanced reaction. Acid–base titration relies on a suitable reaction and a measurable endpoint close to stoichiometric equivalence. The known solution is the titrant, usually delivered from a burette. The analyte aliquot commonly sits in a conical flask. At equivalence, reactant amounts match the equation’s mole ratio, not necessarily equal mole amounts. A 2:1 OH⁻:diprotic-acid reaction needs two hydroxide moles per acid mole. Establish the equation before interpreting titre data.", "diagram": "titration-apparatus"}, {"title": "Primary standards and standardisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A suitable primary standard has known purity and composition, is stable during storage and weighing, and reacts predictably. Accurate weighing and quantitative transfer establish its amount, while a volumetric flask defines solution volume. Some reagents, including NaOH solutions, can change through water uptake or reaction with CO₂, so their concentration is commonly determined by standardisation against a reliable standard. A label on a bottle is not a substitute for verified concentration. Distinguish making a solution of calculated nominal concentration from measuring its actual concentration. Report the standardisation data and precision that support subsequent unknown calculations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Burette readings and delivered volume", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the meniscus at eye level to reduce parallax, using the appropriate reference for the liquid. Delivered titre = final reading − initial reading; the initial value need not be zero. Remove air bubbles from the tip before recording the start. A funnel left in the burette can add unmeasured drops during a trial. Read and record all digits warranted by the instrument, even a final zero. A titre involves two readings, so both contribute uncertainty. Use repeated close titres according to the laboratory’s stated criterion, and justify treatment of outliers rather than silently selecting convenient values.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rinsing equipment with purpose", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rinse a burette with the titrant to avoid diluting it with residual water. Rinse a pipette with the analyte solution to avoid diluting the measured aliquot. A conical flask can be rinsed with distilled water because added water changes volume but not the analyte moles already transferred. Rinsing that flask with analyte and leaving residue adds unmeasured analyte, causing a larger titre. Equipment rules follow amount conservation. Explain the causal chain from contamination or dilution to moles and calculated concentration. Merely saying “rinse for accuracy” does not demonstrate understanding of which error is prevented.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Endpoint versus equivalence point", "kind": "learn", "text": "Equivalence point is the theoretical stoichiometric point. Endpoint is the observed signal, such as a persistent indicator colour change. A suitable indicator changes over the steep pH region near equivalence, making endpoint a useful approximation. The two points are not conceptually identical. Add titrant slowly near the endpoint, swirl and rinse solution from flask walls with distilled water. Excess titrant gives a high recorded titre relative to the desired endpoint. A colour judged too early gives a low titre. A pH meter can trace the curve but needs calibration and sensible sampling near the steep region.", "diagram": "titration-curve"}, {"title": "Indicators as equilibrium systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "An acid-form indicator may be represented HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻, where HIn and In⁻ have different colours. Added acid favours HIn; added base removes H⁺ and favours In⁻. Colour transition occurs over a range rather than at one exact universal pH. Common approximate ranges are methyl orange 3.1–4.4, bromothymol blue 6.0–7.6 and phenolphthalein 8.2–10.0. Use supplied ranges when choosing an indicator. A good selection refers to the curve’s steep region and expected equivalence pH, not simply “this indicator is used for acids.” Excess indicator can itself influence a very small analyte amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong acid–strong base curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a strong monoprotic acid titrated with strong hydroxide at 25 °C, the initial pH is low, then rises as H⁺ is consumed. Near equivalence the slope is steep and equivalence pH is approximately 7 if the resulting salt does not hydrolyse appreciably. Beyond equivalence, excess OH⁻ controls pH. The volume at equivalence follows the balanced ratio and concentrations. The curve’s position is not determined by acid strength alone because amount and concentration matter. Use the actual direction of titration: adding acid to base gives a descending curve with corresponding regions.", "diagram": "strong-titration"}, {"title": "Weak acid–strong base curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "A weak acid begins at a higher pH than an equally concentrated strong acid. As base is added, weak acid and conjugate base coexist in a buffer region. At equivalence, the conjugate-base salt hydrolyses to form OH⁻, so pH is above 7 at 25 °C. Beyond equivalence excess strong base dominates. Phenolphthalein often fits the steep region. Half-equivalence has equal amounts of weak acid and its conjugate base and provides useful equilibrium interpretation, though numerical buffer formulas beyond syllabus requirements are optional extension. Do not conclude that the weak acid requires fewer hydroxide moles for complete neutralisation merely because it ionises partly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong acid–weak base and weak–weak cases", "kind": "learn", "text": "When a strong acid titrates a weak base, the conjugate-acid salt formed at equivalence can make pH below 7. Indicator selection should match the relevant steep region. A weak acid–weak base titration may lack a sufficiently sharp transition for reliable visual endpoint detection. Equivalence pH depends on relative acid/base equilibrium tendencies and concentration, not on a universal pH-7 rule. State whether acid or base is being added before describing the curve’s direction. A named salt’s hydrolysis equation explains the equivalence pH more clearly than labelling the reagents weak or strong alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A direct titration calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Calculate titrant moles from concentration × titre in litres. Use the balanced coefficient ratio to find analyte moles in the aliquot. Divide by aliquot volume for its concentration. If that solution was diluted from an original sample, apply the dilution factor afterward. Show chemical labels on each mole quantity. A compact expression can obscure an incorrect ratio, so write at least the equation and ratio step in an exam. Check whether the question asks concentration of the diluted solution, the original sample or mass of a component in a larger batch.", "diagram": "titration-path"}, {"title": "Purity and percentage composition", "kind": "learn", "text": "To determine sample purity, use titration moles to find the reacting compound amount in the entire prepared solution, then convert to mass. Divide the mass of the target pure compound by the original impure sample mass and multiply by 100. Scale aliquot moles by flask volume/aliquot volume before calculating total mass. A nominally pure result above 100% suggests error, water/impurity assumptions or an incorrect reaction interpretation. Not every impurity is inert: an additional acid- or base-reactive impurity can bias the result. The question must specify or justify which components react under the conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Back titration as a calculation strategy", "kind": "learn", "text": "When direct titration is unsuitable, a known excess reagent can react with a sample and the remaining excess can be titrated. Target consumption = initially added reagent moles − leftover reagent moles. Then apply the target reaction’s ratio. This is a useful multi-step exam strategy where the data and reactions are supplied. Do not equate the second titre directly with target amount: it measures leftover reagent. State assumptions about completion, selectivity and loss of reagent. Separate the two balanced equations, label intermediate amounts and check that consumed amount is positive and smaller than initial amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Error direction: trace the amount pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "If the endpoint is overshot while calculating an acid from known base, the titre is too large, calculated base moles too high and inferred acid concentration too high. If the burette tip contains air initially and fills during delivery, part of the recorded volume fills the tip rather than entering the flask, again exaggerating delivered volume. If an analyte pipette contains water, the aliquot is diluted, requiring less titrant and underestimating the original analyte concentration. Error directions depend on which quantity is unknown and the analysis pathway. Follow the actual calculation rather than memorising a disconnected table.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concordance and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Close titres suggest repeatability but do not prove absence of bias. Exclude a rough trial from the final mean when it was intentionally used to locate the endpoint, and use the stated criterion for concordant analytical trials. A mean of 18.60, 18.65 and 18.62 mL is 18.623… mL, reported consistently with equipment precision, commonly 18.62 mL. If each burette reading has a specified maximum uncertainty ±0.05 mL, a conservative titre uncertainty is ±0.10 mL; other statistical treatments may be appropriate when specified. Larger titres can reduce relative volume uncertainty, provided the method remains suitable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Exam timing and written evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Before calculation, annotate the unknown, standard concentration, aliquot, titre and dilution factor. Write the reaction and coefficient ratio, convert units and keep intermediate digits. For explanatory questions, refer to specific apparatus or curve regions and state consequences in the calculated result. For a multi-part problem, use an earlier numerical result consistently and make assumptions explicit. Suggested marking guidance here rewards chemically correct equivalent methods and consequential calculation reasoning; it is original practice guidance rather than an official SCSA marking key. Review why an incorrect answer happened, not just the final number.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A SCSA concordance example", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2025 SCSA examination report identifies titres differing by 0.10 mL or less as concordant. Values 21.10, 21.16 and 21.18 mL span 0.08 mL and satisfy that comparison. Their mean is 21.146… mL, normally reported as 21.15 mL for appropriate burette precision. A rough trial is handled separately when it was used to locate the endpoint. Concordance demonstrates consistency, while an uncalibrated instrument or wrong concentration can still bias every titre. Follow specific instructions if an assessment provides a different selection rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Showing all working and drawing evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a structure-drawing task, use paper or the scratch space and show the required atoms, bonds and lone pairs explicitly. Condensed text is not an adequate substitute when a displayed formula is requested. For titration calculations, show numeric mole amounts, ratios, aliquot scaling and units rather than arrows referring to an earlier answer. Marking guidance rewards the intended chemical steps but does not mean a memorised list alone will earn every extended-response mark. Write connected explanations using the actual experiment, including why the salt affects equivalence pH.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive strong-acid titration", "kind": "interactive", "model": "titration"}, {"title": "Worked question: Direct acid concentration", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Direct acid concentration", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Diprotic acid with dilution", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Diprotic acid with dilution", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Indicator choice", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Indicator choice", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Carbonate purity", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Carbonate purity", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Rinsing and calculation bias", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Back-titration amount", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Titration quality evaluation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Direct acid concentration", "prompt": "25.00 mL HCl requires 18.60 mL of 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Determine HCl concentration.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O gives 1:1. [1]", "n(NaOH) = 0.1000 × 0.01860 = 0.001860 mol. [1]", "n(HCl) = 0.001860 mol. [1]", "c(HCl) = 0.001860/0.02500 = 0.07440 mol L⁻¹. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Diprotic acid with dilution", "prompt": "10.00 mL original H₂SO₄ is diluted to 250.0 mL. A 25.00 mL aliquot requires 20.00 mL 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH for full neutralisation. Find original acid concentration.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. [1]", "n(NaOH) = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "n(H₂SO₄) in aliquot = 0.001000 mol. [1]", "Diluted acid c = 0.001000/0.02500 = 0.04000 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "Dilution factor = 250.0/10.00 = 25.00. [1]", "Original c = 1.000 mol L⁻¹. Full stoichiometric neutralisation does not require assuming both acid ionisations are complete initially. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Indicator choice", "prompt": "A weak monoprotic acid is titrated with NaOH. Equivalence lies at pH 8.8, with a steep region from 7.6 to 10.2. Choose between methyl orange 3.1–4.4 and phenolphthalein 8.2–10.0 and justify.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Choose phenolphthalein. [1]", "Its transition lies within the steep region near equivalence. [1]", "Methyl orange changes too early, outside that region. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Carbonate purity", "prompt": "1.500 g impure Na₂CO₃ is dissolved to 250.0 mL. A 25.00 mL aliquot requires 20.00 mL 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ HCl. Assuming inert impurities, find purity using M = 106.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O. [1]", "n(HCl) = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "n(Na₂CO₃) in aliquot = 0.001000 mol. [1]", "Total amount = 0.001000 × 10.00 = 0.01000 mol. [1]", "Pure mass = 0.01000 × 106.0 = 1.060 g. [1]", "Purity = 1.060/1.500 × 100 = 70.67%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Rinsing and calculation bias", "prompt": "The analyte pipette is rinsed only with water before transferring acid. Explain the bias in acid concentration calculated from a correctly standardised base titrant.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Residual water dilutes the acid aliquot. [1]", "The measured aliquot contains fewer acid moles than intended. [1]", "A smaller base titre is required. [1]", "The calculated original acid concentration is too low. Rinse the pipette with analyte. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Back-titration amount", "prompt": "A carbonate sample reacts with 0.01000 mol HCl. Leftover acid requires 30.00 mL of 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Find carbonate amount assuming CO₃²⁻ consumes two H⁺.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Leftover acid = n(NaOH) = 0.003000 mol. [1]", "Acid consumed = 0.01000 − 0.003000 = 0.00700 mol. [1]", "Carbonate amount is half the consumed acid amount. [1]", "n(CO₃²⁻) = 0.00350 mol. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Titration quality evaluation", "prompt": "A student uses titres 19.20, 19.22 and 19.19 mL but leaves a persistent air bubble in the burette tip at the first reading of every trial. Evaluate precision, bias and the inferred analyte concentration.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["The close titres indicate repeatability/precision. [1]", "They do not prove accuracy. [1]", "If part of the recorded delivery fills the tip, less titrant reaches the flask than the reading difference indicates. [1]", "The recorded titre is too high for the actual delivered amount. [1]", "For acid calculated from known base, inferred acid concentration is too high. [1]", "Fill and clear the tip before initial reading and verify the apparatus, rather than relying on repeats alone. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Equivalence means", "o": ["Always equal acid/base concentrations", "Stoichiometric amounts match the balanced equation", "pH must always be 7", "Indicator is necessarily exactly colourless"], "a": 1, "why": "Coefficient ratios determine equivalent amounts."}, {"q": "A conical flask may contain extra distilled water because", "o": ["It adds analyte moles", "It changes volume without changing analyte moles", "It increases acid strength", "It standardises the burette"], "a": 1, "why": "The reacting amount is unchanged by pure water."}, {"q": "A weak acid–strong base equivalence solution is commonly", "o": ["Basic from conjugate-base hydrolysis", "Always neutral", "A pure strong acid", "Unable to contain ions"], "a": 0, "why": "The weak-acid conjugate base reacts with water to form OH⁻."}, {"q": "A standard solution has", "o": ["Verified known concentration", "Any printed bottle label only", "No ions", "A mandatory pH of 7"], "a": 0, "why": "Its concentration must be established reliably."}, {"q": "A burette titre equals", "o": ["Initial minus final", "Final minus initial", "Final reading alone always", "Aliquot volume times pH"], "a": 1, "why": "Delivered volume is the difference of the two readings."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-redox", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "title": "Redox equations and reaction tendency", "scope": "Electron transfer; oxidation numbers; acidic half-equations; metal/halogen displacement; reduction potentials and predictions", "screens": [{"title": "Oxidation and reduction as electron transfer", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain. They occur together because electrons transferred from one species are received by another. Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ is oxidation; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu is reduction. An oxidising agent causes another species to be oxidised and is itself reduced. A reducing agent causes reduction and is itself oxidised. The terms describe roles in a specified reaction. A metal can be a reducing agent even though its oxidised metal ion can participate differently in another system. Write electrons explicitly in half-equations and ensure they cancel in the overall equation.", "diagram": "electron-transfer"}, {"title": "Oxidation numbers: a bookkeeping system", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation numbers assign electrons by a formal rule to track oxidation changes. An uncombined element has oxidation number 0. A monatomic ion has its ionic charge. Hydrogen is usually +1 and oxygen usually −2 in the course examples, with known exceptions such as peroxides. The sum equals the species charge. In MnO₄⁻, Mn + 4(−2) = −1, so Mn is +7. Oxidation number is not necessarily a measured charge on an atom in a covalent molecule. It is useful for comparing before and after, especially where direct electron transfer is hidden in a balanced equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recognising oxidation-number changes", "kind": "learn", "text": "An oxidation-number increase indicates oxidation; a decrease indicates reduction. In Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺, iron rises from +2 to +3 and loses one electron. In MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺, manganese falls from +7 to +2 and gains five electrons per manganese. Track the specific atom and account for how many of those atoms appear. A reaction can transfer electrons even if oxygen is not involved. Conversely, acid–base proton transfer is not automatically redox. Identify changes rather than relying on the presence of an acid, gas or colour change as proof.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Simple metal and halogen half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A metal oxidation equation is M → Mᶻ⁺ + ze⁻. Halogen reduction is X₂ + 2e⁻ → 2X⁻. When magnesium reduces copper(II), Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ and Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu combine directly. Halogens can oxidise less strongly oxidising halide partners according to their reduction tendencies: Cl₂ can oxidise Br⁻ to Br₂. Describe the species that loses electrons and the species that gains them, then identify the agents. A displacement equation must preserve atoms and charge, not simply exchange element names in a formula.", "diagram": "displacement"}, {"title": "Balancing acidic half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "The SCSA Year 12 redox treatment uses acidic conditions. Balance non-H/O atoms first. Add H₂O to balance oxygen, H⁺ to balance hydrogen, then electrons to balance total charge. Finally check every atom and charge. For MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺, add 4H₂O on the right and 8H⁺ on the left. The left charge is +7 and right +2, so add five electrons to the left: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Electron placement should agree with the reduction in oxidation number. Do not introduce OH⁻ in an acidic-only method.", "diagram": "redox-balance"}, {"title": "Dichromate reduction and characteristic colours", "kind": "learn", "text": "For dichromate in acidic solution: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. Each Cr falls from +6 to +3, giving six electrons for two chromium atoms. The SCSA booklet lists dichromate orange and Cr³⁺ deep green. Permanganate is purple and Mn²⁺ pale pink, often appearing nearly colourless when dilute. Colour evidence supports a reaction but does not replace a balanced equation. MnO₄⁻ can produce other manganese species under different conditions, so acidic conditions are essential to the stated equation and observation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combining half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiply half-equations so electrons gained equal electrons lost, add and cancel identical species on opposite sides. MnO₄⁻ reduction gains five electrons while Fe²⁺ oxidation loses one. Multiply iron oxidation by five, producing MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. Check total charge: left −1 + 8 + 10 = +17, right +2 + 15 = +17. Electrons do not remain in the overall equation. Keep the smallest whole-number coefficients. Do not multiply electrode potential values when multiplying a half-equation; potential is not an extensive amount of energy.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Standard reduction potentials", "kind": "learn", "text": "The data booklet lists reduction half-equations and standard electrode potentials E°. A more positive E° indicates a greater tendency for the written reduction under standard conditions, relative to the reference electrode. The species on the reactant side can therefore be a stronger oxidising agent. A strongly negative reduction potential corresponds to a product-side species that can be a strong reducing agent when oxidised. Conditions matter: standard potentials refer to specified states, concentrations, pressures and temperature, and real reaction behaviour can differ outside them. Use the current SCSA table rather than mixing signs from an oxidation table.", "diagram": "potential-scale"}, {"title": "Selecting oxidising and reducing agents", "kind": "learn", "text": "For two potential couples, identify the proposed species actually present. A reduction potential belongs to a pair, not to an isolated element name without oxidation state. Cu²⁺ is the oxidising-agent candidate in Cu²⁺/Cu, while Cu metal is the reducing-agent candidate. A more positive reduction is favoured at the cathode when paired appropriately. Comparing only metal names can reverse agent identification. Write the reduction and the reversed oxidation, then evaluate the proposed net tendency. A reagent cannot take a role merely because its ion appears somewhere in the table if that species is not part of the mixture.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "E°cell and standard reaction tendency", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a proposed reaction, E°cell = E°reduction(cathode) − E°reduction(anode). Using Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn −0.76 V gives +1.10 V for zinc oxidation and copper-ion reduction. A positive result supports spontaneous tendency under the stated standard conditions. A negative result indicates the proposed direction is not favoured under those conditions and may require an external energy source. E° values do not multiply with stoichiometric coefficients. A favourable potential does not guarantee a fast visible reaction because kinetics, passivation and conditions can limit observation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Metal displacement and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) predicts zinc dissolving and a copper deposit, with blue copper-ion colour decreasing if the reaction proceeds. The observed deposit’s appearance can differ from a polished metal surface. A metal with a less favourable reduction pairing may not displace the ion under standard assumptions. Assess the actual concentrations and any protective oxide layers if results conflict with a simple prediction. An exam response should name changes in both species, identify agents and support tendency through potential comparison rather than merely saying one metal is “more reactive.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Halogen displacement and solvent colour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ is a redox displacement. Chlorine is reduced and acts as oxidising agent; bromide is oxidised and acts as reducing agent. Halogen colours depend on phase and solvent: the data booklet distinguishes aqueous and organic-solvent colours. Bromine is orange in aqueous solution, and iodine is brown in water but purple in many organic solvents as listed. A coloured organic layer is analytical evidence of halogen distribution, not a new elemental identity. State which layer and solvent you mean and refer to supplied data for exact observations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combustion as redox", "kind": "learn", "text": "In complete combustion of a hydrocarbon, carbon is generally oxidised toward +4 in CO₂ while oxygen is reduced from 0 in O₂ to −2 in the products. In incomplete combustion, carbon may end at +2 in CO or 0 in soot, so the electron changes differ. Combustion remains redox even when no simple ionic electron transfer is visible. Oxidation-number bookkeeping connects covalent reactions to the electron-transfer model. Use average carbon oxidation number cautiously when different carbon atoms in an organic molecule have different environments. The balanced overall equation and individual structural context govern the analysis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Redox quantities and stoichiometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "The balanced redox equation gives mole ratios for quantitative analysis. One MnO₄⁻ in acidic solution oxidises five Fe²⁺. Use n = cV for a titrant, apply the ratio and then scale aliquots or find sample mass. Oxidation-number changes help derive the coefficients but do not replace the final balanced equation. Redox volumetric examples are original multi-step practice that uses supplied reaction information. Pay attention to reagent concentration units, sample dilution and whether all iron was prepared in the relevant oxidation state. Additional oxidisable substances can interfere and invalidate a simple one-component assumption.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Checks that prevent equation errors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Check element counts, charge counts, electron cancellation and expected electron-change direction. In acidic equations, H₂O and H⁺ are balancing species, not arbitrary decorations. An oxidising agent gains electrons; a reducing agent loses them. Potentials use the reduction-table convention and are not scaled by coefficient multipliers. Match observation to the correct product and conditions. In an extended answer, distinguish thermodynamic tendency from observed reaction rate and identify uncertainty in a standard-state prediction. These checks catch common errors even when a calculator produces a plausible number.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A full observation is different from an inference", "kind": "learn", "text": "For zinc in copper(II) solution, an observation can describe a grey solid changing, a reddish deposit appearing and blue solution colour becoming paler. Naming zinc, copper or copper ions explains identity inferred from the setup and chemistry, rather than directly describing appearance. Gas evolution is observed as bubbles or effervescence; its chemical identity requires inference or a test. In equation-and-observation questions, give the visible before/after change and separately the balanced chemistry. Use the SCSA booklet for the specified species colours and attend to excess reagent and physical state.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Checking a reduction-table prediction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose couple A has E° = +0.50 V and B has −0.20 V. If the oxidised A species and reduced B species are present, A reduction with B oxidation gives +0.70 V. If only the reduced A form and oxidised B form are mixed, that proposed reverse direction gives −0.70 V. The table alone does not supply absent reactants. Identify actual species first, then decide directions. A positive value supports standard thermodynamic tendency but cannot guarantee rate, complete conversion or identical behaviour at different concentrations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Oxidation-number calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Oxidation-number calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Acidic permanganate half-equation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Acidic permanganate half-equation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: A displacement potential", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: A displacement potential", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: An iron analysis", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: An iron analysis", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Dichromate with iron", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Tendency and rate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Charge balance and observations", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Oxidation-number calculation", "prompt": "Determine chromium oxidation number in Cr₂O₇²⁻ and manganese oxidation number in MnO₄⁻.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Let Cr be x: 2x + 7(−2) = −2. [1]", "x = +6. [1]", "Let Mn be y: y + 4(−2) = −1. [1]", "y = +7. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Acidic permanganate half-equation", "prompt": "Balance MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ in acidic solution and explain the electron count.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Balance oxygen using 4H₂O on the right. [1]", "Balance hydrogen with 8H⁺ on the left. [1]", "Add 5e⁻ on the left to balance charge. [1]", "MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. [1]", "Mn falls from +7 to +2, a gain of five electrons. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A displacement potential", "prompt": "Using Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn −0.76 V, calculate E° for Zn reacting with Cu²⁺ and identify both agents.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Cu²⁺ is reduced and Zn is oxidised. [1]", "E° = +0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V. [1]", "Cu²⁺ is the oxidising agent. [1]", "Zn is the reducing agent; positive E° supports standard spontaneous tendency. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "An iron analysis", "prompt": "20.00 mL of 0.02000 mol L⁻¹ MnO₄⁻ oxidises Fe²⁺ in acidic solution. Use a 1:5 ratio and find the Fe²⁺ amount and iron mass, M(Fe) = 55.85 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.02000 × 0.02000 = 0.0004000 mol. [1]", "n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 0.0004000 = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "m(Fe) = 0.002000 × 55.85. [1]", "m = 0.1117 g for the analysed portion. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Dichromate with iron", "prompt": "Combine acidic dichromate reduction with Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ to obtain the net ionic equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Dichromate requires six electrons: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. [1]", "Multiply iron oxidation by six. [1]", "Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6Fe³⁺. [1]", "Atoms and total charge (+24 on each side) balance. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Tendency and rate", "prompt": "A proposed redox reaction has positive E°cell but little visible change. Explain why this does not automatically invalidate the potential calculation.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Positive E° supports thermodynamic tendency under standard conditions. [1]", "A large activation barrier or passivating surface can make the reaction slow. [1]", "Actual concentrations and conditions may also differ from standard values. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Charge balance and observations", "prompt": "In acid, permanganate oxidises Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺. Write the net equation, identify both agents and describe an expected colour consequence using supplied SCSA colours.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. [2: species/coefficient and atom/charge balance]", "MnO₄⁻ gains electrons and is the oxidising agent. [1]", "Fe²⁺ loses electrons and is the reducing agent. [1]", "Purple permanganate is consumed toward pale-pink Mn²⁺, often very weakly coloured when dilute. [1]", "Iron ions also change from pale-green Fe²⁺ toward pale-brown Fe³⁺; the mixture and concentration influence overall appearance. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q7", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "An oxidising agent is itself", "o": ["Oxidised", "Reduced", "Always neutral", "Always a metal"], "a": 1, "why": "It accepts electrons while causing oxidation of another species."}, {"q": "Mn in MnO₄⁻ has oxidation number", "o": ["−1", "+2", "+7", "+8"], "a": 2, "why": "x + 4(−2) = −1 gives +7."}, {"q": "Multiplying a half-equation by two makes its E°", "o": ["Double", "Half", "Unchanged", "Always negative"], "a": 2, "why": "Potential is an intensive quantity."}, {"q": "A redox equation must conserve", "o": ["Atoms only", "Charge only", "Both atoms and charge", "Only colour"], "a": 2, "why": "Electron cancellation and balance ensure conservation."}, {"q": "The larger reduction potential usually identifies the stronger", "o": ["Reducing agent on the reactant side", "Oxidising agent on the reactant side", "Acid in every case", "Catalyst"], "a": 1, "why": "A more favourable reduction means greater electron-accepting tendency."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "title": "Galvanic cells, electrolysis and corrosion", "scope": "Cell models; electron/ion flow; standard voltages; primary/secondary/fuel cells; electroplating and refining; corrosion protection", "screens": [{"title": "Separating half-reactions into a cell", "kind": "learn", "text": "A galvanic cell separates oxidation and reduction so electrons travel through an external conductor and can do electrical work. The anode is where oxidation occurs and the cathode where reduction occurs in every electrochemical cell. For Zn/Cu, Zn atoms become Zn²⁺ at the anode and Cu²⁺ becomes Cu at the cathode. Solutions provide ionic conduction and a salt bridge or other separator supports charge balance. Electron flow through the wire differs from ion migration through solution. A disconnected circuit or loss of charge balance prevents sustained operation. The cell converts chemical energy from a spontaneous reaction into electrical energy.", "diagram": "galvanic"}, {"title": "Electrode signs and electron flow", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a galvanic cell, the anode is negative relative to the cathode because oxidation supplies electrons to the external circuit. Electrons travel anode → cathode. Conventional current in the external wire is opposite electron flow. In an electrolytic cell, a power source drives a non-spontaneous reaction, with a positive anode and negative cathode. The oxidation/reduction definitions do not change. Do not memorise “anode is always negative.” Determine cell type and reaction. A diagram should label signs, reactions and directions separately so one does not substitute for the others.", "diagram": "cell-signs"}, {"title": "Salt bridge and electrolyte", "kind": "learn", "text": "The salt bridge supplies an ionic path and helps prevent charge accumulation while avoiding direct mixing of the main solutions. At a Zn anode, production of Zn²⁺ increases positive charge tendency, so bridge anions migrate toward that half-cell. At a Cu cathode, Cu²⁺ is removed, so bridge cations migrate toward it. Electrons do not flow through the salt bridge as the normal transport mechanism. Use an electrolyte that does not precipitate or react appreciably with the cell species. A porous separator can fulfil a similar charge-balance role in other designs. Ion motion inside each half-cell also contributes to maintaining electroneutrality.", "diagram": "salt-bridge"}, {"title": "Cell notation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conventional galvanic notation places the anode on the left and cathode on the right. Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) || Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s) uses a single line for a phase boundary and a double line for the junction between half-cells. Species within the same phase may be separated by commas. An inert platinum or graphite electrode can provide a conducting surface where the reacting couple has no suitable solid conductor. Notation is a compact representation and does not show every construction detail. Include states and specified concentrations when needed, and pair the notation with half-equations to make electron transfer unambiguous.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Standard voltage and conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with both entries taken as reduction potentials. A Zn/Cu cell gives +1.10 V from +0.34 and −0.76 V. Standard values apply to specified standard conditions, commonly 25 °C with unit activities and standard gas pressure. Actual voltage changes with conditions and load. A measured open-circuit voltage is not the same as power output: power also depends on current and internal resistance. Do not multiply a voltage by the number of transferred electrons when balancing. The number of electrons matters for charge and energy quantities, while voltage is energy per charge.", "diagram": "cell-voltage"}, {"title": "Primary and secondary cells", "kind": "learn", "text": "A primary cell is intended for discharge without practical repeated recharging, such as the Leclanché-type cell context. A secondary cell uses chemistry that can be driven substantially backward during charging, such as a lead-acid accumulator. In discharge, the device acts galvanically; during charge, an external source drives electrolytic reversal. Reaction direction and electrode roles must be considered at each stage. A battery consists of one or more cells and its voltage and capacity depend on design. Evaluate materials, energy density, safety, lifespan, waste and recycling rather than claiming one cell chemistry is universally best for every use.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Lead-acid accumulator as a model", "kind": "learn", "text": "During lead-acid discharge, lead and lead dioxide react with sulfuric acid overall to form lead sulfate and water: Pb + PbO₂ + 2H₂SO₄ → 2PbSO₄ + 2H₂O. Lead metal is oxidised and Pb in PbO₂ is reduced. Charging drives the overall chemistry backward using supplied electrical energy. The reaction is a useful secondary-cell example rather than a requirement to memorise every construction detail. Changes in electrolyte concentration can provide evidence of state of charge. Practical degradation, side reactions and operating constraints limit perfect reversibility, so a model of ideal recharge does not guarantee unlimited battery life.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydrogen fuel cells", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fuel cell continuously receives fuel and oxidant rather than storing all reactants internally. An acidic hydrogen cell can use anode 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ and cathode O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O, giving 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. An alkaline design uses different ion-bearing half-equations but the same net reaction. At the point of use, water is the main chemical product; full environmental impact depends on how hydrogen and electricity for production are sourced. Assess efficiency, infrastructure and storage as well as direct emissions.", "diagram": "fuel-cell"}, {"title": "Electrolytic cells and external energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electrolysis uses a power supply to drive a reaction whose proposed direction is non-spontaneous under the given conditions. The source withdraws electrons from the anode and supplies them to the cathode. Oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode. The positive and negative terminal connections are essential to a diagram. In a simple molten salt, cations are reduced at the cathode and anions oxidised at the anode. In aqueous solutions, water and other species can compete, so products depend on the actual ions, electrodes and conditions. Do not assume every aqueous metal ion plates as metal.", "diagram": "electrolytic"}, {"title": "Molten versus aqueous products", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molten sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻, so ideal electrolysis gives Na at the cathode and Cl₂ at the anode. In aqueous NaCl, water is a possible reduction reactant and sodium metal is not the normal cathode product. The course’s aqueous product predictions should use supplied reduction data and stated conditions, noting concentration and overpotential limitations where relevant. An active electrode can itself oxidise, changing the anode reaction. Compare candidates before writing equations. Standard potentials offer a useful tendency guide but do not uniquely capture every practical electrode surface effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Copper electrolytic refining", "kind": "learn", "text": "In copper refining, impure copper serves as anode and a suitable pure copper sheet as cathode. Anode: Cu(s) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻. Cathode: Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s). Copper transfers from anode to cathode under electrical drive. Some impurities dissolve but are not deposited under chosen conditions, while less easily oxidised material can remain as anode sludge. The electrolyte supplies copper ions, and the ideal net copper-ion concentration can remain approximately stable when matching copper processes occur. Explain electrode mass decrease/increase and the separation of impurities rather than saying current makes every impurity disappear.", "diagram": "copper-refining"}, {"title": "Silver electroplating", "kind": "learn", "text": "The object to be plated is the cathode because metal ions must gain electrons and deposit there. For a simplified silver-ion system, Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag at the object. A silver anode can dissolve by Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻ to replenish ions. Appropriate electrolytes and operating conditions are chosen in practice; do not treat this learning diagram as a complete laboratory recipe. The object must be conductive or appropriately prepared. Coating thickness and uniformity depend on current distribution, time and surface preparation. Swapping the object to the anode would promote oxidation instead of the desired metal deposition.", "diagram": "silver-plating"}, {"title": "Iron corrosion as an electrochemical process", "kind": "learn", "text": "Different regions of a moist iron surface can act as local anodes and cathodes. Iron oxidation is Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻. In aerated near-neutral water, oxygen reduction can be represented O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻. Later reactions form hydrated iron oxide products associated with rust. Oxygen, water and ionic conduction allow the process to continue; salts can increase conductivity. Rust is not a simple uniform Fe₂O₃ coating with no hydration complexity. A local-cell model explains why damage can occur even without a separate visible battery or external wire.", "diagram": "corrosion"}, {"title": "Coatings and sacrificial protection", "kind": "learn", "text": "Paint or a suitable intact coating limits access of water and oxygen. A sacrificial metal such as zinc or magnesium is more readily oxidised than iron and supplies electrons, helping keep iron cathodic. Protection can continue at a scratch if electrical contact and conditions are suitable. A less readily oxidised coating may protect as a barrier when intact but can accelerate local iron corrosion when damaged through galvanic coupling. Explain the difference between exclusion and cathodic protection. An impressed-current system uses an external source to maintain the protected structure as a cathode. Material choice depends on environment and maintenance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluating an electrochemical diagram", "kind": "learn", "text": "Check oxidation at anode, reduction at cathode, external electron direction, electrode signs appropriate to cell type and ionic charge balance. Label actual species and reactions rather than only generic plus/minus symbols. State whether the electrode is inert or consumed. Use cell potentials only with correctly oriented reduction entries. For corrosion and fuel-cell evaluation, connect the diagram to the chemical mechanism and practical limitations. A schematic is not to scale and does not show all internal resistance, side reactions or engineering components. Identifying those limits helps explain differences between calculated and measured performance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Inert electrodes and ion-only couples", "kind": "learn", "text": "A half-cell such as Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ has no conducting metal product to serve automatically as an electrode. An inert conducting surface such as platinum can exchange electrons with the ions without being the net reactant in the idealised process. A cell notation includes that solid electrode and lists ions in the same phase with a comma. The solution still requires an ionic conduction path. Distinguish a chemically active electrode such as copper in refining from an inert one; their anode reactions can be different even when the same solution is used.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Context-specific corrosion protection", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a submerged iron jetty structure, a sacrificial metal can be attached in electrical contact and replaced as it is consumed. The surrounding water provides ionic conduction, while protective coatings reduce access to corrosive conditions. An impressed-current system is another cathodic approach when appropriately engineered. A suggestion must match the context: a paint coating may need maintenance at scratches, and a disconnected sacrificial piece cannot supply electrons to the iron. Explain which component is oxidised and why the protected iron is maintained as a cathode. Do not claim the sacrificial material lasts indefinitely.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Cell voltage and notation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Cell voltage and notation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Electroplating an object", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Electroplating an object", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Sacrificial protection", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Sacrificial protection", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Copper refining evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Fuel-cell environmental claim", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: A failed galvanic cell", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "prompt": "State both half-equations, electron flow and salt-bridge ion migration for a Zn/Cu galvanic cell.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. [1]", "Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. [1]", "Electrons flow from Zn to Cu through the external circuit. [1]", "Bridge anions migrate toward the Zn half-cell. [1]", "Bridge cations migrate toward the Cu half-cell. [1]", "The bridge maintains charge balance through ions, not electron transport. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Cell voltage and notation", "prompt": "Using Ag⁺/Ag +0.80 V and Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V, write a spontaneous galvanic cell notation and calculate E°cell.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Cu is oxidised and Ag⁺ reduced. [1]", "Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(aq) || Ag⁺(aq) | Ag(s). [1]", "E° = +0.80 − +0.34. [1]", "E°cell = +0.46 V; do not double it when using two Ag⁺. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Electroplating an object", "prompt": "Explain where to connect a metal object for silver electroplating and give the cathode and soluble-anode half-equations.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The object connects as the cathode to the negative terminal in an electrolytic cell. [1]", "Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag at the object. [1]", "Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻ at a silver anode. [1]", "The anode can replenish silver ions while the coating grows. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Sacrificial protection", "prompt": "A scratched zinc coating can still protect iron. Explain the mechanism and contrast it with paint.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Zinc is more readily oxidised under suitable conditions. [1]", "It supplies electrons and helps keep iron cathodic. [1]", "Electrical contact and an electrolyte pathway are needed for protection. [1]", "Paint mainly excludes water and oxygen, so a scratch can remove its local barrier protection. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Copper refining evidence", "prompt": "Describe changes at an impure copper anode and pure copper cathode during ideal electrolytic refining, including the role of impurities.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Copper at the anode oxidises to Cu²⁺ and anode mass decreases. [1]", "Cu²⁺ reduces at the cathode and cathode mass increases. [1]", "Both processes involve two electrons per copper atom. [1]", "Some impurities remain in solution under selected conditions. [1]", "Less readily oxidised impurities can collect as anode sludge. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Fuel-cell environmental claim", "prompt": "Evaluate “a hydrogen fuel cell has no environmental impact because it makes water.” Include its net equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. [1]", "Water is the main reaction product at use. [1]", "Hydrogen production and energy sourcing can create emissions and resource impacts. [1]", "Storage, transport and infrastructure also matter, so the absolute claim is unjustified. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A failed galvanic cell", "prompt": "A Zn/Cu galvanic cell is assembled with a wire but without a functioning salt bridge. Explain why sustained current fails, provide the electrode equations and recommend a suitable correction.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ at the anode. [1]", "Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu at the cathode. [1]", "Electrons initially move through the external circuit from Zn toward Cu. [1]", "Charge imbalance develops without a sustained ionic path. [1]", "Provide a functioning bridge/separator with an appropriate non-interfering electrolyte. [1]", "Anions support balance toward the anode and cations toward the cathode; electrons do not use the bridge. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Oxidation occurs at the", "o": ["Anode in every cell", "Cathode in every cell", "Salt bridge", "Negative terminal in every cell"], "a": 0, "why": "Anode/cathode definitions are based on reaction type."}, {"q": "Electrons normally move through the external wire", "o": ["Cathode to anode", "Anode to cathode", "Through the salt bridge first", "Only in electrolytic cells"], "a": 1, "why": "Oxidation supplies electrons and reduction consumes them."}, {"q": "In an electrolytic cell the cathode is", "o": ["Negative", "Positive", "Always neutral", "An oxidation site"], "a": 0, "why": "The supply delivers electrons to its negative-connected cathode."}, {"q": "The object in metal electroplating is", "o": ["The anode", "The cathode", "The salt bridge", "Necessarily dissolved entirely"], "a": 1, "why": "Reduction deposits metal on the cathode surface."}, {"q": "Sacrificial protection requires a metal that is", "o": ["More readily oxidised than the protected iron", "Always a noble metal", "Unable to conduct", "Unconnected to the iron"], "a": 0, "why": "Its oxidation supplies electrons that suppress iron oxidation."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-organic", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "title": "Organic structures, functional groups and reactions", "scope": "C1–C8 functional-group naming; structural and cis–trans isomers; intermolecular forces; oxidation, esterification and identification from data", "screens": [{"title": "Functional groups organise organic chemistry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Organic compounds contain carbon frameworks and may include functional groups that govern characteristic reactions. The course includes alkenes, alcohols, aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, esters, amines and amides. An alkene contains C=C. An alcohol has a hydroxy group bonded to an appropriate carbon. Aldehydes and ketones contain carbonyl C=O in different positions. Carboxylic acids contain –C(=O)OH. Esters contain –C(=O)O– linked to another carbon group. Amines have a nitrogen group, and amides contain –C(=O)N–. Identify the complete group, not a single oxygen or nitrogen atom in isolation.", "diagram": "functional-groups"}, {"title": "Reading condensed and displayed formulae", "kind": "learn", "text": "CH₃CH₂OH shows an O–H bond in ethanol, while CH₃OCH₃ represents an ether with different connectivity. CH₃CHO is an aldehyde, CH₃COCH₃ a ketone and CH₃COOH a carboxylic acid. In a displayed formula, show the carbonyl double bond explicitly. A condensed formula must still communicate which atoms attach where. Check carbon has four bond units, neutral oxygen usually two and neutral nitrogen commonly three plus a lone pair. A molecular formula alone cannot identify a functional group because different arrangements can share it. Draw an unfamiliar condensed structure before inferring its chemistry.", "diagram": "carbonyl"}, {"title": "Naming alcohols and alkenes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Choose the appropriate longest parent chain containing the principal group or double bond and apply its locant and suffix. Alcohol examples include propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol. Alkene examples include but-1-ene and 3-methylpent-1-ene. In simple single-functional-group course cases, number to give that group the appropriate lowest locant before ordinary alkyl branches. Show a locant where it is needed to distinguish positions. A name should reconstruct the exact connectivity. The SCSA scope includes parents up to eight carbons with simple branching and one listed functional group, so highly complex nomenclature is outside the core sequence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming aldehydes, ketones and acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "An aldehyde’s carbonyl carbon is terminal and part of the parent chain, as in ethanal CH₃CHO. Ketones use -one and a carbonyl locant, such as pentan-2-one CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃. Carboxylic acids use -oic acid, such as propanoic acid CH₃CH₂COOH, with the acid carbon counted in the chain. A carboxyl group is not simply an alcohol group plus an unrelated carbonyl: its combined structure gives acid chemistry. When drawing a named compound, count the carbonyl carbon among the parent carbons. Missing that atom produces the wrong homologous member.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming esters", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ester name has the alkyl group from the alcohol first, followed by the carboxylate-derived name from the acid. Ethyl ethanoate is CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃: the ethyl group is attached through oxygen and the ethanoate section includes the carbonyl carbon. Methyl propanoate is CH₃CH₂C(=O)OCH₃. Split the structure at the carbonyl–oxygen connection conceptually to identify the two parts. Do not reverse the acid and alcohol names. An ester is usually not an ionic ethanoate salt: its oxygen connects covalently to an organic group. Use complete structural formulae when demonstrating ester formation.", "diagram": "ester"}, {"title": "Amines and amides", "kind": "learn", "text": "An amine such as ethanamine CH₃CH₂NH₂ has nitrogen that can accept a proton through its lone pair. A simple primary amide such as ethanamide CH₃CONH₂ has nitrogen attached to a carbonyl carbon. In an amide, electron delocalisation involving the carbonyl changes nitrogen’s basic behaviour relative to an ordinary amine. Nitrogen-containing molecules can also form hydrogen bonds if appropriate N–H donors and acceptor sites are available. Naming may include positions and N-substitution where relevant; follow the simple structures and conventions specified. Recognising –CONH– rather than merely “contains N” is essential for peptide and polymer questions.", "diagram": "amine-amide"}, {"title": "Structural isomerism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chain isomers differ in carbon skeleton; position isomers differ in placement of a bond or functional group. Butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol share C₄H₁₀O and differ in O–H group position. Two different functional classes can also share molecular formula, so a formula alone may leave several possibilities. Draw every distinct connectivity, check valency and eliminate duplicate structures related only by rotation of the drawing. Give the requested isomer type rather than calling every difference cis–trans. Physical properties can differ because the structures affect forces, shape and packing, even when molecular mass is identical.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cis–trans isomerism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rotation around C=C is restricted. Geometric isomerism is possible when each double-bonded carbon has two different attached groups. In but-2-ene, the methyl groups can be on the same side, cis, or opposite sides, trans. But-1-ene does not meet the condition because its terminal double-bond carbon has two hydrogens. A flat cis–trans drawing represents the approximate planar region around the double bond. Reorienting the entire diagram does not change the isomer. Geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangement and can have different properties. Do not count free rotation about an ordinary single bond as a new cis–trans isomer.", "diagram": "cis-trans"}, {"title": "Functional groups and physical properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Boiling point depends on the combined intermolecular attractions and molecular structure. Alcohols and carboxylic acids can donate and accept hydrogen bonds. Aldehydes, ketones and esters have polar carbonyl groups and can accept hydrogen bonds from water but lack O–H donor groups in the simple examples. Primary amides can form strong hydrogen-bonding networks. Dispersion occurs in all. A larger hydrocarbon region can reduce water solubility even when the functional group interacts with water. Compare molecules of similar size when isolating group effects and avoid assuming one named force universally dominates every comparison.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols", "kind": "learn", "text": "Classify the carbon bearing OH by how many other carbons attach to it. A primary alcohol usually has one, a secondary two and a tertiary three; methanol is handled as the simplest primary oxidation case. Propan-1-ol is primary, propan-2-ol secondary and 2-methylpropan-2-ol tertiary. Classification is not determined by total molecular carbon count or OH locant alone. Draw the OH carbon and its neighbours. Under common course oxidising conditions, primary alcohols can yield aldehydes then acids, secondary alcohols yield ketones and tertiary alcohols do not undergo the same simple oxidation without C–C cleavage.", "diagram": "alcohol-classes"}, {"title": "Oxidising alcohols: products and conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Controlled primary-alcohol oxidation can give an aldehyde, often removed by distillation to limit further oxidation. With excess oxidant under reflux, a carboxylic acid can form. Secondary alcohols form ketones. A shorthand [O] equation tracks organic transformation: CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O, followed by CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH. This shorthand is not a full ionic redox equation. Where asked, balance oxidation and oxidant reduction half-equations explicitly. Conditions and oxidation-agent observations form part of the chemical answer rather than being assumed.", "diagram": "alcohol-oxidation"}, {"title": "Oxidant colours and full equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acidified dichromate changes from orange toward green as Cr(VI) reduces to Cr(III); acidified permanganate loses its purple colour as Mn(VII) reduces to Mn(II). These changes show oxidant consumption but alone may not uniquely identify the organic reactant. Ethanol-to-ethanal oxidation can be written CH₃CH₂OH → CH₃CHO + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻. Ethanol-to-ethanoic-acid oxidation is CH₃CH₂OH + H₂O → CH₃COOH + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. Combine with the appropriate acidic reduction half-equation and cancel species. Make sure your final equation accounts for the oxygen incorporated into the product.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Esterification and reversible condensation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A carboxylic acid and alcohol can react to form an ester and water, usually with acid catalysis and heating. CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O produces ethyl ethanoate. It is a condensation because a small molecule forms as the organic reactants join. The reaction is reversible, so yield can improve by using excess reagent or removing a product under appropriate conditions. A catalyst speeds approach to equilibrium without changing Kc. In a structural model, identify which acid OH and alcohol H contribute to water and trace the retained atoms into the ester.", "diagram": "esterification"}, {"title": "Acids, amines and functional tests", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carboxylic acids react with hydroxide to form carboxylate and water, and with carbonate to release CO₂. Amines accept H⁺ to form ammonium-type ions. An alkene can decolourise bromine through addition, while an alcohol may consume a suitable oxidant depending on its class. Use a set of tests to distinguish candidates. An aldehyde can also oxidise, so oxidant colour change is not unique to alcohols. A carbonate effervescence test supports an acidic group but must be interpreted within the candidate list. State reagent, conditions, observation and a chemical explanation rather than treating a single colour as a complete identification.", "diagram": "organic-tests"}, {"title": "Empirical and molecular formulae", "kind": "learn", "text": "An empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number atom ratio. Convert each element’s mass or percentage into moles, divide by the smallest and scale near-simple fractional ratios to integers. For a molecular formula, divide measured molar mass by empirical-formula mass and multiply all subscripts by the resulting whole number. Do not round 1.50 to 2 prematurely; multiply the entire ratio by two. Combustion data give carbon from CO₂ and hydrogen from H₂O, with oxygen often found by mass difference when appropriate. State assumptions about composition and completeness. A formula does not establish one unique structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Identifying structure from combined evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Start with molecular formula or empirical data, use reactions to narrow functional groups and then consider isomer candidates. A C₃H₆O compound that oxidises readily may be propanal rather than propanone under suitable conditions. Boiling/solubility evidence can support, but not always uniquely determine, an assignment. If analytical spectra or other secondary data are supplied, interpret the stated features and avoid inventing an unstated peak rule. SCSA requires structure inferred from chemical reactions and other analytical data; advanced standalone instrumental theory is not assumed here. Clearly state when several structures remain consistent with the evidence.", "diagram": "structure-evidence"}, {"title": "A naming reference with complete examples", "kind": "learn", "text": "Examples within the simple course scope include ethanol CH₃CH₂OH; propanal CH₃CH₂CHO; butan-2-one CH₃COCH₂CH₃; pentanoic acid CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH; methyl butanoate CH₃CH₂CH₂COOCH₃; propan-1-amine CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂; and butanamide CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂. Each carbonyl carbon belongs to its parent chain. Amines can be named with an appropriate locant where positional distinction matters. A displayed formula must connect bonds to the correct atoms and show the requested hydrogens. These condensed examples are naming aids rather than substitutes for every required drawn representation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solubility: explicit group and chain reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Methanol can donate and accept hydrogen bonds with water and has a small non-polar carbon region. A long-chain carboxylic acid still has a polar hydrogen-bonding group, but its large non-polar hydrocarbon region makes favourable water interaction less extensive relative to the molecule’s size. Thus a functional-group label alone does not guarantee high water solubility. Explain the actual solute–water forces and the chain’s effect, not merely “polar dissolves in polar.” Dissolution competes with solute–solute and water–water attraction and includes entropy effects, so data strengthen a comparison.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Naming an ester", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Naming an ester", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Isomer requirement", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Isomer requirement", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Alcohol oxidation pathway", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Alcohol oxidation pathway", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Composition to formula", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Composition to formula", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Functional-group discrimination", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Ester equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: A synthesis identification problem", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Naming an ester", "prompt": "Name CH₃CH₂C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ and identify its acid and alcohol precursors.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The acid-derived part is propanoate. [1]", "The oxygen-linked alkyl group is ethyl: ethyl propanoate. [1]", "Precursors are propanoic acid and ethanol. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Isomer requirement", "prompt": "Explain why but-2-ene has cis–trans isomers while but-1-ene does not.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["C=C restricts rotation. [1]", "Each but-2-ene double-bond carbon has H and CH₃ as different groups. [1]", "These allow same-side and opposite-side methyl arrangements. [1]", "But-1-ene has two H on one double-bond carbon, preventing distinct cis–trans arrangements. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Alcohol oxidation pathway", "prompt": "Give products of controlled oxidation and further oxidation of ethanol, with condensed equations and an oxidant observation.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Controlled product: ethanal. [1]", "CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O. [1]", "Further product: ethanoic acid; CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH. [1]", "Acidified dichromate changes orange to green, or permanganate loses purple under the stated acidic conditions. [1]", "Distillation can remove aldehyde, while excess oxidant/reflux favours the acid. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Composition to formula", "prompt": "A compound is 40.0% C, 6.71% H and 53.3% O by mass. Use C 12.01, H 1.008 and O 16.00. Its molar mass is approximately 60.05 g mol⁻¹. Determine empirical and molecular formulae.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Take 100 g: C moles 40.0/12.01 ≈ 3.33; H 6.71/1.008 ≈ 6.66; O 53.3/16.00 ≈ 3.33. [1]", "Divide by approximately 3.33 to get 1:2:1. [1]", "Empirical formula CH₂O, mass ≈ 30.03 g mol⁻¹. [1]", "Molecular multiplier = 60.05/30.03 ≈ 2. [1]", "Molecular formula C₂H₄O₂. Multiple structures may share it. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Functional-group discrimination", "prompt": "Two candidates are propanal and propanone. Explain a suitable oxidising test to distinguish them, including one limitation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Use an appropriate controlled aldehyde oxidation test or suitable oxidant under stated conditions. [1]", "Propanal is oxidised toward propanoic acid. [1]", "Propanone does not undergo the same easy oxidation under mild course conditions. [1]", "Other oxidisable impurities or harsher conditions can interfere, so control conditions and compare references. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ester equilibrium", "prompt": "Write the equation for ethanol and ethanoic acid and explain how using excess ethanol differs from adding more catalyst.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O. [2: reactant/product structures and atom balance]", "Excess ethanol favours net ester formation and can increase equilibrium yield. [1]", "A catalyst supplies a faster pathway. [1]", "It does not change equilibrium composition at fixed temperature. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q6", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A synthesis identification problem", "prompt": "An unknown C₃H₈O compound oxidises to a ketone. Identify a likely structure, give the oxidation product, and explain how its hydrogen bonding differs from the product.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["The likely alcohol is propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃. [2: identity and structure]", "It is secondary and oxidises to propanone, CH₃COCH₃. [1]", "CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₃ + H₂O. [1]", "The alcohol can donate and accept hydrogen bonds. [1]", "The ketone accepts from water but lacks a donor for ordinary self hydrogen bonding. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q7", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "CH₃COCH₃ contains", "o": ["A ketone", "An aldehyde", "An ester", "An amine"], "a": 0, "why": "The carbonyl carbon is bonded to two carbon groups."}, {"q": "The alcohol part of ethyl ethanoate supplies", "o": ["Ethyl attached through O", "The carbonyl carbon", "Every oxygen atom", "The ethanoate carbon skeleton"], "a": 0, "why": "The ester oxygen links to the alcohol-derived ethyl group."}, {"q": "A secondary alcohol usually oxidises to", "o": ["A ketone", "An alkane directly", "An amide", "An ester without another reagent"], "a": 0, "why": "The OH-bearing carbon has two carbon neighbours and forms a ketone carbonyl."}, {"q": "An empirical formula represents", "o": ["A unique structure", "The simplest whole-number atom ratio", "Only ionic charges", "The full molecule necessarily"], "a": 1, "why": "A molecular formula may be an integer multiple of it."}, {"q": "Tertiary alcohols under typical mild oxidation conditions", "o": ["Always become aldehydes", "Do not undergo the simple primary/secondary oxidation pathway", "Lose one carbon automatically", "Always form acids"], "a": 1, "why": "The OH-bearing carbon lacks the hydrogen needed for that simple transformation."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "30 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "title": "Polymers, amino acids and protein models", "scope": "Addition and condensation repeat units; monomer recovery; plastic structure/properties; amino acid zwitterions; peptide bonds and protein organisation", "screens": [{"title": "Monomers, polymers and repeat units", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polymer contains many repeating structural units linked into long molecules. A monomer is a molecule that can be used to make the polymer. A repeat unit shows the smallest or a convenient recurring structural pattern; it is not necessarily identical in formula to an intact monomer, especially in condensation polymerisation. Brackets and a subscript n indicate repetition, with bonds extending through the brackets. A drawing of three units represents a segment, not the exact full chain length. Polymer samples commonly contain a distribution of molecular lengths. Distinguish the covalent backbone within a chain from intermolecular attractions between chains.", "diagram": "polymer"}, {"title": "Addition polymerisation of ethene", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethene monomers join when the C=C double bond is converted into single-bond connections in the chain. nCH₂=CH₂ → [–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ represents polyethene. No small molecule is eliminated in this simplified addition model. Carbon atoms retain four bond units: the former double bond becomes single while each carbon gains a backbone connection. Bonds must extend beyond both sides of the repeat-unit brackets. Do not leave C=C in a conventional polyethene backbone or show unconnected CH₂ groups. Initiation and detailed mechanism are not represented by the simple overall equation.", "diagram": "addition-polymer"}, {"title": "PTFE and substituted addition monomers", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tetrafluoroethene CF₂=CF₂ gives poly(tetrafluoroethene), [–CF₂–CF₂–]ₙ. Propene gives [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ: the methyl group remains a side group rather than becoming an extra backbone carbon. To recover an alkene monomer from an addition repeat unit, identify the two backbone carbons formed from its double bond and reconnect them as C=C while retaining attached groups. More complex copolymers need additional information. PTFE’s chemical resistance and surface properties arise from its fluorinated structure and material organisation; they are not explained merely by calling it “strong plastic.”", "diagram": "ptfe"}, {"title": "Condensation polymerisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Condensation joins monomers with complementary reactive groups and releases small molecules. A diol and dicarboxylic acid can form ester links, producing a polyester and water. A diamine and dicarboxylic acid can form amide links, producing a polyamide and water. For long chains, monomers need appropriate functionality to continue growth at both ends. Repeat-unit drawings commonly omit end groups, so exact water counts for a finite chain can differ from a simplified large-n representation. Identify the linkage and the atoms removed, and do not copy an addition-polymer method that merely opens a double bond.", "diagram": "condensation-polymer"}, {"title": "Polyester repeat units and PET", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polyester contains –C(=O)O– links in its backbone. PET can be formed from ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid in a simplified condensation model. Its repeating backbone includes –OCH₂CH₂O– and –C(=O)C₆H₄C(=O)– sections joined as esters. Aromatic rings and carbonyl-containing segments influence rigidity and interactions. PET is used in fibres and containers, while actual performance depends on processing, crystallinity and additives. A schematic aromatic ring must retain its two opposite attachment points in this example. Draw both monomer structures and the resulting links rather than naming PET without showing chemical relationships.", "diagram": "pet"}, {"title": "Polyamides and nylon", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polyamide has –C(=O)NH– links in its backbone. A dicarboxylic acid and diamine can form a nylon-type polymer. N–H groups can donate hydrogen bonds to carbonyl oxygens on neighbouring chains, contributing to strength and melting behaviour. To recover monomers, cut the amide link conceptually and restore OH on the carbonyl section and H on the nitrogen section for the acid/amine condensation route. Account for functionality at both ends. Nylon is a family of polyamides, not a single universal chain. Use the provided repeat unit to identify the actual monomers in a question.", "diagram": "nylon"}, {"title": "Polymer structures and material properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Longer chains can have more entanglement and more total intermolecular contact. Branching can reduce close packing, affecting density, crystallinity and flexibility. Cross-links connect different chains covalently and restrict motion. Stronger interchain attractions can increase resistance to separation or deformation. These features interact, so one-factor explanations require matched comparisons. Polyethene is non-polar and mainly experiences dispersion between chains, while nylon can hydrogen bond. Thermal and mechanical properties also depend on additives and processing. Link each stated property to a specific structural feature and identify whether the force is covalent within/across chains or intermolecular.", "diagram": "polymer-structure"}, {"title": "Thermoplastics, cross-linking and evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Many polymers with separate chains can soften when heated because chains gain mobility without destroying every covalent backbone. Extensive cross-linking can prevent simple remelting; sufficiently high temperature may instead cause decomposition. This helps explain different reuse and recycling pathways but does not guarantee any labelled plastic is easy to recycle locally. Evaluate collection, separation, contamination, material degradation and processing energy. “Biodegradable” needs conditions and timescale, not an assumption of harmless disappearance anywhere. The chemistry supports informed choices, while economic and infrastructure factors shape practical outcomes. Use evidence rather than equating all polymers with one environmental effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "General structure of an α-amino acid", "kind": "learn", "text": "An α-amino acid has amino and carboxyl groups attached to the same central α carbon, commonly represented H₂N–CH(R)–COOH. R is a side chain determining identity and influencing properties. Glycine has R = H, while alanine has R = CH₃. The general diagram is a chemical structure, not a complete three-dimensional protein model. Many amino acids have ionisable or polar side chains that affect behaviour, so use the SCSA amino-acid data pages when given specific examples. Count functional groups and identify where peptide links form rather than assuming side chains are part of every backbone bond.", "diagram": "amino-acid"}, {"title": "Zwitterions and pH-dependent charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a common aqueous form, the amino group is protonated and the carboxyl group deprotonated, giving ⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COO⁻. This zwitterion contains both positive and negative formal charges with zero net charge for a simple non-ionising side chain. It is not an uncharged structure with no charged sites. At low pH, a carboxylate can gain H⁺, tending toward a net positive form. At high pH, ammonium can lose H⁺, tending toward a net negative form. Side-chain ionisation can alter net charge. Always specify the amino acid and pH context rather than claiming every amino acid has net zero charge in every solution.", "diagram": "zwitterion"}, {"title": "Peptide bond formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Two α-amino acids join through condensation between a carboxyl group and an amino group, forming a –C(=O)NH– peptide link and water. A dipeptide retains an amino end and carboxyl end and contains two side chains. The peptide bond is an amide bond. H₂NCH(R₁)COOH + H₂NCH(R₂)COOH → H₂NCH(R₁)CONHCH(R₂)COOH + H₂O is a simplified neutral-form equation. Joining the same two different amino acids in reverse sequence can give a different dipeptide. Identify the linkage atoms explicitly and distinguish it from an intermolecular hydrogen bond.", "diagram": "peptide"}, {"title": "Primary protein structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence connected by peptide bonds. Sequence determines which side chains occur and where they are positioned along the polypeptide. Changing one residue can affect later folding or function, although consequences depend on its position and chemical character. A chain drawn as beads is useful for sequence but hides the actual amide backbone and side-chain chemistry. The N and C ends give the chain direction. Do not define primary structure as the first shape formed or as a collection of hydrogen bonds. It is the covalent order of the amino-acid residues.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Secondary structure: helix and sheet", "kind": "learn", "text": "Secondary structures include α-helices and β-pleated sheets stabilised by hydrogen bonding involving backbone amide N–H and carbonyl O groups. In an α-helix, interactions occur along different positions of the same chain in the characteristic helical pattern. β-sheet hydrogen bonds connect adjacent aligned strands, which may belong to separate chains or folded regions of one chain. Side chains project away from the backbone arrangements. A ribbon model simplifies many atoms to reveal recurring shape; it does not show all bonds. Hydrogen bonds stabilise secondary organisation, while covalent peptide bonds maintain the backbone itself.", "diagram": "protein-secondary"}, {"title": "Tertiary structure and side-chain interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional folding of a polypeptide. Side-chain interactions can include hydrogen bonding, dipole–dipole and dispersion attractions, ionic interactions and covalent disulfide bridges between suitable cysteine residues. Hydrophobic effects favour clustering of non-polar regions away from water in many proteins. A disulfide bridge is stronger covalent linking and should not be labelled a hydrogen bond. The resulting shape influences access to binding or active sites. Protein structures are not static sculptures; molecular motion remains possible. Match each depicted interaction to the actual side-chain chemistry supplied.", "diagram": "protein-tertiary"}, {"title": "Denaturation, function and shared structural evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat or unsuitable pH can disrupt folding and interactions, changing a protein’s function. Ordinary denaturation need not break every peptide bond, so primary sequence may remain while secondary/tertiary structure changes. Enzyme activity depends on a suitable shape and chemical environment at the active site. The Protein Data Bank provides shared structural evidence that scientists contribute to and use internationally. Experimental models have resolution and uncertainty limits; a ribbon or space-filling display emphasises different features. Explain how structure relates to function and which evidence supports a claim, rather than assuming every loss of activity is permanent chemical destruction of the entire molecule.", "diagram": "denaturation"}, {"title": "Zwitterions and melting behaviour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Amino acids commonly exist with opposite charged sites in a solid zwitterionic arrangement. Strong electrostatic interactions between sites can contribute to relatively high melting/decomposition behaviour compared with similarly sized simple molecular substances. A net-zero zwitterion still has full formal charges, so “non-polar because zero total charge” is an incorrect inference. Hydrogen bonding can also contribute. In a comparison, identify the relevant solid form and interactions and acknowledge that some amino acids decompose before simple melting. Compare provided data rather than assuming every amino acid has one universal melting point.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recovering monomers from polyester links", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a polyester segment containing –O–R–O–C(=O)–R′–C(=O)–, identify the diol as HO–R–OH and the dicarboxylic acid as HOOC–R′–COOH in the acid/diol condensation model. Restore H to oxygen and OH to the carbonyl section when conceptually hydrolysing each ester link. Count atoms carefully and preserve aromatic attachment positions if present. For a polyamide, restore amine and carboxylic acid groups instead. A polymer can sometimes be made by another synthetic route, so infer the requested condensation monomers from the supplied linkage and question context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Addition repeat unit", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Addition repeat unit", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Condensation monomer recovery", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Condensation monomer recovery", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: A dipeptide structure", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: A dipeptide structure", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Zwitterion interpretation", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Zwitterion interpretation", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Protein levels and denaturation", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: A nylon–polyethene comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Interpreting a protein model", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Addition repeat unit", "prompt": "Draw the repeat unit from CH₂=CHCH₃ and explain what changes at the double bond.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Repeat unit [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ with bonds extending through brackets. [2]", "The C=C becomes C–C single bonding in the backbone. [1]", "New links join units without elimination of a small molecule in the addition model. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q1", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Condensation monomer recovery", "prompt": "A polymer segment is –NH(CH₂)₆NHC(=O)(CH₂)₄C(=O)–. Identify the two condensation monomers and linkage type.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Diamine H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂. [1]", "Dicarboxylic acid HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH. [1]", "The polymer contains amide links and is a polyamide. [1]", "Acid/amine joining releases water in the simplified condensation route. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A dipeptide structure", "prompt": "Write a neutral-form condensed structure for glycine followed by alanine and identify the peptide link.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Glycine has R = H and alanine R = CH₃. [1]", "H₂NCH₂C(=O)NHCH(CH₃)COOH. [2: sequence/connectivity and retained terminal groups]", "The peptide link is –C(=O)NH–; one water forms on joining. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q3", "allocations": [1, 2, 1]}, {"title": "Zwitterion interpretation", "prompt": "Draw alanine’s simple zwitterion and state what happens to its main groups in strongly acidic solution.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["⁺H₃N–CH(CH₃)–COO⁻. [2: both correct charged sites and connectivity]", "It has zero net charge but charged internal groups in this form. [1]", "Acid can protonate COO⁻ to COOH, yielding a net-positive form with NH₃⁺ retained. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q4", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Protein levels and denaturation", "prompt": "Distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary structure and explain why denaturation need not destroy primary structure.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Primary is amino-acid sequence joined by peptide bonds. [1]", "Secondary is recurring helix/sheet organisation supported by backbone hydrogen bonds. [1]", "Tertiary is overall three-dimensional folding involving side-chain interactions. [1]", "Heat/pH can disrupt those stabilising interactions and folding. [1]", "Peptide bonds may remain intact, retaining the primary sequence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A nylon–polyethene comparison", "prompt": "Explain why nylon and polyethene can differ in interchain attraction and relate this to a material property. State one comparison limitation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Nylon contains amide N–H donors and carbonyl acceptors. [1]", "It can hydrogen bond between chains, while polyethene mainly has dispersion interactions. [1]", "Stronger interchain attraction can increase resistance to separation or deformation under matched conditions. [1]", "Chain length, packing, crystallinity, cross-links and processing also affect properties. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Interpreting a protein model", "prompt": "A ribbon model shows a helix, sheet and a link between two cysteine side chains. Identify the structural levels and interactions shown, then explain one limitation of using this image to infer function.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Helix and sheet are secondary structures. [1]", "Backbone N–H/carbonyl hydrogen bonds stabilise them. [1]", "The overall folded arrangement is tertiary structure. [1]", "The cysteine link can be a covalent disulfide bridge. [1]", "Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence, not fully visible from a simple ribbon alone. [1]", "A ribbon omits atom details, dynamics and experimental uncertainty; function needs additional structural/chemical evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Addition polymerisation normally", "o": ["Eliminates water from each alkene", "Consumes C=C to link the backbone", "Leaves every C=C unchanged", "Requires amino acids"], "a": 1, "why": "The alkene double bond becomes backbone single connections."}, {"q": "A peptide bond is a type of", "o": ["Ester", "Amide", "Ionic pair only", "Metallic bond"], "a": 1, "why": "Its linkage is –C(=O)NH–."}, {"q": "A zwitterion can have", "o": ["No charges anywhere", "Both positive and negative groups", "Only positive groups", "No functional groups"], "a": 1, "why": "A simple amino-acid zwitterion has opposite formal charges and zero net charge."}, {"q": "Protein primary structure is", "o": ["Overall folding", "Amino-acid sequence", "Only an α-helix", "Every hydrogen bond"], "a": 1, "why": "Sequence is the covalent order of residues."}, {"q": "A disulfide bridge is", "o": ["A covalent interaction", "A hydrogen bond", "A free electron", "An ester link"], "a": 0, "why": "It is a covalent S–S link between suitable residues."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "29 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}, {"id": "che12-synthesis", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "title": "Chemical synthesis, industrial chemistry and surfactants", "scope": "Limiting reagent and yield; Haber/Contact processes; ethanol and biodiesel pathways; enzymes; saponification; soaps, detergents and hard water; green chemistry", "screens": [{"title": "Synthesis pathways and design choices", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemical synthesis constructs a desired product through one or more reactions. A useful plan identifies starting materials, functional-group transformations, reagents, conditions and separation steps. Selecting a pathway involves yield, rate, selectivity, energy use, hazards, feedstock availability and waste. High percentage yield alone does not establish a sustainable process. A series of steps multiplies losses: two 80% yield stages give 64% overall yield before other losses. Relate chosen conditions to the chemistry rather than treating an industrial flowsheet as a set of boxes to memorise. A reaction equation describes chemical change but not every engineering operation.", "diagram": "synthesis-path"}, {"title": "Theoretical amount and limiting reagent", "kind": "learn", "text": "For each reactant, calculate moles and divide by its balanced coefficient. The smaller value limits reaction extent. Theoretical product = limiting extent × product coefficient. If some reactant is impure, calculate the reactive portion first. If a solution is used, obtain moles from concentration and volume. A yield calculation using the non-limiting reagent gives an incorrect denominator even when arithmetic is accurate. State which reagent limits and how much excess remains when asked. This method applies equally to gas synthesis, esterification and supplied biodiesel equations, provided the reaction and assumptions are clear.", "diagram": "limiting"}, {"title": "Percentage yield and practical losses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Percentage yield = actual product amount / theoretical product amount × 100, using comparable units for the same substance. Low yield can result from incomplete conversion, equilibrium limitation, competing reactions or losses during isolation. Distinguish reaction conversion from isolated yield: complete reactant consumption can still give low target-product yield if side products form. An apparent yield above 100% may reflect retained solvent, water or impurity. Report purity assumptions. Improving separation can increase isolated yield without changing Kc, whereas removing a product during a reversible reaction can also affect composition. Diagnose a cause before proposing an improvement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rate and yield are distinct goals", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fast reaction need not have a high equilibrium product fraction, and a product-favoured reaction can be too slow for production. Temperature often improves rate but may reduce equilibrium yield for an exothermic forward reaction. Catalysts increase rate without directly changing equilibrium composition. Pressure, reagent ratios and product removal can change equilibrium extent under suitable conditions. A practical optimum balances throughput, equipment costs and energy with yield and safety. When a question asks “explain the chosen conditions,” address both kinetic and equilibrium consequences and name the actual reaction rather than giving generic high-temperature statements.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Haber process: the chemical equilibrium", "kind": "learn", "text": "N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) is exothermic. Higher pressure favours ammonia because the product side has fewer gas moles. Lower temperature favours ammonia equilibrium fraction, while higher temperature improves rate. An iron-based catalyst supplies a more accessible pathway. Industrial conditions commonly use an elevated-temperature compromise and high pressure, with actual values depending on plant design. Treat approximate example conditions as illustrative rather than a universal plant specification. Condensing ammonia removes product and unreacted gases can be recycled, increasing overall utilisation beyond a single-pass equilibrium conversion.", "diagram": "haber"}, {"title": "Explaining Haber compromises", "kind": "learn", "text": "Very low temperature can give favourable equilibrium composition but unacceptable rate. Very high pressure can improve equilibrium yield but requires stronger equipment and more compression energy. A catalyst allows viable rates under a chosen compromise but does not move the equilibrium by itself. Product separation and recycling reduce waste and improve feedstock use. A complete exam evaluation links each condition to a separate chemical and practical reason, then gives a reasoned recommendation. Do not describe recycling as increasing Kc. Kc depends on temperature; the flowsheet changes the material handled and allows repeated passes. Hydrogen sourcing also matters to life-cycle emissions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Contact process: the equilibrium stage", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Contact process includes sulfur oxidation to SO₂ and catalytic conversion 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), which is exothermic. Lower temperature favours SO₃, while a moderate elevated temperature gives a useful rate with a vanadium(V)-oxide-based catalyst. Higher pressure favours fewer gas moles but practical gains and costs must be assessed; many implementations do not use the extreme pressure typical of Haber. Specific numerical conditions vary. Explain reaction stoichiometry and enthalpy before discussing the compromise. A catalyst changes the rate, not equilibrium yield at fixed conditions.", "diagram": "contact"}, {"title": "Sulfuric acid production and absorption", "kind": "learn", "text": "SO₃ is commonly absorbed into concentrated sulfuric acid to form oleum, which is then carefully reacted with water to obtain the desired acid concentration. Direct SO₃ addition to water is difficult to control and can generate an acid mist rather than a readily collected product. A simplified model is SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇, followed by H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄. These equations show chemical relationships, not a student procedure. Compare feedstock purification, catalyst protection, absorption, energy recovery and emission control when evaluating the full process. The equilibrium stage is only one part of industrial synthesis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ethanol by ethene hydration", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethene can react with steam: CH₂=CH₂(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CH₃CH₂OH(g). Acid catalysis and elevated temperature/pressure support production, with recovery and recycling of unreacted feedstock. The forward reaction is exothermic and reduces gas mole count in the written model, so equilibrium favours lower temperature and higher pressure while rate needs a compromise. The process can be continuous and gives a relatively defined chemical feedstock path. Evaluate the source of ethene, energy and separation requirements. A route based on fossil-derived ethene and one using bio-derived feedstock need not have identical environmental profiles.", "diagram": "ethanol-path"}, {"title": "Ethanol by fermentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simplified fermentation equation is C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Enzymes in microorganisms catalyse conversion under suitable relatively mild conditions, with oxygen availability and temperature controlled for the desired process. Ethanol accumulation and other conditions can limit biological performance. Product separation commonly requires distillation and energy input. Fermentation can use renewable feedstocks, but land use, fertiliser, water, transport and competing food uses also matter. Compare this with hydration through actual process features: biological specificity and mild conditions versus throughput, feedstock, conversion and purification. “Natural” is not itself a chemical proof of sustainability.", "diagram": "fermentation"}, {"title": "Linking ethanol to an ester product", "kind": "learn", "text": "A multi-step route can hydrate ethene to ethanol, then react ethanol with ethanoic acid to form ethyl ethanoate. State the two equations and conditions separately. The final esterification is reversible and may use excess reactant or suitable product removal to improve yield. If the first stage has 75% yield and the second 80%, the idealised overall yield relative to the initial stoichiometric potential is 0.75 × 0.80 = 60%, assuming compatible amount ratios. Keep track of which substance limits each stage. A common error applies the second percentage to the original feedstock instead of to the intermediate actually available.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Triglycerides and fatty acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "A triglyceride contains glycerol joined through three ester bonds to fatty-acid-derived chains. The chains may differ and may contain C=C bonds. A simplified formula represents the three acyl groups as RCO–, but R is not always identical in a real fat. An ester linkage is –C(=O)O–. The hydrocarbon tails contribute non-polar character, while ester groups contain polar bonds. Hydrolysis or transesterification changes the ester connections. Distinguish triglycerides from free fatty acids and from soaps, which are fatty-acid salts. A model should label glycerol-derived backbone, carbonyl groups and hydrocarbon chains.", "diagram": "triglyceride"}, {"title": "Biodiesel by transesterification", "kind": "learn", "text": "A triglyceride reacts with an alcohol, commonly methanol, to give three fatty-acid methyl esters and glycerol in the simplified stoichiometry. The methyl esters are biodiesel components. This is transesterification: the ester’s alcohol-derived part changes. Base catalysis can give a fast economical process but feedstock water and free fatty acids can cause difficulties including soap formation. Excess alcohol can favour conversion, and product separation/recovery matters. A base catalyst is not itself the source of every atom in the product. The environmental assessment depends on oil source, land use and processing, not only its biological origin.", "diagram": "biodiesel"}, {"title": "Lipase-catalysed biodiesel", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lipases are enzyme catalysts that can support biodiesel production under milder conditions and may tolerate some feedstocks differently from a base process. Their protein structure governs activity, so temperature, pH or solvent effects can alter performance. Cost, stability, reaction time, reuse and separation influence feasibility. An immobilised enzyme may be recovered and used repeatedly, but activity can decline. Compare a base and lipase route through specific evidence rather than declaring the enzyme automatically superior. A catalyst improves a pathway’s rate; final yield still depends on reaction conditions, equilibrium, side reactions and isolation.", "diagram": "enzyme"}, {"title": "Saponification: making soap", "kind": "learn", "text": "Base hydrolysis of a triglyceride produces glycerol and carboxylate salts. With sodium hydroxide, three ester links require three hydroxide amounts in the simplified equation: triglyceride + 3NaOH → glycerol + 3RCOO⁻Na⁺. These salts of long-chain fatty acids are soaps. Saponification differs from methanol transesterification that produces biodiesel esters. Identify which bond breaks and which products form. The carboxylate head is ionic, while the long hydrocarbon tail is non-polar. Write a general structure with an explained R group rather than inventing one exact fatty acid for every oil.", "diagram": "saponification"}, {"title": "Soap, detergent and micelles", "kind": "learn", "text": "A soap molecule has a non-polar hydrocarbon tail and charged carboxylate head. In water, suitable aggregates can place tails inward around oil and heads outward interacting with water. Agitation helps disperse oily material so it can be carried away. Anionic detergents derived from dodecylbenzene have a long non-polar region and a sulfonate head. A micelle drawing represents an aggregate arrangement, not a single molecule changing into water. Cleaning involves interfaces, emulsification and transport as well as molecular attraction. Use hydrophobic/hydrophilic descriptions with the actual structural groups identified.", "diagram": "micelle"}, {"title": "Hard water and calcium salts", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hard water contains appreciable calcium and/or magnesium ions. Calcium can form poorly soluble salts of soap carboxylates: Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s). This consumes useful soap and forms scum, reducing cleaning effectiveness. Many corresponding sulfonate detergent calcium salts are more soluble, so the detergent can perform better in hard water. Use the solubility difference rather than claiming detergents contain no charged groups. The extent depends on formulation and conditions. A lather observation is not a direct precise measure of every hardness ion without calibration and controlled sample amounts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Green chemistry and justified decisions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A synthesis evaluation compares atom use, unwanted products, solvent/reagent hazards, energy, renewable feedstocks, purification and catalyst recovery. Atom economy, when given or used as extension, concerns the fraction of stoichiometric reactant mass retained in target product; percentage yield concerns actual product relative to theoretical product. They are distinct. An enzyme route may reduce temperature but increase catalyst cost or solvent demands. A high-yield route can still generate harmful waste. Conclude using the supplied evidence and acknowledge trade-offs. Recommendations should be specific to the process rather than broad claims that one category of chemicals is always green.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Forces in cleaning: a complete explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The non-polar soap tail experiences dispersion interactions with the oily material. The charged carboxylate head interacts with polar water through ion–dipole attraction. Suitable aggregates orient tails toward oil and heads toward water, helping disperse droplets so agitation and rinsing can carry them away. Tail-to-water “hydrogen bonding” is not the mechanism. The sulfonate head of an anionic detergent is also charged and water-interacting. Include a labelled diagram and name each region’s force when asked. Hard-water precipitation is a separate chemical consumption of soap that can reduce the amount available for this interfacial action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Specific links in biodiesel and soap products", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an acyl chain R, a methyl ester is R–C(=O)OCH₃, while a soap anion is R–C(=O)O⁻ with a counterion such as Na⁺. The methyl ester is a neutral molecular product; the carboxylate salt contains ionic species. In transesterification, alcohol-derived connections exchange and glycerol is released. In base hydrolysis, carboxylate formation changes the acid-derived end into its salt. A diagram of a triglyceride must connect its three glycerol carbons by C–C bonds, not show three separate ester molecules. Real feedstocks contain a mixture of chains, so one R model represents a class of products.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked question: Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Haber conditions", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Haber conditions", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Fermentation yield", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Fermentation yield", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Hard-water mechanism", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Hard-water mechanism", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Two-stage synthesis", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Biodiesel versus soap", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Industrial recommendation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 7 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "prompt": "For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, start with 0.500 mol N₂ and 1.20 mol H₂. Actual NH₃ collected is 0.600 mol. Find limiting reagent, theoretical amount and yield.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n/coefficient: N₂ = 0.500; H₂ = 1.20/3 = 0.400. [1]", "H₂ limits. [1]", "Theoretical NH₃ = 2 × 0.400 = 0.800 mol. [1]", "Yield = 0.600/0.800 × 100. [1]", "Yield = 75.0%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Haber conditions", "prompt": "Explain why a Haber plant uses a temperature compromise, elevated pressure, a catalyst and recycling.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Lower temperature favours exothermic ammonia equilibrium yield. [1]", "Higher temperature increases rate, so a compromise is chosen. [1]", "Higher pressure favours the two-gas-mole product side over four reactant moles. [1]", "Compression and strong equipment cost energy/money. [1]", "A catalyst improves rate without changing Kc at fixed temperature. [1]", "Removing ammonia and recycling reactants improves overall utilisation. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Fermentation yield", "prompt": "Calculate theoretical ethanol mass from 18.0 g glucose using C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, M(glucose) = 180 and M(ethanol) = 46.0 g mol⁻¹. Actual mass is 7.36 g. Find yield.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(glucose) = 18.0/180 = 0.100 mol. [1]", "n(ethanol) theoretical = 0.200 mol. [1]", "m(ethanol) theoretical = 0.200 × 46.0 = 9.20 g. [1]", "Yield = 7.36/9.20 × 100. [1]", "Yield = 80.0%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Hard-water mechanism", "prompt": "Explain why soap can form scum in hard water and why an anionic sulfonate detergent can remain more effective.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ca²⁺ or Mg²⁺ can react with soap carboxylate ions. [1]", "Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s). [1]", "Precipitation removes available soap and forms scum. [1]", "Many corresponding detergent calcium salts are more soluble, leaving more cleaning-active material. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Two-stage synthesis", "prompt": "An initial feedstock could theoretically produce 0.500 mol intermediate. Stage 1 has 80.0% yield, then a 1:1 product step has 75.0% yield. Find final product amount and overall yield.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Intermediate actually available = 0.500 × 0.800 = 0.400 mol. [1]", "Final product = 0.400 × 0.750 = 0.300 mol. [1]", "Overall fraction = 0.800 × 0.750 = 0.600. [1]", "Overall yield = 60.0%, rather than adding the percentages. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Biodiesel versus soap", "prompt": "Compare products from triglyceride reaction with methanol and from base hydrolysis. Explain one trade-off of lipase rather than base catalysis for biodiesel.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Methanol transesterification gives fatty-acid methyl esters and glycerol. [1]", "Base hydrolysis gives fatty-acid carboxylate salts, soaps, and glycerol. [1]", "The processes change ester connections differently. [1]", "Lipase can operate under milder conditions or offer different feedstock tolerance. [1]", "Its cost, stability, reuse and reaction rate must be assessed rather than assuming a universal benefit. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Industrial recommendation", "prompt": "Compare base-catalysed and lipase-catalysed biodiesel routes for a feedstock with variable water/free-fatty-acid content. Propose evidence needed for a justified selection and distinguish yield from sustainability.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Base routes can be fast/economical but water/free acids can promote difficulties including soap formation. [1]", "A lipase route may offer milder conditions or different feedstock tolerance. [1]", "Compare measured conversion/isolated yield for the actual feedstock. [1]", "Compare catalyst cost, recovery/reuse, lifetime and rate. [1]", "Assess separation, energy, waste and feedstock impacts. [1]", "High yield alone does not establish the most sustainable route; select based on the combined evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}], "bank": [{"q": "Percentage yield compares", "o": ["Actual with theoretical product", "Catalyst mass with solvent volume", "Kc with pH", "Product with pressure"], "a": 0, "why": "Use amounts of the same product on a consistent basis."}, {"q": "Higher pressure favours ammonia because", "o": ["Products have fewer gas moles", "It lowers nuclear charge", "The catalyst disappears", "It must raise Kc at constant T"], "a": 0, "why": "N₂ + 3H₂ gives four gaseous amounts to two in NH₃."}, {"q": "Biodiesel transesterification with methanol gives", "o": ["Fatty-acid methyl esters", "Only sodium soaps", "Only hydrocarbons", "Ammonia"], "a": 0, "why": "The triglyceride ester groups exchange their alcohol-derived connections."}, {"q": "A soap’s hydrophilic region is", "o": ["Its long non-polar tail", "Its charged carboxylate head", "An absent oxygen atom", "A metallic core"], "a": 1, "why": "The ionic head interacts with water."}, {"q": "Two successive 80% yields give overall yield", "o": ["160%", "80%", "64%", "40%"], "a": 2, "why": "Multiply fractions: 0.80 × 0.80 = 0.64."}], "icon": "🧪", "subtitle": "32 screens · 7 written questions · explained models"}];
